(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1a.6a4b07eb5f.js"]={"1":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what do the TART criteria for diagnosing somatic dysfunction stand for?","choices":{"A":"Tension, Atrophy, Restriction of motion, and Trigger points found on palpation of the region","B":"Tenderness, Atrophy, Rotation, and Tone changes found on segmental testing","C":"Tissue texture changes, positional Asymmetry, Restriction of motion, and Tenderness","D":"Tissue tension, Asymmetry, Rigidity, and Temperature changes of the region","E":"Texture, Alignment, Range of motion, and Tone changes of the paraspinals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists the TART criteria as Tissue texture changes, positional Asymmetry, Restriction of motion, and local Tenderness, and says somatic dysfunction is diagnosed by identifying at least some of them. TART does not stand for tension, atrophy, restriction, and trigger points; it stands for tissue texture change, asymmetry, restriction of motion, and tenderness. Atrophy, rotation, and tone changes are not the TART criteria, which are tissue texture, asymmetry, restriction, and tenderness. Rigidity and temperature changes are not part of TART, which includes tissue texture changes, asymmetry, restriction, and tenderness. Alignment, range of motion, and tone do not match the course TART criteria of tissue texture, asymmetry, restriction, and tenderness.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: somatic dysfunction is diagnosed by identifying at least some of the TART criteria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"TART does not stand for tension, atrophy, restriction, and t","B":"Atrophy, rotation, and tone changes are not the TART criteri","D":"Rigidity and temperature changes are not part of TART, which","E":"Alignment, range of motion, and tone do not match the course"}},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the physician's primary role in supporting the body's inherent capacity to heal?","choices":{"A":"To eliminate mechanical and physiological barriers to homeostatic recovery","B":"To suppress symptoms with medication until the patient recovers","C":"To replace the body's self-regulatory mechanisms with external control","D":"To restrict activity and prescribe rest until the pain fully resolves","E":"To correct structural findings on the spine without regard to function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that the body possesses self-regulatory and self-healing mechanisms, and that the physician's primary role is to eliminate mechanical and physiological barriers to support the patient's homeostatic recovery. Enforced rest until the pain resolves and correcting structure without regard to function both miss this aim: the course defines somatic dysfunction by altered function and plans care that keeps the patient active. The course says the physician's role is to remove barriers to the body's own recovery, not to suppress symptoms with medication. The body has its own self-regulatory mechanisms, and the physician supports them rather than replacing them with external control.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the osteopathic tenet that the body is capable of self-regulation and self-healing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course says the physician's role is to remove barriers t","C":"The body has its own self-regulatory mechanisms, and the phy"}},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how is somatic dysfunction defined?","choices":{"A":"A structural abnormality that is visible on plain radiographs of the lumbar spine, pelvis, and sacrum, with scoliosis","B":"Weakness confined to the myotome of one lumbar nerve root, with a matching dermatomal sensory loss","C":"Pain that persists beyond the expected healing time of twelve weeks, with depression and anxiety","D":"A bony deformity that does not respond to manual treatment of the region, with loss of motion","E":"Impaired or altered function of the body framework and its related vascular, lymphatic, and neural elements"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course defines somatic dysfunction as impaired or altered function of the body framework, meaning skeletal, arthrodial, myofascial, and related vascular, lymphatic, and neural elements. A radiographic abnormality, a single-root motor and sensory deficit, pain beyond twelve weeks (the course's cutoff for chronic pain), and a bony deformity are not this definition; the course diagnoses somatic dysfunction clinically by the TART criteria.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the definition extends beyond the skeleton to the vascular, lymphatic, and neural elements of the body framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A radiographic abnormality, a ","B":"A radiographic abnormality, a "}},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which laboratory tests are indicated to evaluate infection, systemic inflammatory conditions, or spondyloarthropathies?","choices":{"A":"Serum calcium and alkaline phosphatase levels with phosphorus","B":"Serum uric acid and creatinine levels with urea nitrogen","C":"Serum vitamin D and parathyroid hormone levels together","D":"Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","E":"Serum lipid panel and fasting glucose with hemoglobin A1c"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists the inflammatory markers ESR and CRP as indicated to evaluate infection, systemic inflammatory conditions, or spondyloarthropathies. The course's other indicated labs are serum protein electrophoresis for myeloma and urinalysis or renal function for genitourinary referral; calcium and alkaline phosphatase, uric acid, vitamin D and parathyroid hormone, and lipid or glucose panels are not among the labs it lists for back pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: routine laboratory testing is not recommended for initial mechanical back pain without red flags.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is associated with early, non-indicated MRI within 4 to 6 weeks of onset of acute low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Faster recovery, better outcomes, and lower costs of care","B":"Longer disability, worse outcomes, and unnecessary costs","C":"Shorter disability, fewer surgeries, and earlier return to work","D":"Unchanged disability, unchanged outcomes, and unchanged costs","E":"Improved function, earlier detection, and reduced medication use"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that early, non-indicated imaging such as MRI within 4 to 6 weeks is associated with increased length of disability, worse outcomes, and unnecessary medical costs or surgeries. Early non-indicated MRI is associated with longer disability and worse outcomes, not faster recovery or lower costs. Early non-indicated MRI is associated with longer disability and unnecessary surgeries, not shorter disability or fewer surgeries. The course associates early non-indicated imaging with longer disability and worse outcomes, not unchanged results. Early non-indicated imaging is associated with worse outcomes and unnecessary costs, not improved function.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: guidelines recommend against routine or early imaging for acute low back pain without focal deficits or red flags.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Early non-indicated MRI is associated with longer disability and worse outcomes,","C":"Early non-indicated MRI is associated with longer disability and unnecessary sur","D":"The course associates early non-indicated imaging with longe","E":"Early non-indicated imaging is associated with worse outcome"}},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what does a radiolucent line on the 45-degree oblique view of the lumbar spine indicate?","choices":{"A":"Metastatic disease shown as a missing pedicle on the film","B":"Spondylolisthesis shown as anterior vertebral slippage on it","C":"Spondylolysis shown as a collar on the Scotty dog's neck","D":"Reduced lumbar lordosis measured on the Cobb angle view","E":"Psoas muscle border loss shown on the AP view of the pelvis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On the 45-degree oblique view, a radiolucent line through the pars interarticularis forms a collar on the neck of the Scotty dog and indicates spondylolysis. A missing pedicle (the winking-owl sign of metastasis) is read on the AP view, not the oblique view. Spondylolisthesis is seen as slippage on the lateral view; the radiolucent line through the pars on the oblique view indicates spondylolysis. A radiolucent line on the oblique view does not indicate reduced lordosis, which is assessed on a lateral film. Psoas border loss is not the oblique-view finding; the course reads a radiolucent line through the pars (a collar on the Scotty dog's neck) as spondylolysis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lateral view evaluates spondylolisthesis, and the AP view can show scoliosis and loss of a pedicle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Spondylolisthesis is seen as slippage on the lateral view; t","D":"A radiolucent line on the oblique view does not indicate red","E":"Psoas border loss is not the oblique-view finding; the cours"}},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what makes the nucleus pulposus incompressible?","choices":{"A":"Dense concentric lamellae of collagen fibers","B":"A high collagen content cross-linked by elastin fibers","C":"Calcified cartilaginous endplates above and below","D":"A high water content bound by proteoglycans","E":"Elastic fibers of the adjacent ligamentum flavum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The nucleus pulposus is highly hydrated and incompressible because of its high water content, which is bound by a matrix of proteoglycans synthesized by a sparse population of cells in the nucleus. Concentric collagen lamellae make up the annulus fibrosus, which contains the nucleus's pressure; the course credits neither the endplates (the disc's diffusion source) nor the ligamentum flavum with the nucleus's incompressibility. The nucleus pulposus is incompressible because of water bound by proteoglycans, not collagen content cross-linked with elastin.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the annulus fibrosus is the tough outer ring of concentric lamellae that contains the nucleus's hydrostatic pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The nucleus pulposus is incompressible because of water boun","C":"Concentric collagen lamellae m"}},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how does the intervertebral disc obtain its nutrients?","choices":{"A":"By direct arterial branches that enter the annulus fibrosus from outside","B":"By diffusion from the capillaries of the adjacent vertebral endplates","C":"By lymphatic drainage that moves through the nucleus pulposus itself","D":"By perfusion from segmental arteries that run within the nucleus matrix","E":"By absorption from the surrounding cerebrospinal fluid of the thecal sac"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The disc is entirely avascular and relies on diffusion of nutrients from the capillaries of the adjacent vertebral endplates. It also lacks lymphatic scavenger cells, so metabolic waste accumulates over time. Because the disc is entirely avascular, no arterial branches or segmental arteries perfuse it, and the course names no cerebrospinal fluid route. The disc lacks lymphatic scavenger cells and is avascular, so lymphatic drainage through the nucleus is not its nutrient route.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: insufficient diffusion starts the sequence of nutritional failure and disc degeneration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The disc lacks lymphatic scavenger cells and is avascular, s"}},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which part of a healthy intervertebral disc is innervated?","choices":{"A":"The central nucleus pulposus","B":"The inner third of the annulus fibrosus","C":"The full thickness of the annulus fibrosus","D":"The cartilaginous vertebral endplates","E":"The outer third of the annulus fibrosus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that, in a healthy disc, the outer third of the annulus fibrosus is the innervated portion. The course describes neither the nucleus pulposus nor the endplates as innervated; new nerve endings reach deeper only when granulation tissue grows into an annular tear. The inner annulus is not innervated in a healthy disc; only the outer third is. Only the outer third of the annulus is innervated in a healthy disc, not the full thickness.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the outer third of the healthy annulus is the innervated portion of the disc.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The inner annulus is not innervated in a healthy disc; only ","C":"Only the outer third of the annulus is innervated in a healt"}},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which key muscle group is tested to assess the L4 nerve root?","choices":{"A":"Hip adductors for adduction of the thigh","B":"Tibialis anterior for ankle dorsiflexion","C":"Hamstring muscles for flexion of the knee","D":"Gastrocnemius and soleus for plantarflexion","E":"Iliopsoas for flexion of the hip joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the course's table, the L4 root has the tibialis anterior as its key muscle, assessed by ankle dorsiflexion. Iliopsoas and the hip adductors are the course's L1 and L2 key muscles, the quadriceps (knee extension) is L3's, and the gastrocnemius and soleus (plantarflexion) are S1's. First Aid instead ties an L4 root lesion to weak knee extension with a reduced patellar reflex, and the course table also gives the patellar reflex for L4, so the quadriceps share L4 supply; the key muscle the course table names for L4 is the tibialis anterior.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course's table pairs each lumbar root with a dermatome, a key muscle, a reflex, and a functional test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which deep tendon reflex is used to localize the L4 nerve root?","choices":{"A":"The Achilles reflex","B":"The cremasteric reflex","C":"The tibialis posterior reflex","D":"The patellar reflex","E":"The bulbocavernosus reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists the patellar reflex for the L4 root, and notes that the L3 patellar reflex is variable. The Achilles reflex tests the S1 root, not L4. The course's root table lists the cremasteric reflex for L1 and L2, not L4. The course's root table lists the tibialis posterior reflex for L5, not L4. The course's root table lists the patellar reflex for L4; it does not list the bulbocavernosus reflex for that root.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: reflexes localize nerve root compression alongside dermatomal sensation and myotomal strength.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The Achilles reflex tests the S1 root, not L4.","B":"The course's root table lists the cremasteric reflex for L1 ","C":"The course's root table lists the tibialis posterior reflex ","E":"The course's root table lists the patellar reflex for L4; it"}},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which areas show sensory loss in saddle anesthesia?","choices":{"A":"The perianal and perineal regions","B":"The anterior mid-thigh region","C":"The anterior lower thigh to the knee","D":"The medial leg and inner calf","E":"The lateral edge of the foot and heel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course defines saddle anesthesia as sensory loss in the perianal and perineal regions. The course also lists a digital rectal examination, bulbocavernosus and anal wink reflexes, and postvoid residual volume for cauda equina syndrome. In the course's table, the anterior lower thigh to the knee is the L3 dermatome, the medial leg and inner calf L4, and the lateral foot and heel S1.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cauda equina syndrome is a surgical emergency requiring immediate MRI and decompression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the effect of incorporating abdominal diaphragm myofascial release in nonspecific low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Resolution of a herniated disk protrusion on repeat imaging","B":"Correction of the lumbar lordosis measured on radiographs","C":"Statistically significant improvements in pain and disability","D":"Elimination of a leg length discrepancy at the hip level","E":"Improvement in lumbar range of motion with no change in pain scores"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that myofascial release of the abdominal diaphragm yields statistically significant improvements in pain and disability in patients with nonspecific low back pain. The course reports pain and disability outcomes only; it does not claim resolution of a herniation on imaging, radiographic lordosis correction, or a change in leg length. The course reports improved pain and disability, not range of motion with unchanged pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the abdominal and pelvic diaphragms are part of the respiratory-circulatory model.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"The course reports improved pain and disability, not range o"}},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which techniques treat iliolumbar ligament syndrome highly effectively?","choices":{"A":"Side-lying thrust, rib raising, or lumbar traction therapy","B":"Muscle energy, rib raising, or the splenic pump technique","C":"Still technique, mesenteric lift, or percussion vibrator therapy","D":"Cranial vault hold, lymphatic pump, or soft tissue articulation with stretch","E":"Counterstrain, direct inhibition, or ligamentous articular strain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that iliolumbar ligament syndrome is treated highly effectively with counterstrain using an indirect approach, direct inhibition, or ligamentous articular strain. Thrust, rib raising, muscle energy, the splenic pump, Still technique, the mesenteric lift, and percussion vibrator are not among the techniques the course lists for this syndrome.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the tender point lies at the iliac insertion, just superior and lateral to the PSIS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how does osteopathy in the cranial field help patients with chronic low back pain and central sensitization?","choices":{"A":"It down-regulates sympathetic activity, improves sleep latency, and addresses dural tension to the sacrum","B":"It lengthens the iliolumbar ligaments, restores lumbar lordosis, and reduces paraspinal hypertonicity","C":"It reduces disk herniation, relieves nerve root compression, and restores the normal disk height","D":"It increases hamstring flexibility, corrects leg length difference, and normalizes gait","E":"It strengthens the abdominal core, improves postural endurance, and reduces the spinal loading"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In chronic back pain with central sensitization, sleep disturbances, and depression, osteopathy in the cranial field targets the sphenobasilar synchondrosis and primary respiratory mechanism to down-regulate sympathetic activity, improve sleep latency, and address dural tensions transmitted to the sacrum. Lengthening the iliolumbar ligaments, reversing a disk herniation, or correcting leg length and gait are not effects the course attributes to cranial treatment, which acts through autonomic tone, sleep, and dural tension. Strengthening the abdominal core and postural endurance is exercise therapy, not an effect the course attributes to cranial osteopathy.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the cranial approach targets the sphenobasilar synchondrosis and the primary respiratory mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Strengthening the abdominal core and postural endurance is e"}},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with low back pain has fear-avoidance beliefs, anxiety, and poor social support. According to the course, which osteopathic model addresses these findings?","choices":{"A":"The metabolic model controlling inflammation through diet and nutrition","B":"The behavioral model integrating coping strategies and psychosocial yellow flags","C":"The neurological model normalizing autonomic tone and facilitation","D":"The respiratory-circulatory model optimizing diaphragmatic excursion and lymph flow","E":"The biomechanical model correcting posture, gait, and joint mechanics"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The behavioral model integrates coping strategies, screens for psychosocial yellow flags such as fear-avoidance beliefs, anxiety, and depression, and leverages social support systems. The metabolic model addresses inflammation through nutrition, not fear-avoidance and poor social support. The neurological model normalizes autonomic tone and facilitation, not psychosocial yellow flags. The respiratory-circulatory model addresses diaphragm excursion and lymph flow, not psychosocial findings. The biomechanical model corrects posture, gait, and joint mechanics, not fear-avoidance beliefs and anxiety.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the biomechanical, neurological, metabolic, and respiratory-circulatory models complete the five models of osteopathic patient care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The metabolic model addresses inflammation through nutrition","C":"The neurological model normalizes autonomic tone and facilit","D":"The respiratory-circulatory model addresses diaphragm excurs","E":"The biomechanical model corrects posture, gait, and joint me"}},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has a pelvic dysfunction and low back pain, and the DO also examines her thoracic spine, ribs, and lower extremities. According to the course, why?","choices":{"A":"Local lumbosacral dysfunction can trigger compensatory strain through the myofascial kinetic chain","B":"The lumbar spine moves independently of the pelvis and rib cage during gait and posture","C":"Strain stays at the segment where the dysfunction began and does not spread to the other regions of the body","D":"Thoracic findings arise from the lumbosacral dysfunction in a fixed reverse sequence of events","E":"Extremity findings are examined after the imaging is negative and the diagnosis is still unclear"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes a biologic tensegrity model in which local pelvic or lumbosacral dysfunction can trigger compensatory strains through the myofascial kinetic chain, so regions above and below the lumbosacral junction are evaluated. Under this model the lumbar spine does not move independently of the pelvis and ribs, and the regional exam belongs to the initial evaluation rather than waiting for negative imaging. Under the tensegrity model strain spreads through the myofascial chain rather than staying local. The course does not describe thoracic findings arising in a fixed reverse sequence; strain spreads through the kinetic chain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: tensegrity explains why regional interconnectedness matters in evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Under the tensegrity model strain spreads through the myofas","D":"The course does not describe thoracic findings arising in a "}},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key osteopathic concepts related to the evaluation and management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has low back pain, and the DO finds a somatic dysfunction alongside a mechanical disk problem. According to the course, how are these related?","choices":{"A":"Somatic dysfunction rules out an orthopedic diagnosis in an adult patient","B":"Somatic dysfunction appears after the disk problem has resolved and the pain has ended","C":"Somatic dysfunction is a visceral reflex arising from the abdominal organs and retroperitoneum","D":"Somatic dysfunction can produce the symptoms or coexist with orthopedic and visceral pathologies","E":"Somatic dysfunction is a radiographic finding of a mechanical disk problem at the level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that somatic dysfunction can directly cause low back pain or exist in association with other orthopedic, visceral, and mechanical pathologies. Somatic dysfunction does not rule out an orthopedic diagnosis; it can coexist with it. Somatic dysfunction can coexist with other pathology and does not only appear after the disk problem resolves. Somatic dysfunction is a musculoskeletal finding, not a visceral reflex from abdominal organs. Somatic dysfunction is diagnosed on physical exam by TART findings, not as a radiographic finding.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: somatic dysfunction can directly cause low back pain or coexist with orthopedic, visceral, and mechanical pathologies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — osteopathic approach to evaluating low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Somatic dysfunction does not rule out an orthopedic diagnosi","B":"Somatic dysfunction can coexist with other pathology and doe","C":"Somatic dysfunction is a musculoskeletal finding, not a visc","E":"Somatic dysfunction is diagnosed on physical exam by TART fi"}},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with low back pain has unexplained elevated globulins on routine bloodwork. According to the course, which laboratory test is indicated?","choices":{"A":"Urinalysis with renal function studies","B":"Serum protein electrophoresis testing","C":"An antinuclear antibody titer panel","D":"A fasting lipid panel with glucose","E":"A lumbar puncture with cell counts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists serum protein electrophoresis as the multiple myeloma screen when there is an unexplained increase in total protein or elevated globulins. Urinalysis and renal function are for genitourinary referral such as stones or pyelonephritis; antinuclear antibodies, lipid panels, and lumbar puncture are not among the course's back pain labs.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: labs are reserved for patients who fail to respond to therapy or have a sudden change in pain character.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman who has had lumbar surgery has recurrent leg pain. According to the course, which imaging differentiates post-surgical scar tissue from a recurrent disk herniation?","choices":{"A":"Plain radiographs with oblique views of the lumbar spine","B":"CT of the lumbar spine without contrast","C":"Electromyography of the affected leg with nerve conduction","D":"A lateral radiograph with a Cobb lordosis angle measurement","E":"MRI with and without gadolinium contrast"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that MRI with and without gadolinium contrast is indicated to diagnose malignancies or to differentiate post-surgical scar tissue from a recurrent disk herniation. Plain films (obliques for the pars, the lateral view for the Cobb angle) show bony anatomy and EMG assesses nerve conduction; neither separates scar from disc. The course names MRI with and without gadolinium contrast to separate post-surgical scar from recurrent herniation, not a noncontrast CT.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: MRI is the gold standard for spinal imaging because of its soft-tissue detail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course names MRI with and without gadolinium contrast to"}},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the diagnostic criteria, relevant laboratory findings, and imaging studies used in the assessment of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has leg pain 10 days after an injury, and his EMG is normal. According to the course, how should this result be interpreted?","choices":{"A":"A normal EMG excludes nerve root compression at any point in care","B":"EMG changes appear within the first 24 hours of injury to the root","C":"Electrical signs of nerve damage often do not appear until 21 days post-injury","D":"EMG should be repeated each day until it becomes abnormal on the tracing","E":"EMG cannot be used to separate radiculopathy from a peripheral neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course notes that electrical signs of nerve damage often do not appear on EMG until 21 days after the initial injury. In fact the course says EMG and nerve conduction studies help differentiate radiculopathy from distal peripheral neuropathies. A normal EMG does not exclude root compression because electrical signs often do not appear until 21 days after injury. EMG changes do not appear in the first 24 hours; they often appear about 21 days post-injury. Daily repeat EMG is not recommended; signs often take about 21 days to appear.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: EMG and nerve conduction studies help distinguish radiculopathy from distal peripheral neuropathies, myopathies, or entrapments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — red flags and chronicity in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A normal EMG does not exclude root compression because elect","B":"EMG changes do not appear in the first 24 hours; they often ","D":"Daily repeat EMG is not recommended; signs often take about "}},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has searing leg pain that follows a disk annular tear, and imaging shows no direct nerve root compression. According to the course, what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Chemical radiculitis from leakage of acidic proinflammatory cytokines","B":"Mechanical radiculopathy from the nucleus pulposus compressing the root","C":"Microinstability from loss of segmental motion at the involved level","D":"Referred pain from sacroiliac joint dysfunction on the same side","E":"Referred visceral pain from a retroperitoneal organ near the root"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leakage of acidic proinflammatory cytokines through the annular tear irritates the adjacent nerve root and causes sharp, searing radicular pain even without direct physical compression. Microinstability is the segmental motion disturbance of a degenerating segment found on palpatory testing, not a cause of searing radicular pain. Mechanical radiculopathy requires nuclear material compressing the root, but imaging shows no direct compression; the pain comes from chemical irritation. Sacroiliac referral is not the mechanism; the course attributes searing pain without compression to chemical radiculitis from leakage of acidic proinflammatory cytokines. Retroperitoneal visceral referral is not the mechanism; the course attributes searing pain without compression to chemical radiculitis from leakage of acidic proinflammatory cytokines.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mechanical radiculopathy instead produces focal dermatomal sensory loss, reduced reflexes, and eventual weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Mechanical radiculopathy requires nuclear material compressi","D":"Sacroiliac referral is not the mechanism; the course attribu","E":"Retroperitoneal visceral referral is not the mechanism; the "}},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A degenerating disk has lost proteoglycan synthesis, and its hydrostatic pressure and water content have fallen. According to the course, what happens next?","choices":{"A":"The cells of the nucleus pulposus increase their output of proteoglycans and rehydrate the nucleus","B":"The vertebral endplates grow new capillaries into the nucleus and restore the diffusion of nutrients","C":"The segment becomes more stable as the nucleus stiffens and the annulus bears less of the load","D":"The outer annulus loses its innervation and becomes painless as the tears of the lamellae heal","E":"The annulus fibrosus tears under overload because the nucleus cannot distribute vertical loads"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After dehydration, the nucleus can no longer distribute vertical loads, so compressive forces transfer to the annulus fibrosus and cause tears or fissures. A degenerating disc has lost proteoglycan synthesis, so its nucleus cannot rehydrate. The disc is avascular and does not receive new capillaries from the endplates to restore diffusion. After dehydration the compressive forces transfer to the annulus fibrosus and cause tears; the course does not describe the segment becoming more stable. The course says the annulus tears under the overload; it does not describe the tears healing or the annulus losing its innervation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the sequence runs from nutritional failure through dehydration and annular fissures to nociceptor activation, radiculopathy, and microinstability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A degenerating disc has lost proteoglycan synthesis, so its ","B":"The disc is avascular and does not receive new capillaries f","C":"After dehydration the compressive forces transfer to the ann","D":"The course says the annulus tears under the overload; it doe"}},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the structural and functional anatomy of the intervertebral disc and its relationship to low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with a disk annular tear has localized axial back pain. According to the course, what generates this pain?","choices":{"A":"Compression of the nerve root by the nucleus pulposus at the foramen","B":"New nerve endings in ingrown granulation tissue releasing nerve growth factor","C":"Leakage of acidic cytokines through the tear onto the dorsal root ganglion","D":"Loss of the proteoglycan matrix in the vertebral endplates above and below","E":"Hypertonicity of the psoas muscle on the same side as the symptoms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"As tears develop, well-vascularized, highly innervated granulation tissue grows into the defect. These new nerve endings release nerve growth factor, sensitizing local nociceptors and generating axial back pain. Cytokine leakage onto the root causes radicular pain (chemical radiculitis) rather than localized axial pain, proteoglycan loss belongs to the nucleus and starts dehydration rather than generating pain, and psoas hypertonicity is not the course's mechanism here.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: axial pain is localized to the spine, whereas radicular pain follows a dermatome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — disk protrusions and degenerative disk disease","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Cytokine leakage onto the root"}},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has weak great toe extension against resistance and numbness over the lateral lower leg, the dorsum of the foot, and the first two toes. According to the course, which nerve root is involved?","choices":{"A":"The L4 root","B":"The S1 root","C":"The L3 root","D":"The L5 root","E":"The L2 root"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the course's table, the L5 root supplies sensation to the lateral lower leg, dorsum of the foot, and first two toes, and its key muscle is the extensor hallucis longus for great toe extension. The L4 root supplies the medial leg and medial foot (tibialis anterior, patellar reflex), not the dorsum of the foot. The S1 root supplies the lateral edge of the foot, heel, and sole, with plantarflexion by gastrocnemius and soleus, not great toe extension. The L3 root supplies the anterior lower thigh to the knee, not the dorsum of the foot. The L2 root supplies the anterior mid-thigh (hip adductors and iliopsoas), not the dorsum of the foot.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the L5 reflex is the tibialis posterior reflex, which is variable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The L4 root supplies the medial leg and medial foot (tibiali","B":"The S1 root supplies the lateral edge of the foot, heel, and","C":"The L3 root supplies the anterior lower thigh to the knee, n","E":"The L2 root supplies the anterior mid-thigh (hip adductors a"}},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman cannot walk on her toes and has a diminished Achilles reflex. According to the course, which muscle group is weak?","choices":{"A":"Tibialis anterior muscle group","B":"Extensor hallucis longus muscle","C":"Quadriceps femoris muscle group","D":"Hip adductor and iliopsoas muscles","E":"Gastrocnemius and soleus muscles"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the course's table, toe walking and the Achilles reflex assess the S1 root, whose key muscle group is the gastrocnemius and soleus for ankle plantarflexion. Tibialis anterior tests the L4 root (ankle dorsiflexion), not toe walking and the Achilles reflex. Extensor hallucis longus tests the L5 root (great toe extension), not S1 toe walking. The quadriceps femoris is the L3 key muscle (patellar reflex), not the S1 group tested by toe walking and the Achilles reflex. The hip adductors and iliopsoas are upper lumbar root muscles (L1 to L2), not the S1 group tested by toe walking and the Achilles reflex.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course's table lists a dermatome, a key muscle, a reflex, and a functional test for each root.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Tibialis anterior tests the L4 root (ankle dorsiflexion), no","B":"Extensor hallucis longus tests the L5 root (great toe extens","C":"The quadriceps femoris is the L3 key muscle (patellar reflex","D":"The hip adductors and iliopsoas are upper lumbar root muscle"}},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform and interpret neurologic tests relevant to the diagnosis of lumbar spine pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A seated patient slumps with the neck flexed, and the DO extends the knee and dorsiflexes the ankle, reproducing the patient's leg pain. According to the course, what does this positive test indicate?","choices":{"A":"Tightness of the hamstring muscle group","B":"Arthropathy of the lumbar facet joints","C":"Tension of the neural and dural structures","D":"Dysfunction of the sacroiliac joint","E":"Spasm of the psoas muscle on that side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A positive slump test reproduces radicular pain and indicates neural or dural tension. The course's neural tension maneuvers tension nerve roots and dura without moving musculoskeletal tissue, so they separate neural irritation from hamstring tightness or hip pathology; a facet, sacroiliac, or psoas problem is not what the test stresses.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the straight leg raise is the supine dural tension test and is highly sensitive for lumbar radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 2 — neurologic examination in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course's neural tension ma","E":"The course's neural tension ma"}},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has pain at the posterior iliac crest and groin. According to the course, how does the DO confirm the diagnosis of Maigne syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Counterstrain of the iliolumbar insertion eases the referred pain","B":"A heel lift that levels the sacral base removes the referred pain","C":"Lumbosacral decompression of the paraspinals clears the referred pain","D":"Segmental manipulation of T10 to L2 resolves the referred pain","E":"Counterstrain of the posterior piriformis point calms the referred pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Altered facet mechanics between T12 and L2 irritate the posterior rami and refer pain to the iliac crest, groin, or inguinal region. Segmental manipulation of T10 to L2 often completely resolves the referred pain, which confirms the diagnosis. Counterstrain of the iliolumbar insertion treats iliolumbar ligament syndrome, not confirmation of Maigne syndrome. A heel lift is not the confirming maneuver; the course confirms Maigne syndrome when segmental manipulation of T10 to L2 resolves the referred pain. Lumbosacral decompression is not the confirming maneuver; the course confirms Maigne syndrome when segmental manipulation of T10 to L2 resolves the referred pain. Counterstrain of the piriformis point is not the confirming maneuver; the course confirms Maigne syndrome when segmental manipulation of T10 to L2 resolves the referred pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Maigne syndrome is the thoracolumbar junction syndrome, in which altered T12 to L2 facet mechanics irritate the posterior rami.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Counterstrain of the iliolumbar insertion treats iliolumbar ","B":"A heel lift is not the confirming maneuver; the course confi","C":"Lumbosacral decompression is not the confirming maneuver; th","E":"Counterstrain of the piriformis point is not the confirming "}},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old gymnast has spondylolisthesis with a hyperlordotic posture and extension-based pain. According to the course, where should gentle OMT be directed relative to the slipped segment?","choices":{"A":"Above and below the level of the slip","B":"At the level of the slip itself with a thrust","C":"At the cervical spine and the occiput","D":"At the sternum and the clavicles","E":"At the hip joint capsule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course directs gentle indirect OMT above and below the level of the slip, using counterstrain, myofascial release, and balanced ligamentous tension. The neck and occiput, the sternum and clavicles, or the hip capsule do not answer where to work relative to the unstable level; the course's distant work is the thoracic spine, pelvis, ribs, and lower extremities, added so the patient can stay active in a brace. A thrust at the level of the slip would stress an unstable segment; the course directs gentle indirect OMT above and below it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: related thoracic, pelvic, rib, and lower extremity dysfunctions are also treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"A thrust at the level of the slip would stress an unstable s"}},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) principles to the clinical management of patients presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has acute psoas spasm and a hip side shift. According to the course, why must the upper lumbar key dysfunction be treated first?","choices":{"A":"It is the most tender finding in the lumbar region on palpation","B":"It holds the greatest rotation of any segment in the lumbar spine","C":"Otherwise pelvic and lumbosacral dysfunctions recur rapidly","D":"It is the segment where counterstrain is applied for the lumbar region","E":"It restores hip extension without any need to treat the pelvis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that the upper lumbar key segment, a Type II FRS dysfunction at L1 or L2, must be treated first to obtain lasting relief. Without it, pelvic and lumbosacral dysfunctions rapidly recur. The course gives the reason as preventing rapid recurrence of pelvic and lumbosacral dysfunction, not that it is the most tender finding. The reason for treating it first is preventing recurrence of dysfunctions, not that it has the greatest rotation. The course does not give counterstrain as the reason; the key segment is a Type II FRS dysfunction at L1 or L2 treated first to obtain lasting relief. Treating the key segment does not remove the need to treat the pelvis; without it, pelvic and lumbosacral dysfunctions recur.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: HVLA or muscle energy resolves the key dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course gives the reason as preventing rapid recurrence o","B":"The reason for treating it first is preventing recurrence of","D":"The course does not give counterstrain as the reason; the ke","E":"Treating the key segment does not remove the need to treat t"}}],"4":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which group of lumbar spine elements most commonly gives rise to low back pain?","choices":{"A":"The anterior elements, such as the posterior longitudinal ligament and vertebral body","B":"The middle elements, such as the meninges, spinal cord, and nerves","C":"The visceral elements, such as the retroperitoneal organs and kidneys","D":"The posterior elements, such as the intervertebral joints and ligaments","E":"The lateral elements, such as the psoas and quadratus lumborum muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course classifies lumbar pain as arising from anterior, middle, or posterior elements and states that low back pain most commonly stems from the posterior elements: the spinous and transverse processes, lamina, pedicles, intervertebral joints, ligaments, and joint capsules. The course states low back pain most commonly stems from the posterior elements, not the anterior elements. The middle elements such as meninges, cord, and nerves are not the most common source of low back pain. The course's anatomic classification has no visceral group; visceral pain is a separate referred cause, and the posterior elements are the most common source. The course classifies lumbar pain into anterior, middle, and posterior elements with no lateral muscle group, and names the posterior elements as the most common source.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the anterior elements include the intervertebral disk and the longitudinal ligaments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course states low back pain most commonly stems from the","B":"The middle elements such as meninges, cord, and nerves are n","C":"The course's anatomic classification has no visceral group; ","E":"The course classifies lumbar pain into anterior, middle, and"}},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, approximately what percentage of low back pain is nonspecific, with an unknown exact etiology?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 90 percent","B":"Approximately 10 percent","C":"Approximately 40 percent","D":"Approximately 60 percent","E":"Approximately 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course divides low back pain into specific pain with a medically known etiology and nonspecific pain, which represents approximately 90 percent of cases. Ten percent understates it; approximately 90 percent of low back pain is nonspecific. Forty percent understates the nonspecific proportion, which is approximately 90 percent. Sixty percent understates the nonspecific proportion, which is approximately 90 percent. Ninety-five percent overstates it; the course gives approximately 90 percent.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: musculoskeletal syndromes and somatic dysfunctions can account for part or all of nonspecific low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Ten percent understates it; approximately 90 percent of low ","C":"Forty percent understates the nonspecific proportion, which ","D":"Sixty percent understates the nonspecific proportion, which ","E":"Ninety-five percent overstates it; the course gives approxim"}},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which findings typically accompany iliolumbar ligament syndrome?","choices":{"A":"A contralateral pelvic side shift, a tight ipsilateral adductor, and tight quadratus lumborum","B":"Bilateral hamstring tightness, decreased lumbar lordosis, and a flexed posture","C":"An ipsilateral foot drop, an absent patellar reflex, and loss of thigh sensation","D":"Saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, and decreased rectal sphincter tone","E":"An ipsilateral pelvic side shift, a tight ipsilateral adductor, and tight quadratus lumborum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Iliolumbar ligament syndrome presents with tenderness at the iliac insertion of the ligament, typically with an ipsilateral pelvic side shift, a tight ipsilateral adductor muscle, and tight quadratus lumborum. Bilateral hamstring tightness with decreased lordosis and a highly flexed posture is the course's picture of bilateral psoas spasm, and saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, and lost sphincter tone are cauda equina red flags. The pelvic side shift in iliolumbar ligament syndrome is ipsilateral, not contralateral. Foot drop, an absent patellar reflex, and loss of thigh sensation are not iliolumbar ligament findings; the course lists iliac-insertion tenderness with an ipsilateral side shift, tight adductor, and tight quadratus lumborum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the tender point lies 1 inch superior and lateral to the medial edge of the PSIS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The pelvic side shift in iliolumbar ligament syndrome is ips","C":"Foot drop, an absent patellar reflex, and loss of thigh sens"}},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, acute low back pain is defined as pain lasting how long?","choices":{"A":"Less than 4 weeks","B":"Less than 8 weeks","C":"Less than 6 weeks","D":"Less than 10 weeks","E":"Less than 12 weeks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course classifies low back pain as acute when it lasts less than 6 weeks, subacute from 6 to 12 weeks, and chronic when it lasts more than 12 weeks. Under four weeks is too short; the course defines acute low back pain as lasting less than 6 weeks. Less than eight weeks is too long, since subacute pain begins at 6 weeks. Less than ten weeks overlaps with subacute pain, which runs from 6 to 12 weeks. Twelve weeks is the boundary above which pain is chronic, not acute.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronicity determines which diagnostic tests are ordered and guides management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Under four weeks is too short; the course defines acute low ","B":"Less than eight weeks is too long, since subacute pain begin","D":"Less than ten weeks overlaps with subacute pain, which runs ","E":"Twelve weeks is the boundary above which pain is chronic, no"}},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which are the four main categories of red flags for serious underlying pathology in low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Cauda equina syndrome, infection, fracture, and malignancy","B":"Cauda equina syndrome, infection, depression, and malignancy","C":"Fracture, malignancy, fear-avoidance beliefs, and infection","D":"Infection, fracture, osteoporosis, and sleep problems","E":"Malignancy, catastrophizing, cauda equina syndrome, and smoking"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists four red flag categories: cauda equina syndrome, infection, fracture, and malignancy. Depression, fear-avoidance beliefs, obesity, sleep problems, catastrophizing, and smoking are yellow flags.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a single red flag has low sensitivity and specificity, but clusters of red flags raise the probability of serious disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which two predictive instruments does the course name for identifying yellow flags?","choices":{"A":"The Mini-Mental State Examination and the Apgar scoring system","B":"The STarT Back tool and the Orebro Musculoskeletal Pain Screening Questionnaire","C":"The Glasgow Coma Scale and the Montreal Cognitive Assessment tool","D":"The Wells criteria for pulmonary embolism and the CURB-65 pneumonia score","E":"The Ottawa ankle rules and the Canadian C-spine imaging decision rule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course names the STarT Back tool and the Orebro Musculoskeletal Pain Screening Questionnaire as predictive screening instruments for psychosocial yellow flags. The Mini-Mental State Examination, Apgar score, Wells criteria, CURB-65, Ottawa ankle rules, and Canadian C-spine rule serve other purposes and are not part of the course's yellow-flag screen. The Glasgow Coma Scale and Montreal Cognitive Assessment are not the instruments the course names; it names the STarT Back tool and the Orebro questionnaire.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: psychosocial yellow flags play a critical role in the transition from acute pain to chronic pain and disability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The Glasgow Coma Scale and Montreal Cognitive Assessment are"}},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man stands flexed forward with a lean to the right, restricted hip extension, and a pelvic side shift to the left. According to the course, which condition fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Left-sided psoas syndrome from iliopsoas spasm","B":"Right-sided iliolumbar ligament syndrome","C":"Right-sided psoas syndrome from iliopsoas spasm","D":"Pregnancy-related low back pain with a bilaterally flexed sacrum","E":"Right-sided thoracolumbar junction syndrome of T12 to L2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Psoas spasm produces a flexed forward posture with a lean toward the side of the tight muscle, restricted hip extension, and a pelvic side shift away from the tight psoas. Here the lean is right and the shift is left, so the right psoas is tight. Iliolumbar ligament syndrome presents with iliac-insertion tenderness and an ipsilateral side shift, not this psoas pattern of forward flexion with restricted hip extension. The course describes the flexed forward lean with restricted hip extension and a side shift as psoas spasm, not pregnancy-related low back pain. Thoracolumbar junction syndrome presents with posterior iliac crest pain and T12 to L2 tenderness; the flexed lean with restricted hip extension is the psoas pattern.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: psoas syndrome typically carries a Type II FRS dysfunction of L1 or L2 rotated to the side of the tight psoas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Here the lean is right and the","B":"Iliolumbar ligament syndrome presents with iliac-insertion t","D":"The course describes the flexed forward lean with restricted","E":"Thoracolumbar junction syndrome presents with posterior ilia"}},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old gymnast has mechanical low back pain worsened by repetitive flexion-extension and lumbar extension. She has a hyperlordotic posture and hamstring tightness, and she has tender points over the iliolumbar ligaments, sacral sulcus, and gluteus medius without a step-off of the spinous processes. According to the course, what is the lesion?","choices":{"A":"Anterior translation of one vertebral body on the segment below","B":"Eccentric bulging of a disk with an intact annulus fibrosus","C":"Posterior translation of one vertebral body on the segment below","D":"Impingement of the superior cluneal nerve at the iliac crest","E":"A stress fracture of the pars interarticularis primarily at L5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spondylolysis is a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis, primarily at L5 in 95 percent of cases, presenting with mechanical pain worsened by repetitive flexion-extension and extension. Spondylolisthesis, the anterior translation of a vertebral body, is characterized instead by a tender step-off of the spinous processes. Eccentric bulging with an intact annulus defines a disk protrusion, and superior cluneal nerve impingement is the peripheral variant of Maigne syndrome, which presents with posterior iliac crest pain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: spondylolysis involves L5 in 95 percent of cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the clinical conditions that cause low back pain as described in osteopathic medical literature.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has leg and buttock pain that worsens with walking and spinal extension and improves with sitting or forward flexion. She has a positive Kemp sign. According to the course, what is this classic presentation called?","choices":{"A":"Neurogenic claudication","B":"Vascular claudication of the legs","C":"Failed back surgical syndrome","D":"Cauda equina syndrome","E":"Referred visceral pain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes spinal stenosis as presenting classically with neurogenic claudication, in which extension narrows the spinal canal and sitting or forward flexion relieves the pain, with a positive Kemp sign on extension. Failed back surgical syndrome follows a lumbar decompression or stabilization procedure, and cauda equina syndrome presents with saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, and sphincter changes rather than posture-dependent leg pain. The course notes vascular claudication can mimic neurogenic claudication, but relief with sitting or forward flexion and a positive Kemp sign mark spinal stenosis. Referred visceral pain is diffuse, poorly localized aching, not leg pain that worsens with extension and eases with flexion alongside a positive Kemp sign.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: vascular claudication can mimic neurogenic claudication, so the DO checks leg circulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 4 — treatment strategies for specific low back pain conditions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course notes vascular claudication can mimic neurogenic ","E":"Referred visceral pain is diffuse, poorly localized aching, "}},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has right low back pain. According to the course, when should the DO examine the painful region during the osteopathic structural examination?","choices":{"A":"First, to locate the restrictive barrier","B":"After imaging confirms the affected level","C":"At the same time as the neurologic examination","D":"Last, to prevent guarding and compensation","E":"Before the standing postural examination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that the painful region should be examined last during the structural exam to prevent patient guarding and structural compensation during the rest of the exam. Examining it first invites that guarding, and the course's structural exam does not wait for imaging; it follows the history, general medical, and neurologic exams. The standing postural examination is part of the structural exam, but the painful region is examined last, not before it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: active motion is tested first by the patient and passive motion by the physician.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Examining it first invites tha","B":"Examining it first invites tha","E":"The standing postural examination is part of the structural "}},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has an anterior pelvic tilt, increased hip flexion, and compensatory lumbar hyperlordosis. According to the course, which muscle pattern defines this lower crossed syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Tight iliopsoas and erector spinae with weak gluteus maximus and abdominals","B":"Tight gluteus maximus and abdominals with weak iliopsoas and erector spinae","C":"Tight hamstrings and gluteus medius with weak quadratus lumborum and psoas","D":"Tight rectus abdominis and obliques with weak tensor fascia lata and rectus femoris","E":"Tight piriformis and hip adductors with weak gluteus maximus and erector spinae"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Janda's lower crossed syndrome combines tightness of the iliopsoas, rectus femoris, erector spinae, and tensor fascia lata with weakness or inhibition of the gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, rectus abdominis, and obliques. This reverses the pattern; the iliopsoas and erector spinae are tight and the gluteus maximus and abdominals are weak in lower crossed syndrome. This is not Janda's pattern, in which the iliopsoas and erector spinae are tight and the gluteus maximus and abdominals are weak. The abdominals and obliques are weak or inhibited in lower crossed syndrome, not tight. The gluteus maximus is weak and the erector spinae tight in this syndrome, so this pairing is not correct.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Zink's common compensatory pattern is a different framework, with somatic dysfunctions at the spinal transitional zones showing alternating fascial rotational preferences throughout the body.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"This reverses the pattern; the iliopsoas and erector spinae ","C":"This is not Janda's pattern, in which the iliopsoas and erec","D":"The abdominals and obliques are weak or inhibited in lower c","E":"The gluteus maximus is weak and the erector spinae tight in "}},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the osteopathic approach to evaluating a patient presenting with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A supine patient has sharp pain shooting below the knee when the DO passively raises the extended leg. According to the course, how does the DO distinguish neural pain from hamstring tightness?","choices":{"A":"Raise the leg higher and hold until the radiating pain eases","B":"Slightly lower the leg and add passive ankle dorsiflexion or neck flexion","C":"Flex the knee and plantarflex the ankle before repeating the full raise","D":"Ask the patient to contract the hamstrings against the DO's resistance","E":"Rotate the hip internally and externally while the leg stays raised"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Structural differentiation maneuvers, such as slightly lowering the leg and adding passive ankle dorsiflexion, hip adduction, or neck flexion, tension the dural sac without moving musculoskeletal tissue and so separate neural irritation from hamstring tightness. Raising the leg higher or rotating the hip moves musculoskeletal tissue along with the nerve, so neither isolates neural tension. The course separates neural pain from hamstring tightness by slightly lowering the leg and adding ankle dorsiflexion or neck flexion, not by flexing the knee and plantarflexing the ankle. The course separates neural pain from hamstring tightness by slightly lowering the leg and adding ankle dorsiflexion or neck flexion, not by hamstring contraction against resistance.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a positive straight leg raise typically occurs between 30 and 70 degrees of flexion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 4 — neurologic tests for lumbar spine pathology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Raising the leg higher or rota","C":"The course separates neural pain from hamstring tightness by slightly lowering the leg and adding ankle dorsiflexion or neck flexion, not by flexing the knee an","D":"The course separates neural pain from hamstring tightness by slightly lowering the leg and adding ankle dorsiflexion or neck flexion, not by hamstring contracti","E":"Raising the leg higher or rota"}}],"5":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how long is the standard conservative trial for nonspecific mechanical back pain?","choices":{"A":"6 to 8 weeks","B":"2 to 4 weeks","C":"10 to 12 weeks","D":"3 to 4 months","E":"6 months"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes a standard multidisciplinary protocol for nonspecific mechanical back pain as a 6-to-8-week trial combining patient education, home exercises, core stabilization, physical or occupational therapy, and judicious OMT. Two to four weeks, ten to twelve weeks, three to four months, and six months are not the course's length for this trial.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a written summary of the recommendations should be given to the patient at the end of the visit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Two to four weeks, ten to twel","C":"Two to four weeks, ten to twel"}},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, when is initial follow-up recommended for an acute outpatient presentation of low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Within 12 to 24 hours","B":"Within 1 to 2 weeks","C":"Within 3 to 4 weeks","D":"Within 2 to 3 months","E":"Within 2 to 3 days"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For an acute outpatient presentation the course recommends initial follow-up within 2 to 3 days. Twelve to twenty-four hours is shorter than the course's recommended 2 to 3 days. One to two weeks is later than the recommended initial follow-up of 2 to 3 days. Three to four weeks is far later than the recommended follow-up of 2 to 3 days. Two to three months is far later than the recommended follow-up of 2 to 3 days.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the DO palpates at each visit to adjust the treatment dosage or modality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Twelve to twenty-four hours is shorter than the course's rec","B":"One to two weeks is later than the recommended initial follo","C":"Three to four weeks is far later than the recommended follow","D":"Two to three months is far later than the recommended follow"}},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what must the physician determine first when stratifying immediacy and risk in a patient with low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Whether the patient can set realistic, measurable recovery goals","B":"Whether the dysfunction is better treated with a direct or an indirect technique","C":"Whether an emergent or urgent medical condition needs referral or advanced imaging","D":"Whether the most tender segment suits direct treatment at the first visit","E":"Whether scheduled anti-inflammatory medication would help control the acute pain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that the physician must first determine whether an emergent or urgent condition requires immediate specialist referral, surgical consultation, or advanced imaging. Goal setting comes after the physician first rules out an emergent or urgent condition. Technique selection comes after first determining whether an emergent or urgent condition requires referral. Choosing direct treatment of a tender segment comes after screening for emergent conditions. Medication decisions come after first determining whether referral or advanced imaging is needed.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a conservative timeline is established if no immediate life-threatening process is present.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Goal setting comes after the physician first rules out an em","B":"Technique selection comes after first determining whether an","D":"Choosing direct treatment of a tender segment comes after sc","E":"Medication decisions come after first determining whether re"}},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which technique is indicated when localized muscle and joint pain with dominant tender points characterizes the presentation?","choices":{"A":"Strain-counterstrain","B":"Cranial osteopathic technique","C":"Articulatory technique","D":"Still technique","E":"HVLA thrust technique"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists strain-counterstrain as indicated when localized muscle and joint pain and tender points dominate, and as an ideal gentle indirect technique for acute flare-ups. The course indicates cranial technique for chronic low back pain with central sensitization, anxiety, depression, or poor sleep, not tender-point dominant pain. Articulatory technique is indicated for chronic facet stiffness, not tender-point dominant pain. Still technique is indicated for acute complex restrictions, not tender-point dominant localized pain. The course indicates HVLA for specific localized Type II dysfunctions and restricted L5-S1 joint play, not tender-point dominant pain, which calls for gentle indirect counterstrain.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the Jones points treated include anterior and posterior lumbar and pelvic points.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course indicates cranial technique for chronic low back ","C":"Articulatory technique is indicated for chronic facet stiffn","D":"Still technique is indicated for acute complex restrictions,","E":"The course indicates HVLA for specific localized Type II dys"}},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is side-lying lumbar articulation indicated for?","choices":{"A":"Severe paraspinal hypertonia with loss of fascial compliance","B":"Chronic facet stiffness with loss of passive range of motion","C":"Acute complex restrictions of the pelvis with an innominate upslip","D":"Hypertonic psoas and piriformis muscles with limited motion","E":"Specific localized dysfunctions at L5-S1 without contraindications"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists side-lying lumbar joint mobilization as indicated for chronic zygapophysial facet stiffness and to gently restore passive range of motion. Paraspinal hypertonia with lost fascial compliance calls for myofascial release, acute complex pelvic restrictions such as an upslip call for Still technique, and localized L5-S1 dysfunction without contraindications calls for HVLA. Lengthening hypertonic psoas and piriformis muscles is the course's indication for muscle energy, not for lumbar articulation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: articulation targets chronic zygapophysial stiffness in a side-lying position.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Lengthening hypertonic psoas and piriformis muscles is the c"}},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which approach is indicated for sacral torsions and for lengthening hypertonic psoas and piriformis muscles?","choices":{"A":"Osteopathy in the cranial field","B":"Balanced ligamentous tension","C":"Diaphragmatic myofascial release","D":"Muscle energy technique","E":"Side-lying lumbar thrust"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists muscle energy technique for segmental Type II FRS and ERS dysfunctions and sacral torsions, and to lengthen hypertonic muscles such as the psoas and piriformis. Cranial treatment and balanced ligamentous tension target central sensitization and ligamentous laxity, diaphragmatic release targets pain and disability through core pressure and lymphatic return, and side-lying thrust targets localized Type II restrictions.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: muscle energy uses active, patient-directed isometric contractions against a counterforce.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which technique class is absolutely contraindicated in conditions such as acute vertebral fracture, bony metastasis, or cauda equina syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Gentle indirect techniques such as balanced ligamentous tension","B":"Direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA","C":"Passive myofascial release of the paraspinal tissues","D":"Diaphragmatic lymphatic drainage techniques","E":"Cranial manipulation of the sphenobasilar synchondrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA are absolutely contraindicated in these absolute-contraindication conditions, and that in many cases all manual manipulation at the affected region must be withheld. Passive paraspinal myofascial release is not the class the course singles out, though it too is often withheld at the affected region. The course singles out direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA as absolutely contraindicated, not gentle indirect techniques such as balanced ligamentous tension. The course singles out direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA as absolutely contraindicated, not diaphragmatic lymphatic drainage. The course singles out direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA as absolutely contraindicated, not cranial manipulation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the absolute list covers conditions with a risk of catastrophic structural failure, cord injury, or hemorrhage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course singles out direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA as absolutely contraindicated, not gentle indirect t","D":"The course singles out direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA as absolutely contraindicated, not diaphragmatic lym","E":"The course singles out direct-action thrust techniques such as HVLA as absolutely contraindicated, not cranial manipulat"}},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which of these is an absolute contraindication to OMT in low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Spondylolisthesis with segmental instability","B":"Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","C":"Active inflammatory joint disease","D":"Bony metastasis at the target segment","E":"Acute herniated disk with active radiculopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists bony metastasis at the target segment as an absolute contraindication because tumors weaken the vertebral architecture. Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication, active inflammatory joint disease, and an acute herniation with active radiculopathy are relative contraindications in the course. The course lists spondylolisthesis as a relative contraindication: HVLA is avoided at the unstable segment, with gentle indirect OMT above and below the slip.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: primary or metastatic tumors from breast, lung, prostate, renal, or thyroid cancers weaken the vertebral architecture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course lists spondylolisthesis as a relative contraindic"}},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what does OMT look like for a patient with a relative contraindication?","choices":{"A":"High-velocity thrust applied directly at the involved level","B":"Forceful direct articulation of the unstable spinal segment","C":"Direct inhibition applied at the involved spinal level","D":"Forced lumbar extension applied to the affected lumbar region","E":"Gentle indirect OMT at associated or distant somatic dysfunctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For relative contraindications the physician avoids high-force, direct-action techniques at the involved level, while gentle indirect OMT directed at associated or distant somatic dysfunctions remains safe and beneficial. Forcing lumbar extension is what the course says to avoid in spinal stenosis, because extension further narrows the canal. High-velocity thrust at the involved level is a high-force direct technique that the course says to avoid in relative contraindications. Forceful direct articulation of an unstable segment is a high-force direct technique that must be avoided.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course names strain-counterstrain, myofascial release, and balanced ligamentous tension as the gentle options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"High-velocity thrust at the involved level is a high-force d","B":"Forceful direct articulation of an unstable segment is a hig"}},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what should be avoided in a patient with acute psoas spasm?","choices":{"A":"Applying cold therapy to the spasm","B":"Gentle self-directed home stretches","C":"Counterstrain of the anterior psoas points","D":"Applying local heat to the spasm","E":"Muscle energy of the lower lumbar spine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course advises avoiding local heat, which typically worsens acute psoas spasm, and using cold therapy and gentle home stretching instead. Muscle energy is not on the course's avoid list (it uses muscle energy or HVLA for the L1-L2 key dysfunction); besides heat, it warns against heavy core exercise or psoas strengthening during the spasm. Gentle home stretches are recommended for acute psoas spasm, not avoided. The course uses counterstrain for the anterior psoas and iliacus tender points in psoas syndrome, so it is part of the plan, not something to avoid.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cold therapy and gentle self-directed stretching are the course's self-care advice for acute psoas spasm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Gentle home stretches are recommended for acute psoas spasm,","C":"The course uses counterstrain for the anterior psoas and ili"}},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which device is used in the acute phase of spondylolisthesis to limit shear?","choices":{"A":"A lordotic hyperextension-type brace","B":"A TLSO spinal orthosis","C":"A rigid cervical collar","D":"A sacroiliac support belt","E":"A knee immobilizer brace"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course recommends a spinal orthosis, the TLSO brace, in the acute phase to limit shear, along with an antilordotic physical therapy program. A cervical collar or a sacroiliac belt does not brace the unstable lumbar segment; the course names only the TLSO. A hyperextension brace pushes the spine into extension, which the course's antilordotic program for spondylolisthesis avoids; it names a TLSO to limit shear. A knee immobilizer does not brace the lumbar spine; the course names a TLSO spinal orthosis to limit shear in the acute phase.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the antilordotic program stresses core strengthening, hamstring stretching, and avoiding extension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A hyperextension brace pushes the spine into extension, whic","E":"A knee immobilizer does not brace the lumbar spine; the cour"}},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which OMT is provided for short leg syndrome before heel lift therapy is introduced?","choices":{"A":"Prescribing a rigid lumbosacral brace to hold the pelvis level during standing and walking","B":"Prescribing high-impact plyometric training to equalize strength in both legs","C":"Pan-corporeal OMT for pelvic shears and sacral torsions with extremity work","D":"Strengthening the psoas muscle to level the pelvis in standing and walking","E":"Resting in bed for several weeks until the leg discrepancy resolves on its own"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes the structural OMT for short leg syndrome as pan-corporeal treatment of pelvic shears, sacral torsions, lower extremity joint restrictions, and quadratus lumborum myofascial release, before heel lift therapy levels the sacral base. A rigid brace, plyometric training, and psoas strengthening are not part of the course's short leg plan, and the course advises against bed rest beyond 3 days.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: leg length discrepancy is a primary perpetuating factor for quadratus lumborum trigger points.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"A rigid brace, plyometric trai"}},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":1,"stem":"In the course's case of a 52-year-old man with right-sided low back pain, which event started the compensatory chain?","choices":{"A":"A left knee meniscus injury that altered his gait","B":"A right bicipital tendonitis flare from playing tennis","C":"A lumbar disk protrusion at L5-S1 that irritated the root","D":"An exhalation restriction of the right first rib","E":"A fracture of the right ankle during a golf swing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The case begins with a left knee meniscus injury playing tennis that forced him to limp, which set off restricted ankle dorsiflexion, pelvic unleveling, a sacral torsion, piriformis hypertonicity, and an L5 somatic dysfunction. The L5-S1 disc protrusion is a later MRI finding in the case, not the starting event. Right bicipital tendonitis is only part of his past history; the case starts the chain with a left knee meniscus injury that made him limp. The right first rib exhalation restriction is a structural finding handled in the respiratory-circulatory model, not the injury that began the gait chain. The case has no ankle fracture; restricted ankle dorsiflexion followed the left knee injury and altered gait.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the case uses the biomechanical model to link the knee injury to the back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Right bicipital tendonitis is only part of his past history;","D":"The right first rib exhalation restriction is a structural f","E":"The case has no ankle fracture; restricted ankle dorsiflexio"}},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":1,"stem":"In the course's case, which model includes short-term scheduled NSAIDs, advice against bed rest beyond 3 days, and dietary guidance?","choices":{"A":"The biomechanical model targeting gait kinematics","B":"The neurological model targeting segmental facilitation","C":"The respiratory-circulatory model targeting venous return","D":"The behavioral model targeting fear-avoidance and coping beliefs","E":"The metabolic energy model targeting systemic inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The metabolic energy model plan includes short-term scheduled NSAIDs, avoiding bed rest beyond 3 days, and dietary guidance to decrease systemic inflammation and protect cellular repair. The biomechanical model addresses gait and structural mechanics, not scheduled NSAIDs and dietary guidance. The neurological model addresses segmental facilitation, not NSAIDs and diet. The respiratory-circulatory model addresses venous return and diaphragm function, not NSAIDs and diet. The behavioral model addresses coping beliefs and fear-avoidance, not NSAIDs and diet.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: hypercholesterolemia and a systemic inflammatory state can delay recovery of the meniscus, fascia, and avascular disk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The biomechanical model addresses gait and structural mechan","B":"The neurological model addresses segmental facilitation, not","C":"The respiratory-circulatory model addresses venous return an","D":"The behavioral model addresses coping beliefs and fear-avoid"}},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":1,"stem":"In the course's case, what did the MRI show?","choices":{"A":"A 2-mm broad-based L4-L5 protrusion with mild right neuroforaminal stenosis at L4","B":"A 5-mm broad-based L5-S1 protrusion with mild right neuroforaminal stenosis at L5","C":"A 2-mm broad-based L5-S1 protrusion with mild right neuroforaminal stenosis","D":"A 2-mm broad-based L5-S1 protrusion with mild left neuroforaminal stenosis at L5","E":"A normal study with a hypertonic piriformis muscle on the right"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The subsequent diagnostics showed normal EMG and nerve conduction studies and an MRI with a 2-mm broad-based L5-S1 disk protrusion with mild right-sided neuroforaminal stenosis at L5. The protrusion was at L5-S1, not L4-L5. The protrusion was 2 mm, not 5 mm. The foraminal stenosis was on the right, not the left. The MRI showed a 2-mm L5-S1 protrusion, not a normal study.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: in the course's case the EMG and nerve conduction studies were normal, so the MRI supplies the structural findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The protrusion was at L5-S1, not L4-L5.","B":"The protrusion was 2 mm, not 5 mm.","D":"The foraminal stenosis was on the right, not the left.","E":"The MRI showed a 2-mm L5-S1 protrusion, not a normal study."}},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with low back pain has fever, a history of IV drug use, and a recent indwelling catheter. According to the course, what comes first in his treatment plan?","choices":{"A":"Immediate referral or advanced imaging before OMT","B":"A conservative trial of home exercises and OMT","C":"Scheduled NSAIDs with reassessment at the next visit","D":"Treatment of thoracic and pelvic dysfunctions before further workup","E":"Reassurance and a written exercise handout"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fever, IV drug use, and indwelling catheters are infection red flags in the course's evaluation section. The treatment plan section says the physician must first determine whether an emergent condition, such as infection, requires immediate specialist referral, surgical consultation, or advanced imaging before a conservative plan. Scheduled NSAIDs, reassurance, and an exercise handout belong to the conservative plan, which the course starts only after an emergent condition is excluded. Treating thoracic and pelvic dysfunctions before workup would delay evaluation of a possible infection; referral or advanced imaging comes first.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a conservative timeline starts only after emergent processes are excluded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Treating thoracic and pelvic dysfunctions before workup woul"}},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO treated an acute outpatient with low back pain, and the patient has improved at each visit. According to the course, how should the interval between treatment sessions change?","choices":{"A":"It shortens as the dysfunction resolves and the pain subsides","B":"It stays fixed at the initial interval regardless of response","C":"It lengthens once imaging has been repeated and reviewed","D":"It progressively lengthens as dysfunction and inflammation resolve","E":"It shortens once the patient reaches a functional milestone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that as somatic dysfunctions and local inflammatory responses resolve, the intervals between treatment sessions progressively lengthen. Reaching a functional milestone does not shorten the interval; the course treats milestones as recovery goals, and the interval only lengthens as the dysfunction resolves. The interval does not stay fixed; it progressively lengthens as dysfunction and inflammation resolve. The interval lengthens with resolving dysfunction and inflammation, not once imaging has been repeated.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the DO adjusts dosage and modality at every visit based on palpated tissue changes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The interval does not stay fixed; it progressively lengthens","C":"The interval lengthens with resolving dysfunction and inflam"}},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Outline the key components of developing an osteopathic treatment plan for a patient with low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with chronic low back pain has a lumbar dysfunction that is influenced by forces above and below the lumbosacral region. According to the course, which regions should the treatment plan address?","choices":{"A":"Cervical spine, sphenoid, temporal bones, mandible, and hyoid bone","B":"Lumbar spine with the remaining regions deferred to later visits","C":"Thoracic spine, costal cage, pelvis, sacrum, and lower extremities","D":"Pelvis and sacrum, which hold the primary lumbosacral dysfunction","E":"Lumbar paraspinal muscles, thoracolumbar fascia, and iliolumbar ligaments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lumbar mechanics are affected by altered forces above and below the lumbosacral region, so the physician must evaluate and plan treatment for the thoracic spine, costal cage, pelvis, sacrum, and lower extremities. The cranial structures are not the regions above and below the lumbosacral region that the course names. Deferring the other regions ignores the forces above and below the lumbosacral region the course says must be evaluated. The pelvis and sacrum alone leave out the thoracic spine, costal cage, and lower extremities. Lumbar soft tissues alone leave out the regions above and below the lumbosacral region.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: restoring posture and mechanical efficiency requires a multi-regional plan.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — key osteopathic concepts in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The cranial structures are not the regions above and below t","B":"Deferring the other regions ignores the forces above and bel","D":"The pelvis and sacrum alone leave out the thoracic spine, co","E":"Lumbar soft tissues alone leave out the regions above and be"}},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with chronic nonspecific low back pain has anxiety, depression, and poor sleep latency, with signs of central sensitization. According to the course, which technique is indicated?","choices":{"A":"High-velocity thrust at the L5-S1 segment of the lumbosacral spine region","B":"Side-lying articulation of the lumbar facet joints for stiffness","C":"Prone lumbar pressure with counter-leverage on the paraspinals","D":"Osteopathic cranial manipulation of the sphenobasilar synchondrosis","E":"Still technique to the innominate bone for a pelvic upslip"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course indicates osteopathy in the cranial field for chronic low back pain with central neuropathic sensitization, anxiety, depression, or sleep disturbances, targeting the sphenobasilar synchondrosis and primary respiratory mechanism. Thrust at L5-S1 is for localized Type II restrictions, side-lying articulation for chronic facet stiffness, prone pressure with counter-leverage for paraspinal hypertonia, and Still technique for acute pelvic restrictions such as upslips.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: OCMM down-regulates sympathetic nerve activity and improves sleep latency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has a localized, subacute Type II dysfunction at L5-S1 with no bony or neurologic contraindications. According to the course, which approach is indicated?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar prone pressure with counter-leverage","B":"Side-lying lumbar thrust technique","C":"Diaphragmatic myofascial release","D":"Balanced ligamentous tension of the iliolumbar ligaments","E":"Passive positioning to shorten tissues at Jones points"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists HVLA as indicated for localized, subacute or chronic segmental restrictions, especially at L5-S1, when no bony or neurologic contraindications exist. Prone pressure with counter-leverage and diaphragmatic release are myofascial techniques for paraspinal hypertonia and nonspecific pain, balanced ligamentous tension treats ligamentous laxity and microinstability, and positioning at Jones points is counterstrain for tender points and acute flare-ups.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: side-lying lumbar thrust is the course's HVLA technique for the lumbar region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the osteopathic manipulative treatment (OMT) techniques applicable to the lumbar region and their indications for low back pain management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has lumbar paraspinal hypertonia, thoracolumbar fascial stiffness, reduced fascial compliance, and coccydynia. According to the course, which technique is indicated?","choices":{"A":"Active isometric contractions against a physician counterforce","B":"Moving the dysfunction from ease through the restrictive barrier","C":"Decompression of the occipitoatlantal joint and cranial base","D":"Passive positioning toward the point of greatest ease","E":"Soft tissue and myofascial release with lumbosacral decompression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course indicates soft tissue and myofascial release to reduce passive resistance in the paraspinal tissues, address thoracolumbar fascial stiffness, and treat coccydynia, using techniques such as lumbosacral decompression and abdominal diaphragm release. Isometric contractions against a counterforce describe muscle energy, and moving from ease through the barrier describes Still technique; the course indicates neither for fascial stiffness or coccydynia. Occipitoatlantal decompression is cranial treatment, which the course indicates for central sensitization, anxiety, depression, or poor sleep rather than fascial stiffness and coccydynia. Passive positioning toward the point of greatest ease is counterstrain, which the course indicates for tender-point dominant pain, not fascial stiffness.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: diaphragmatic myofascial release improves pain and disability scores in chronic nonspecific low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 3 — Still technique indications and contraindications","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Occipitoatlantal decompression is cranial treatment, which t","D":"Passive positioning toward the point of greatest ease is cou"}},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has an acute herniated nucleus pulposus with active radiculopathy. According to the course, how is this classified?","choices":{"A":"A relative contraindication requiring avoidance of direct thrusts at that level","B":"An absolute contraindication that withholds manipulation of the entire region","C":"A condition that is not a contraindication when the radicular pain is stable and mild","D":"A contraindication that applies to the cervical spine and upper thoracic spine","E":"A relative contraindication that permits direct thrusts one level above the herniation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute herniated nucleus pulposus with active radiculopathy is a relative contraindication. Direct thrusts at the herniation level are avoided, while gentle indirect techniques relieve the surrounding protective spasm. It is a relative contraindication, not an absolute contraindication that withholds manipulation of the entire region. The course lists acute herniation with active radiculopathy as a relative contraindication, so it still calls for avoiding direct thrusts at that level. The course lists acute herniation with active radiculopathy among the relative contraindications for OMT in low back pain, not as a cervical or upper thoracic rule. For this relative contraindication the course pairs avoiding thrusts at the herniation level with gentle indirect techniques for the surrounding spasm, not thrusts at a neighboring level.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: avoiding thrusts protects the nerve root from further compression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"It is a relative contraindication, not an absolute contraind","C":"The course lists acute herniation with active radiculopathy as a relative contra","D":"The course lists acute herniation with active radiculopathy among the relative c","E":"For this relative contraindication the course pairs avoiding"}},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has sudden perianal numbness and urinary retention after lifting. According to the course, how should the DO proceed?","choices":{"A":"Perform gentle indirect OMT at distant regions and reassess at the next visit","B":"Withhold OMT and arrange emergent surgical referral for decompression","C":"Treat the affected segment with a side-lying thrust technique","D":"Begin conservative therapy with home exercises and reassess at follow-up","E":"Treat the lumbar spine with prone pressure and counter-leverage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Perianal numbness and urinary retention suggest cauda equina syndrome, a surgical emergency. The course lists it as an absolute contraindication because any delay in decompression or mechanical pressure on the canal can cause permanent paralysis or incontinence. Gentle indirect OMT at distant regions and a conservative trial apply only to relative contraindications or after emergent conditions are excluded, and a thrust is absolutely contraindicated here. Cauda equina syndrome is an absolute contraindication and surgical emergency, so lumbar manual work such as prone pressure is withheld in favor of urgent decompression.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: any delay in decompression or mechanical pressure on the canal can cause permanent paralysis or incontinence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Gentle indirect OMT at distant","D":"Gentle indirect OMT at distant","E":"Cauda equina syndrome is an absolute contraindication and su"}},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate between absolute contraindications and relative contraindications for utilizing OMT in the management of low back pain.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis has a flare of sacroiliitis. According to the course, which approach is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Direct articulation to restore normal sacroiliac joint motion","B":"A high-velocity thrust to release the sacroiliac joint","C":"Muscle energy mobilization of the restricted sacroiliac joint","D":"Forceful rhythmic springing of the sacrum to mobilize it","E":"Gentle indirect techniques that do not move the inflamed joint"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Active inflammatory joint disease such as sacroiliitis in ankylosing spondylitis is a relative contraindication. Direct-action manipulation aggravates joint inflammation, so the course uses gentle lymphatic drainage, balanced ligamentous tension, or counterstrain without moving the inflamed joint. Forceful springing of the sacrum is direct-action manipulation of the inflamed joint, which aggravates it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the goal is to reduce local nociceptive afferent output.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct-action manipulation agg","B":"Direct-action manipulation agg","C":"Direct-action manipulation agg"}},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has acute psoas spasm with a pelvic side shift and a Type II FRS dysfunction rotated to the side of the tight psoas. According to the course, which region must be treated first?","choices":{"A":"The sacral base on the side of the shift","B":"The ilium on the side of the tight psoas muscle","C":"The upper lumbar key level at L1 or L2","D":"The lumbosacral junction at L5-S1","E":"The pubic symphysis and the pubic rami"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course calls the upper lumbar L1 or L2 Type II FRS dysfunction the key dysfunction that must be resolved first. Failing to release it causes lower lumbosacral and sacroiliac dysfunctions to recur rapidly. The sacral base, ilium, and pubic region are not the key; the course says pelvic and sacroiliac dysfunctions recur rapidly unless the L1-L2 dysfunction is released first.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: HVLA or muscle energy resolves the L1 and L2 dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The sacral base, ilium, and pu","E":"The sacral base, ilium, and pu"}},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old football lineman has L5 spondylolisthesis with extension-sensitive low back pain. He wears a TLSO brace during the acute phase and is now referred for physical therapy. According to the course, which program is prescribed?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar extension exercises with hip flexor strengthening","B":"Back extensor strengthening with prone press-ups","C":"Psoas strengthening with daily lumbar extension stretches","D":"Abdominal core strengthening with hamstring stretching","E":"Bed rest followed by extension-biased mobility drills"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course prescribes an antilordotic physical therapy program for spondylolisthesis: abdominal core strengthening, hamstring stretching, and avoidance of spinal extension, with a TLSO brace in the acute phase to limit shear. Lumbar extension exercises, prone press-ups, and extension-biased drills all load the unstable pars defect, and the pain is extension-sensitive. Hip flexor and psoas strengthening pull the lumbar spine forward and add compressive load, which is why the course uses gentle psoas release instead. Bed rest is not part of the plan; the course wants the patient to stay active while braced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spondylolisthesis is anterior translation of one vertebra over the one below, most often at L5-S1. COMLEX: antilordotic rehabilitation means core strengthening, hamstring stretching, and no extension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Lumbar extension exercises, pr","E":"Bed rest is not part of the pl"}},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply osteopathic principles to select appropriate treatment strategies for patients with specific low back pain conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman develops a new leg length discrepancy and pelvic unleveling after a total hip replacement. According to the course, which measure is added to OMT?","choices":{"A":"Bed rest for 1 to 2 weeks to let the pelvis settle after surgery","B":"Psoas strengthening exercise to correct the pelvic tilt","C":"Pelvic HVLA for each pelvic shear found on the exam","D":"Heavy lifting restrictions for 6 months after the surgery","E":"Customized shoe or heel orthotics to level the sacral base"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For post-surgical patients with a new acute leg length discrepancy, the course combines OMT with customized shoe or heel orthotics to restore sacral base levelness. Pelvic shears are already part of the OMT itself, and the course does not add psoas strengthening or lifting restrictions; it advises against bed rest beyond 3 days.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the discrepancy places traction on the iliolumbar ligaments and perpetuates quadratus lumborum trigger points.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 1 — clinical conditions that cause low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":2,"stem":"In the course's biomechanical analysis of the case, a 52-year-old man has intermittent sharp pain radiating along the back of the leg but not past the knee. According to the course, why does this mimic radiculopathy?","choices":{"A":"The L5 disk extrudes and compresses the S1 nerve root directly","B":"Piriformis spasm refers pain down the right posterior thigh","C":"Sacral torsion compresses the sciatic nerve at the greater sciatic notch","D":"Left knee meniscus inflammation refers pain upward along the leg","E":"A hypertonic psoas compresses the femoral nerve in the pelvis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the biomechanical analysis, the hypertonic right piriformis muscle directly refers pain down the right posterior thigh, mimicking radiculopathy. The course does not describe sciatic compression by the sacral torsion; the torsion is a link in the gait chain that leads to the piriformis hypertonicity. The case attributes the posterior leg pain to hypertonic right piriformis referral that mimics radiculopathy, not to an extruded disc compressing S1. The left knee meniscus injury started the gait chain and its pain has resolved; the course credits the posterior thigh pain to right piriformis spasm. The case attributes the pain to hypertonic piriformis referring pain down the posterior thigh, not to femoral nerve compression.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: counterstrain is used to release the hypertonic piriformis in this case.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The case attributes the posterior leg pain to hypertonic rig","D":"The left knee meniscus injury started the gait chain and its","E":"The case attributes the pain to hypertonic piriformis referr"}},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":2,"stem":"In the course's case, the patient has chronic asthma. According to the course, which finding reflects its chronic viscerosomatic reflex?","choices":{"A":"An FRS dysfunction of L5 with a positive lumbar spring test","B":"An inhalation restriction of the right 12th rib and diaphragm","C":"Tissue texture changes and tenderness at the T4 to T5 paraspinals","D":"A left sacral torsion on a right oblique axis of the sacrum","E":"Tenderness of the left medial meniscus counterstrain point"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The neurological analysis states that chronic asthma maintains localized autonomic facilitation that appears as a persistent viscerosomatic reflex, with tissue texture changes and tenderness in the T4 to T5 paraspinal region. The L5 FRS with a positive spring test, the 12th rib and diaphragm restriction, and the left-on-right sacral torsion belong to the case's gait chain and diaphragm findings, not to the asthma reflex. The course ties the chronic asthma reflex to tissue texture changes and tenderness at the T4 to T5 paraspinals, not to a knee counterstrain point.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: treating the T4 to T5 reflex helps normalize sympathetic outflow to the lungs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"The course ties the chronic asthma reflex to tissue texture "}},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the Five Osteopathic Models to a foundational clinical case of low back pain to outline how these factors influence a patient's presentation and recovery.","batch":2,"stem":"In the course's case, the patient has trouble finding a comfortable position in bed. According to the course, which sleep ergonomics does the behavioral plan teach?","choices":{"A":"Lying on his side with knees bent around a pillow","B":"Lying prone with a pillow under the abdomen","C":"Lying supine with a rolled towel under the neck and shoulders","D":"Sleeping upright in a reclining chair at night","E":"Lying supine with the legs straight and no pillow support"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The behavioral plan teaches supportive sleep ergonomics, such as lying on his side with knees bent around a pillow, to reduce dural tension and improve sleep quality. Sleeping upright in a recliner is not in the course's plan; side-lying with knees bent is also the position the patient reports relieves his pain. Lying prone with a pillow under the abdomen is not the position the behavioral plan teaches. Lying supine with a rolled towel under the neck is not the sleep position taught. Lying supine with legs straight and no pillow support is not the sleep ergonomics taught; the plan uses side-lying with knees bent.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the plan also gives reassurance that most acute low back pain episodes resolve within 6 weeks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 -> Obj 1 — the five models of osteopathic patient care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Lying prone with a pillow under the abdomen is not the posit","C":"Lying supine with a rolled towel under the neck is not the s","E":"Lying supine with legs straight and no pillow support is not"}}],"7":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is gerontology?","choices":{"A":"The clinical care of the medical issues of the elderly","B":"The treatment of age-related pathological states","C":"The study of normal child development","D":"The science of the aging process itself","E":"The pharmacology of age-related drug clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course defines gerontology as the field of science pertaining specifically to the study of the aging process itself. Geriatrics is the clinical field dedicated to the health, care, and unique medical issues of the elderly. Treating age-related pathological states is the clinical side, geriatrics, and drug clearance is a pharmacokinetic change the course places under the metabolic model. Gerontology is the science of the aging process itself, not the study of normal child development.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: gerontologic change is normal aging, whereas geriatric disease is a pathological state.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Gerontology is the science of the aging process itself, not "}},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which of these is a gerontologic change rather than a geriatric disease?","choices":{"A":"Dementia","B":"Osteoarthritis","C":"Multiple myeloma","D":"Severe pneumonia","E":"Sarcopenia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists sarcopenia, bone demineralization, increased body fat, and decreased total body water as gerontologic changes. Dementia, osteoarthritis, multiple myeloma, and severe pneumonia are the course's examples of geriatric diseases, which are non-universal pathological states.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: gerontologic changes are universal and not diseases, but they reduce physiologic reserve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which feature distinguishes dementia from the cognitive changes of normal aging?","choices":{"A":"Mild benign delay in recalling memories with preserved independence","B":"Subtle cerebral volume loss with slower recall that does not limit daily activities","C":"Severe short-term memory loss with disorientation and functional decline","D":"Slower processing speed with intact orientation and daily function","E":"Occasional word-finding trouble that improves with rest or cues"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course contrasts the mild, benign recall delays of normal aging with dementia, a progressive degenerative disease with severe short-term memory loss, disorientation, and significant functional decline. Occasional word-finding trouble that improves with cues fits the mild, benign recall delay of normal aging, not dementia. Subtle volume loss with slower recall that does not limit daily activities is normal aging, not dementia. Slower processing with intact orientation and daily function is normal aging; dementia causes disorientation and functional decline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal aging includes subtle cerebral volume loss and slower recall, which are not dementia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Subtle volume loss with slower recall that does not limit da","D":"Slower processing with intact orientation and daily function"}},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is homeostenosis?","choices":{"A":"A fixed hypothalamic thermostat set point in older adults","B":"Sudden loss of homeostasis after a single minor stressor","C":"Expansion of reserves after chronic stress in aging","D":"Progressive constriction of homeostatic reserves with aging","E":"Narrowing of arteries from age-related calcification"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Homeostenosis is the progressive constriction of the body's homeostatic reserves that occurs as a natural consequence of physiological aging. It is neither a fixed thermostat set point nor arterial narrowing; what narrows is the margin of physiologic reserve. Homeostenosis is a progressive narrowing of reserves, not a sudden loss after a single stressor. Homeostenosis is a constriction, not an expansion, of homeostatic reserves with aging.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: homeostenosis is a normal consequence of aging, not itself a disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Homeostenosis is a progressive narrowing of reserves, not a ","C":"Homeostenosis is a constriction, not an expansion, of homeos","E":"It is neither a fixed thermost"}},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which is a key feature of homeostenosis?","choices":{"A":"A wider margin for iatrogenic disruption","B":"A decreased ability to adapt to stimuli","C":"Enhanced recovery after stressors","D":"Organ systems working with a large unused capacity","E":"Expanded alveolar surface area"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because baseline reserves are depleted, the elderly person has impaired adaptability, a decreased ability to adapt to internal or external stimuli. A wider margin, enhanced recovery, large unused capacity, and expanded alveolar surface are the opposite of the course's features: a narrow margin for error, reduced forces of recovery, organs near maximum capacity, and decreased alveolar surface area.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: impaired adaptability means a smaller stimulus can destabilize an older adult.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does the course advise starting low and going slow with medications and interventions in older adults?","choices":{"A":"Homeostenosis makes their effects unpredictable","B":"Older patients clear drugs faster than young adults","C":"Starting low avoids the need to monitor responses","D":"Slow advancement prevents the development of drug tolerance","E":"Frail patients tolerate larger stimuli but recover slowly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that homeostenosis makes the effects of medications and therapeutic interventions highly unpredictable, so clinicians prescribe at lower initial doses and advance them slowly. Older adults clear drugs more slowly, not faster (reduced GFR, creatinine clearance, and liver mass), and starting low does not remove the need to monitor, because the effects are unpredictable. The reason given is unpredictable effects from homeostenosis, not prevention of drug tolerance. The course says elderly patients are highly sensitive to OMT dosage and need a smaller physical stimulus, not a larger one.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the same principle applies to OMT dose in frail patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The reason given is unpredictable effects from homeostenosis","E":"The course says elderly patients are highly sensitive to OMT"}},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the recommended starting heel lift in an older adult with a leg length discrepancy?","choices":{"A":"1/4 inch to correct the full radiographic discrepancy","B":"1/2 inch with upward adjustment at each weekly visit","C":"1/8 inch or half the radiographic discrepancy","D":"The full measured discrepancy in a single step","E":"3/4 inch followed by gradual reduction over months"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course advises a very small, gentle dose, typically starting at 1/8 inch or less, or half of the measured radiographic discrepancy, and adjusting upward in 1/8 inch increments every 2 to 4 weeks. One-quarter inch is larger than the course's starting lift of 1/8 inch or less, or half the radiographic discrepancy. One-half inch is far larger than the 1/8 inch starting lift; the course adjusts upward in 1/8 inch steps every 2 to 4 weeks. Correcting the full discrepancy at once is too aggressive; the course starts at 1/8 inch or less, or half the measured discrepancy. Three-quarters of an inch is far larger than the recommended starting lift of 1/8 inch.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: balance the sacral base with OMT and core strengthening before attempting lift therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — adapting OMT for the low-reserve patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"One-quarter inch is larger than the course's starting lift o","B":"One-half inch is far larger than the 1/8 inch starting lift;","D":"Correcting the full discrepancy at once is too aggressive; t","E":"Three-quarters of an inch is far larger than the recommended"}},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which dysfunctions should OMT target to restore equilibrium and reduce lower leg muscle tension in older adults?","choices":{"A":"Extension dysfunctions of the lumbar, sacral, and pelvic regions and the hips","B":"Flexion dysfunctions of the upper thoracic, cervical, and craniocervical regions","C":"Rotational dysfunctions of the hip, knee, and ankle joints of the lower limb","D":"Inhalation dysfunctions of the lower, middle, and upper ribs and the diaphragm","E":"Sidebending dysfunctions of the clavicle, scapula, and humerus of the shoulder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OMT should target flexion dysfunctions of the upper thoracic, cervical, and cranio-cervical junctions. Resolving them restores normal head position, returns the visual field to the horizon, and shifts the vestibular apparatus back to a stable neutral position. The dysfunctions the course targets are flexion dysfunctions of the upper thoracic, cervical, and craniocervical regions, not lumbar and pelvic extension dysfunctions. Rotation dysfunctions of the lower limb joints are not the target the course names for restoring equilibrium. Rib inhalation dysfunctions are not the target; the course names upper thoracic, cervical, and craniocervical flexion dysfunctions. Shoulder girdle sidebending dysfunctions are not the target the course names.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course places this under the Neurological Model.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — adapting OMT for the low-reserve patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The dysfunctions the course targets are flexion dysfunctions","C":"Rotation dysfunctions of the lower limb joints are not the t","D":"Rib inhalation dysfunctions are not the target; the course n","E":"Shoulder girdle sidebending dysfunctions are not the target "}},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how much water should an older adult consume daily, prescribed like a medication?","choices":{"A":"1,200 to 1,500 ml","B":"500 to 800 ml","C":"2,000 to 2,500 ml","D":"1,600 to 1,800 ml","E":"800 to 1,000 ml"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Under the Metabolic Model the course directs 1,200 to 1,500 ml of water daily and protein intake of 0.8 g/kg of body weight to preserve muscle mass. Five hundred to eight hundred ml is below the course's prescription of 1,200 to 1,500 ml daily. Two to two and a half liters exceeds the course's prescription of 1,200 to 1,500 ml daily. 1,600 to 1,800 ml is higher than the course's prescription of 1,200 to 1,500 ml daily. 800 to 1,000 ml is below the course's prescription of 1,200 to 1,500 ml daily.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a senescent decline in thirst receptors leaves older adults vulnerable to rapid dehydration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — adapting OMT for the low-reserve patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Five hundred to eight hundred ml is below the course's presc","C":"Two to two and a half liters exceeds the course's prescripti","D":"1,600 to 1,800 ml is higher than the course's prescription o","E":"800 to 1,000 ml is below the course's prescription of 1,200 "}},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has bilateral high-frequency hearing loss and reduced lens elasticity but lives independently and has intact cognition. According to the course, how are these findings classified?","choices":{"A":"Gerontologic changes of normal aging","B":"Geriatric diseases from failed homeostasis","C":"Early signs of dementia","D":"Complications of multiple myeloma","E":"Atypical presentations of infection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists presbycusis, bilateral sensorineural high-frequency hearing loss, and presbyopia, loss of lens elasticity, among the universal, non-pathological gerontologic changes. Because these changes are universal and non-pathological, they are not geriatric disease, dementia (which needs severe memory loss and functional decline), myeloma, or an atypical infection.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: gerontologic changes are not diseases, but they increase vulnerability to disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman with a chronic lower urinary tract infection presents with fatigue and confusion but no urinary complaints. According to the course, how is this best described?","choices":{"A":"A gerontologic change with localized urinary symptoms","B":"Normal aging that does not require further evaluation","C":"A gerontologic change from reduced bladder compliance","D":"A homeostenosis state that resolves without treatment","E":"A geriatric disease presenting as an atypical systemic illness"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists severe infections such as pneumonia or chronic lower urinary tract infections as geriatric diseases that present as atypical, systemic illnesses rather than localized complaints. Infection is a pathological geriatric disease, not normal aging or a gerontologic change, and homeostenosis reduces the forces of recovery, so it does not resolve on its own.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: infections in older adults can present as atypical, systemic illnesses rather than localized complaints.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review gerontology and discuss the difference between gerontologic change and geriatric disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has symptomatic knee degeneration with cartilage destruction. According to the course, how does this differ from normal aging?","choices":{"A":"A gerontologic change that appears universally in older adults as they age","B":"A pathological geriatric disease called osteoarthritis","C":"Sarcopenia from progressive loss of skeletal muscle mass with aging","D":"A clonal plasma cell disorder that infiltrates the bone marrow","E":"Demineralization of bone with brittleness and fracture risk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course describes osteoarthritis as symptomatic joint degeneration and cartilage destruction, as opposed to the minor, asymptomatic joint changes that may occur with age. Sarcopenia and bone demineralization are gerontologic body-composition changes, and a clonal plasma cell disorder is multiple myeloma. Osteoarthritis is not a universal aging change; it is symptomatic degeneration with cartilage destruction, unlike the minor asymptomatic changes of aging.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: normal gerontologic changes in the joints can be minor and asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — falls and decreased range of motion in geriatric patients","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Osteoarthritis is not a universal aging change; it is sympto","C":"Sarcopenia and bone deminerali"}},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 80-year-old man on stable diuretic therapy develops volume depletion and acute renal failure after a minor increase in diuresis. According to the course, which feature of homeostenosis does this illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced tolerance of iatrogenic disruption","B":"Expanded physiologic reserve in advanced age","C":"Faster recovery after dehydration","D":"A narrow margin for clinical error","E":"Compensatory hormonal expansion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course gives this example: a minor overdiuresis in a stable frail patient can rapidly precipitate volume depletion and acute renal failure, and slightly rapid fluid administration can trigger congestive heart failure. Enhanced tolerance, expanded reserve, faster recovery, and compensatory expansion all contradict homeostenosis, the narrowing of reserves.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: frail older adults have very little tolerance for iatrogenic or environmental disruptions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 101-year-old man decompensates into multi-system organ failure after a minor stressor. According to the course, which feature of homeostenosis explains his instability?","choices":{"A":"Excess physiologic reserve that delays recovery","B":"Increased adaptability to external stimuli","C":"Reduced forces of recovery in advanced age","D":"A wide margin that requires faster dosing","E":"Rapid restoration of baseline homeostasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course notes that in advanced age, particularly in centenarians, the inherent forces keeping them from decompensation are reduced, so a stressor can push the patient into instability and rapid multi-system failure. Excess reserve, increased adaptability, and a wide margin are the opposite of homeostenosis, whose margin for error is narrow. Rapid restoration of baseline is the opposite of homeostenosis, in which recovery forces are reduced.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: unaddressed decompensation can progress to organ failure and death.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Rapid restoration of baseline is the opposite of homeostenos"}},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the concept of Homeostenosis.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman has reduced CD8 T-cell function, decreased alveolar surface area, and a lower GFR. According to the course, which feature of homeostenosis do these changes represent?","choices":{"A":"Reduction of systemic reserves","B":"Expansion of reserves through compensation","C":"A wide margin for physiologic error","D":"Failure of a single organ system","E":"Progressive loss of thirst receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course cites reduced CD8 T-cell function, decreased alveolar surface area, and a drop in GFR as examples of reduced systemic reserves, leaving organ systems operating closer to maximum capacity under basal conditions. A wide margin is the opposite of homeostenosis, and the decline in thirst receptors is a metabolic-model change linked to dehydration, not one of these cited reserve losses. Reduced CD8 function, alveolar surface, and GFR are losses of reserve, not an expansion of reserves. These changes affect immune, pulmonary, and renal systems, so they reflect widespread reduced reserves rather than failure of a single system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GFR declines with normal aging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — immune and endocrine changes with aging","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Reduced CD8 function, alveolar surface, and GFR are losses o","D":"These changes affect immune, pulmonary, and renal systems, s"}},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":2,"stem":"An older woman is admitted with pneumonia and is a candidate for adjunctive OMT. According to the SDL 7 course notes, what did standardized twice-daily OMT protocols show for elderly patients in the MOPSE trial?","choices":{"A":"Shorter hospitalization with no change in mortality","B":"Lower mortality with no change in length of stay","C":"No change in stay or mortality","D":"Shorter hospitalization and lower mortality","E":"A higher rate of rib fracture"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under the Respiratory-Circulatory Model the course states that standardized, twice-daily adjunctive OMT protocols, as in the MOPSE trial, significantly reduced the length of hospitalization and decreased mortality in elderly patients admitted with pneumonia. A higher fracture rate is not an outcome the course reports for the trial, which it cites for shorter stays and lower mortality. The SDL 23 notes break the result down by age (shorter stay in adults aged 50–74 and lower mortality in patients aged 75 and older), and the pulmonary lab slides report the per-protocol reduction in length of stay; taken together, the trial is cited for both outcomes.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: OMT focuses on restoring motion of the thoracic cage, ribs, and diaphragm to promote congestion clearance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — lymphatic pump and rib raising in respiratory disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man is taught slow deep breathing to improve autonomic tone. According to the course, which rate and effect are targeted?","choices":{"A":"10 to 12 breaths per minute, raising sympathetic tone","B":"20 to 24 breaths per minute, lowering baroreflex sensitivity","C":"5 to 7 breaths per minute, raising heart rate variability","D":"30 to 40 breaths per minute, raising vital capacity","E":"3 to 4 breaths per minute, lowering vagal tone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course prescribes slow, deep breathing at 5 to 7 breaths per minute, which stimulates Traube-Hering-wave oscillations in autonomic tone and enhances heart rate variability and baroreflex sensitivity. Ten to twelve breaths per minute is not the targeted rate; the course prescribes slow deep breathing at 5 to 7 breaths per minute to enhance autonomic rhythms. Twenty to twenty-four breaths per minute is not the targeted rate; the course prescribes slow deep breathing at 5 to 7 breaths per minute to enhance autonomic rhythms. Thirty to forty breaths per minute is not the targeted rate; the course prescribes slow deep breathing at 5 to 7 breaths per minute to enhance autonomic rhythms. Three to four breaths per minute is not the targeted rate; the course prescribes slow deep breathing at 5 to 7 breaths per minute to enhance autonomic rhythms.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this prescription falls under the Respiratory-Circulatory Model.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 6 — integrative treatment plans that balance autonomic tone","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten to twelve breaths per minute is not the targeted rate; t","B":"Twenty to twenty-four breaths per minute is not the targeted","D":"Thirty to forty breaths per minute is not the targeted rate;","E":"Three to four breaths per minute is not the targeted rate; t"}},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss how gerontologic changes alter the osteopathic approach to the maintenance of homeostasis through the lens of the five models of osteopathic care.","batch":2,"stem":"A geriatric clinic wants a framework for structuring age-friendly care. According to the course, which four elements make up the 4Ms?","choices":{"A":"Mobility, Mood, Memory, and Muscle strength","B":"What Matters, Medication, Mentation, and Mobility","C":"Medication, Morbidity, Mortality, and Medical costs","D":"What Matters, Metabolism, Mentation, and Meal planning","E":"Mentation, Mood, Mobility, and Medical history"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under the Behavioral Model the course uses the 4Ms framework of What Matters, Medication, Mentation, and Mobility to structure care and tailor plans to the patient's goals. Mood, memory, and muscle strength are not the 4Ms; the 4Ms are What Matters, Medication, Mentation, and Mobility. Morbidity, mortality, and medical costs are not the 4Ms elements. Metabolism and meal planning are not among the 4Ms. Mood and medical history are not among the 4Ms, which are What Matters, Medication, Mentation, and Mobility.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: talking about What Matters allows comfortable trade-offs between symptom control and treatment burden.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for low-reserve patients","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Mood, memory, and muscle strength are not the 4Ms; the 4Ms a","C":"Morbidity, mortality, and medical costs are not the 4Ms elem","D":"Metabolism and meal planning are not among the 4Ms.","E":"Mood and medical history are not among the 4Ms, which are Wh"}}],"8":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how are articular restrictions treated in a frail patient?","choices":{"A":"High-velocity, low-amplitude rotary thrust","B":"Rapid, high-force direct techniques at the barrier","C":"Low-velocity, moderate-to-high-amplitude articulation","D":"Forceful, sustained traction held at end range","E":"Repeated, rapid springing of the restricted segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course treats articular restrictions with low-velocity, moderate-to-high-amplitude articulation procedures rather than rapid, high-force direct techniques. Forceful traction held at end range is a high-force direct approach, which the course replaces with gentle articulation in frail patients. High-velocity, low-amplitude thrusts are rapid direct techniques that the course replaces with gentle articulation in frail patients. Repeated rapid springing is a rapid direct technique that the course does not use in frail patients.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the technique is chosen by the structural component that predominates in the somatic dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"High-velocity, low-amplitude thrusts are rapid direct techni","E":"Repeated rapid springing is a rapid direct technique that th"}},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which techniques manage neural reflex and nociceptive irritability in a frail patient?","choices":{"A":"Strain-counterstrain and facilitated positional release","B":"Muscle energy and high-velocity thrust procedures at end range","C":"Myofascial release and deep soft tissue stretching","D":"Articulation and high-velocity thrust techniques","E":"Lymphatic pump and visceral manipulation methods"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neural reflex and nociceptive irritability are managed with indirect, non-threatening, passive techniques such as strain-counterstrain and facilitated positional release to reduce neural reflex activity. Muscle energy and thrust are direct techniques (muscle energy is used only in modified form, for myofascial tension), and lymphatic pump or visceral methods are not the course's choice for nociceptive irritability. The course uses gentle myofascial release and soft tissue stretching for myofascial tension, not for neural reflex irritability, which calls for passive indirect techniques. Articulation and high-velocity thrust are direct techniques, not the indirect techniques the course uses for nociceptive irritability.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: myofascial and soft tissue tension is addressed with gentle myofascial release, soft tissue stretching, or modified muscle energy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The course uses gentle myofascial release and soft tissue st","D":"Articulation and high-velocity thrust are direct techniques,"}},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how long can post-treatment rebound reactions last in elderly patients?","choices":{"A":"30 to 90 minutes","B":"12 to 48 hours","C":"5 to 7 days","D":"2 to 6 hours","E":"2 to 3 weeks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rebound reactions in the elderly, from mild fatigue to increased pain at the chief complaint site, are slower and can last 12 to 48 hours. Thirty to ninety minutes is shorter, and five to seven days or two to three weeks longer, than the course's range. Two to six hours is shorter than the course's rebound range of 12 to 48 hours.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: warn the patient about potential post-treatment rebound reactions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Two to six hours is shorter than the course's rebound range "}},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, a Timed Up and Go test longer than how many seconds places an older adult at high risk for falling?","choices":{"A":"6 seconds","B":"8 seconds","C":"20 seconds","D":"12 seconds","E":"10 seconds"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An older adult taking more than 12 seconds to complete the Timed Up and Go test is categorized as at high risk for falling. Six seconds is well under the course's high-risk threshold of more than 12 seconds. Eight seconds is below the threshold; high risk begins at more than 12 seconds. Twenty seconds is higher than the course's stated threshold of more than 12 seconds. Ten seconds is below the course's threshold of more than 12 seconds.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: benzodiazepine use is also highly associated with falls, delirium, and depression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3 — gerontologic changes through the five models","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Six seconds is well under the course's high-risk threshold o","B":"Eight seconds is below the threshold; high risk begins at mo","C":"Twenty seconds is higher than the course's stated threshold ","E":"Ten seconds is below the course's threshold of more than 12 "}},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, weakness or inhibition of which muscle leaves older patients vulnerable to falling sideways?","choices":{"A":"Gluteus maximus","B":"Rectus femoris","C":"Gluteus medius","D":"Tibialis anterior","E":"Iliacus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weakness or inhibition of the gluteus medius impairs lateral pelvic stabilization in the coronal plane, leaving patients vulnerable to falling sideways, which carries the highest risk of hip fracture. The rectus femoris, tibialis anterior, and iliacus are not the course's lateral pelvic stabilizers; the other hip muscle it links to falls is a tight psoas major, which stresses the femoral neck. The course ties sideways falls to weakness of the gluteus medius, which stabilizes the pelvis in the coronal plane, not the gluteus maximus.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronic spasm of the psoas major stresses the femoral neck and contributes to mechanical falls.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3 — gerontologic changes through the five models","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course ties sideways falls to weakness of the gluteus me"}},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which four findings independently predict hip fracture from falls?","choices":{"A":"Slow gait speed, poor tandem walk, reduced visual acuity, and small calf circumference","B":"Slow gait speed, reduced grip strength, poor hearing, and a large calf circumference","C":"Fast gait speed, good tandem walk, reduced hearing, and a small calf circumference","D":"Reduced vibratory sense, short reaction time, poor dentition, and a low body weight","E":"Slow reaction time, reduced vibratory sense, low thirst drive, and a large calf circumference"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists slower gait speed, difficulty with tandem walk, reduced visual acuity, and small calf circumference, which reflects gastrocnemius atrophy, as independent predictors of hip fracture from falls. Reduced grip strength, poor hearing, and a large calf circumference are not the course's predictors; a small calf circumference predicts fracture. The course names slower gait speed and difficulty with tandem walk as predictors of hip fracture, not fast gait speed and good tandem walk.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: benzodiazepine use is also highly associated with falls, delirium, and depression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 3 — gerontologic changes through the five models","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Reduced grip strength, poor hearing, and a large calf circum","C":"The course names slower gait speed and difficulty with tande"}},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which of these is an absolute contraindication to osteopathic treatment in a geriatric patient?","choices":{"A":"Spondylolisthesis","B":"Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","C":"Acute herniated disc with active radiculopathy","D":"Cauda equina syndrome","E":"Severe osteoporosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course lists cauda equina syndrome as an absolute contraindication, a medical and surgical emergency in which any manual delay or direct pressure on the compromised canal is contraindicated. The other listed conditions are relative, not absolute, contraindications.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: other absolute contraindications include acute vertebral fracture, ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, bony metastasis at the target segment, spinal osteomyelitis, and medical instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The other listed conditions ar","B":"The other listed conditions ar","C":"The other listed conditions ar","E":"The other listed conditions ar"}},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how must direct cervical mobilization be performed in a geriatric patient?","choices":{"A":"Rapidly with a rotary thrust through the barrier","B":"With maximal rotation before the barrier is engaged","C":"Briefly with a short lever thrust applied at end range","D":"With sustained traction and a forceful rotary stretch","E":"Slowly and gently with the barrier engaged before force"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Abrupt or rapid direct rotary forces on the cervical spine are contraindicated. Direct cervical mobilization must be slow and gentle, with the barrier clearly engaged before any force. A short-lever thrust at end range is an abrupt force, which the course contraindicates in the cervical spine. Maximal rotation before engaging the barrier is not allowed; the barrier must be engaged before any force. The course requires slow, gentle cervical mobilization with the barrier engaged first, so a forceful rotary stretch is not permitted.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cervical spine rotary restrictions are listed as a relative contraindication to direct techniques.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Maximal rotation before engaging the barrier is not allowed;","D":"The course requires slow, gentle cervical mobilization with "}},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which motion should be avoided in a patient with spinal stenosis and neurogenic claudication?","choices":{"A":"Forced lumbar flexion","B":"Forced lumbar extension","C":"Gentle cervical sidebending","D":"Passive hip abduction","E":"Diaphragmatic breathing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forced lumbar extension structurally narrows the exit foramina and central canal, so it is avoided in spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication. Gentle cervical sidebending, passive hip abduction, and diaphragmatic breathing do not narrow the lumbar canal, so the course does not restrict them. Forced flexion is not the motion to avoid; extension narrows the canal, whereas flexion relieves neurogenic claudication.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: forced lumbar extension narrows the exit foramina and central canal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Forced flexion is not the motion to avoid; extension narrows"}},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old frail woman has lumbosacral, pelvic, and lower extremity somatic dysfunctions. According to the course, what is the logical treatment sequence?","choices":{"A":"Sacrum, then lumbar spine, then psoas major, with the ilia and pubes last","B":"Pubes, then sacrum, then ilia, with the lumbar spine and psoas major last","C":"Ilia, then sacrum, then psoas major, with the lumbar spine and pubes last","D":"Lumbar spine, then psoas major, then ilia and pubes, with the sacrum last","E":"Psoas major, then pubes, then sacrum, with the ilia and lumbar spine last"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course advises treating the lumbar spine, psoas major, ilia, and pubes before the sacrum because the sacrum must accommodate forces from both above and below. The sacrum is treated last, not first, because it must accommodate forces from above and below. The sacrum is treated last after the lumbar spine, psoas major, ilia, and pubes; starting with the pubes is not the course sequence. The lumbar spine and pubes are not last; the sacrum is treated last after the lumbar spine, psoas, ilia, and pubes. The lumbar spine is treated first, and the sacrum last, not the psoas first and the lumbar spine last.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: sacral work comes last in lumbosacral, pelvic, and lower extremity treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The sacrum is treated last, not first, because it must accom","B":"The sacrum is treated last after the lumbar spine, psoas maj","C":"The lumbar spine and pubes are not last; the sacrum is treat","E":"The lumbar spine is treated first, and the sacrum last, not "}},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old frail man remains unresponsive after three OMT sessions. According to the course, what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Increase the dose and frequency of treatment until the patient finally responds","B":"Switch to high-velocity thrust techniques at the restricted segments","C":"Continue the same plan for six more sessions before changing course","D":"Discharge the patient from OMT care and close the treatment plan","E":"Reevaluate the diagnosis for occult viscerosomatic or visceral pathology"},"correct":"E","explanation":"If a patient remains unresponsive after 2 or 3 treatments, the course says to reevaluate the diagnosis and investigate occult, unrecognized viscerosomatic etiologies or underlying visceral pathologies. Escalating to thrust is not the answer to non-response; in frail patients the course replaces rapid high-force techniques with gentle, low-velocity ones. Frail patients get lower doses advanced slowly, and non-response after 2 or 3 treatments prompts reevaluation of the diagnosis, not escalation. Continuing the same plan for six more sessions ignores the course's advice to reevaluate after 2 or 3 unresponsive treatments. The course says to reevaluate the diagnosis for occult pathology rather than discharge the patient.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: viscerosomatic reflexes are addressed primarily by treating the underlying visceral pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — treatment focus of viscerosomatic reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Frail patients get lower doses advanced slowly, and non-resp","C":"Continuing the same plan for six more sessions ignores the c","D":"The course says to reevaluate the diagnosis for occult patho"}},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Formulate a treatment plan to address the SD of the frail, debilitated or those with decreased functional reserve.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 79-year-old woman has increased pain at her chief complaint site 24 hours after soft tissue OMT. According to the course, how should the next visit be timed?","choices":{"A":"The next day at a higher dose to overcome the reaction","B":"Within 24 hours with repeated soft tissue work at higher force","C":"3 days to 2 weeks later to allow tissues to recover","D":"As soon as fatigue resolves using thrust techniques","E":"After 2 months with no treatment in the interim"},"correct":"C","explanation":"If rebound reactions occur, the course says patients should be given 3 days to 2 weeks between interventions to allow tissues to recover. Without rebound, acute conditions can be followed up in 2 to 3 days with a decreased initial dose. Thrust techniques do not suit a frail patient with a rebound reaction, and the course's spacing tops out at 2 weeks rather than months without care. A higher dose the next day ignores the 3 days to 2 weeks the course gives for tissue recovery. Repeating higher-force soft tissue work within 24 hours ignores the 3-day to 2-week recovery period.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: frail patients need a smaller stimulus because they are prone to exaggerated responses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 -> Obj 2 — homeostenosis and low reserve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A higher dose the next day ignores the 3 days to 2 weeks the","B":"Repeating higher-force soft tissue work within 24 hours igno"}},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man has decreased passive ankle dorsiflexion and an unsteady gait. According to the course, how does this contribute to falls?","choices":{"A":"It increases cervical extension during the stance phase","B":"It strengthens lateral pelvic stabilization while walking","C":"It shortens reaction time to unexpected perturbations","D":"It alters foot clearance and ground-reactive forces","E":"It lowers the visual field below the level of the horizon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of passive ankle dorsiflexion and restricted ankle mobility alter foot clearance and ground-reactive forces, directly contributing to gait instability. Cervical posture and a lowered visual field come from upper thoracic flexion with a forward-flexed head, not from the ankle, and ankle restriction does not strengthen lateral pelvic stabilization, which depends on the gluteus medius. Restricted ankle dorsiflexion does not shorten reaction time; it alters foot clearance and ground-reactive forces.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: restoring ankle flexion and extension is essential for upright balance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Neurological Model and standing balance","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Cervical posture and a lowered","C":"Restricted ankle dorsiflexion does not shorten reaction time"}},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has increased upper thoracic flexion and decreased cervico-occipital extension. According to the course, why does this raise her fall risk?","choices":{"A":"The head-flexed position lowers the visual field and shifts the vestibular neutral position","B":"The flexed head raises the visual field above the horizon and overstimulates the vestibular system","C":"The posture increases ankle dorsiflexion and overloads ground-reactive force absorption at the foot","D":"The posture lengthens her Timed Up and Go time by weakening the gluteus medius","E":"The posture impairs the righting reflex by causing cervical radiculopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increased upper thoracic flexion with decreased cervico-occipital extension forces a forward head-flexed position that lowers the visual field below the horizon, shifts the neutral position of the vestibular apparatus, and triggers postural instability. A flexed head lowers the visual field below the horizon rather than raising it. The posture described is upper thoracic and cervical, not ankle dorsiflexion and ground-reactive forces. The posture alters the visual field and vestibular neutral position and does not act by weakening the gluteus medius. The course attributes the fall risk to a lowered visual field and shifted vestibular neutral position, not cervical radiculopathy.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a forward head-flexed position lowers the visual field below the horizon and shifts the vestibular neutral position.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Neurological Model and standing balance","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"A flexed head lowers the visual field below the horizon rath","C":"The posture described is upper thoracic and cervical, not an","D":"The posture alters the visual field and vestibular neutral p","E":"The course attributes the fall risk to a lowered visual fiel"}},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify common risk factors for falls in geriatric patients including decreased Range of Motion in the ankle, hips and lumbar spine.","batch":2,"stem":"An 85-year-old man with cataracts loses visual cues for balance. According to the course, what does he do to compensate, and why does it fall short?","choices":{"A":"He relaxes the lower leg muscles, but cataracts impair that compensation","B":"He stiffens the lower leg muscles, but sarcopenia impairs that compensation","C":"He widens his gait and base of support, but presbycusis impairs that compensation","D":"He flexes the cervical spine downward, but sarcopenia impairs that compensation","E":"He leans backward at the hips, but reduced vibratory sense impairs that compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When visual cues are lost, patients stiffen the lower leg muscles to compensate, but age-related sarcopenia impairs this compensation. Patients stiffen rather than relax the lower leg muscles when visual cues are lost. The course names stiffening of the lower leg muscles, impaired by sarcopenia, not a wider gait limited by presbycusis. The compensation is stiffening the lower leg muscles, not downward cervical flexion. The compensation is stiffening the lower leg muscles, not leaning backward at the hips.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: progressive CNS decline also reduces vibratory sense and the righting reflex and prolongs reaction time.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Neurological Model and standing balance","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Patients stiffen rather than relax the lower leg muscles whe","C":"The course names stiffening of the lower leg muscles, impair","D":"The compensation is stiffening the lower leg muscles, not do","E":"The compensation is stiffening the lower leg muscles, not le"}},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman has severe osteoporosis. According to the course, which technique should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"High-force downward compression on the thoracic cage in prone or supine positions","B":"Gentle strain-counterstrain applied at regions distant from the thoracic cage","C":"Balanced ligamentous tension applied to the sacrum in a supine position","D":"Gentle myofascial release of the thoracic paraspinal muscles with the patient seated","E":"Respiratory-assisted articulation of the ribs using patient inhalation and exhalation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In severe osteoporosis, high-force procedures that apply downward compression on the thoracic cage carry a high risk of costochondral, costovertebral, and midshaft rib fractures and must be avoided. Sacral balanced ligamentous tension and respiratory-assisted rib articulation are among the gentle approaches the course recommends for frail patients. Gentle strain-counterstrain at distant regions is a gentle approach the course recommends for frail patients. Gentle myofascial release is a gentle approach the course recommends for frail patients.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: severe osteoporosis raises the risk of rib fracture with high-force procedures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — HVLA contraindications in the thoracic lab","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Gentle strain-counterstrain at distant regions is a gentle a","D":"Gentle myofascial release is a gentle approach the course re"}},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has fever, night pain, and focal tenderness at L2-L3 two weeks after an episode of bacteremia, and MRI confirms acute diskitis at that level. According to the course, why must direct force at this segment be withheld?","choices":{"A":"It can rupture a dilated abdominal aorta","B":"It can worsen compression of the nerve root","C":"It can collapse the segment or spread infection","D":"It can narrow the central canal and exit foramina","E":"It can fracture ribs at the costochondral joints"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists acute spinal osteomyelitis or diskitis as an absolute contraindication because direct force can cause pathological collapse of the segment or hematogenous spread of the infection. Vascular rupture is the risk of an abdominal aortic aneurysm, not of an infected disc. Worsening nerve root compression is the reason to avoid thrusts at an acute disc herniation. Narrowing of the canal and foramina is the reason to avoid forced extension in spinal stenosis. Costochondral and rib fractures are the risk of thoracic cage compression in severe osteoporosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis. COMLEX: osteomyelitis or diskitis at the target segment is an absolute contraindication to direct force.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 3 — absolute and relative contraindications in low back pain","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify contraindications to osteopathic treatment in the geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has an acute herniated nucleus pulposus with active radiculopathy. According to the course, which approach is appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Apply a high-velocity thrust at the herniation level to decompress the nerve root","B":"Avoid thrusts at the herniation level and use gentle indirect OMT at related or distant regions","C":"Apply forced rotation at the herniated segment to reduce the nucleus pulposus protrusion","D":"Defer OMT entirely until the radiculopathy has fully resolved and imaging is repeated","E":"Apply sustained high-force traction through the herniated segment to retract the nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Direct thrusts at the level of herniation are contraindicated to avoid worsening nerve root compression. Safe management uses gentle indirect OMT such as strain-counterstrain, myofascial release, or balanced ligamentous tension at related or distant regions. OMT need not be deferred entirely; gentle indirect treatment at related or distant regions remains safe. Forced rotation at the herniated segment is a direct force at the herniation level that risks worsening root compression. Sustained high-force traction through the herniated segment is a direct force at the herniation level that the course avoids.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: acute herniation with active radiculopathy is a relative, not absolute, contraindication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — OMT principles in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Forced rotation at the herniated segment is a direct force a","D":"OMT need not be deferred entir","E":"Sustained high-force traction through the herniated segment "}}],"9":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how does Still technique move a somatic dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"From its position of ease through its restrictive barrier with continuous force","B":"Directly into its restrictive barrier with a rapid, small-amplitude impulse","C":"Into its position of ease without crossing the barrier","D":"Through its barrier using a patient isometric contraction against counterforce","E":"Toward the tender point of greatest comfort and then slowly back to neutral"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes Still technique as moving a somatic dysfunction from its position of ease (indirect) through its restrictive barrier (direct) while applying a continuous force vector. Moving toward a tender point's position of greatest comfort is the passive positioning of counterstrain; Still technique carries the segment on through the barrier. Still technique carries the dysfunction from its position of ease through the restrictive barrier with continuous force (course), not with a rapid small-amplitude impulse. Still technique carries the dysfunction from its position of ease through the restrictive barrier (course); it does not stop in the position of ease. Still technique carries the dysfunction through the restrictive barrier with continuous force (course), not with a patient isometric contraction against counterforce.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this indirect-to-direct path is why the course calls Still a hybrid technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Still technique carries the dysfunction from its position of ease through the restrictive barrier wi","C":"Still technique carries the dysfunction from its position of ease through the restrictive barrier (c","D":"Still technique carries the dysfunction through the restrict"}},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which dysfunctions does Still technique rapidly resolve in low back pain?","choices":{"A":"Iliolumbar, posterior sacroiliac, and sacrotuberous ligament laxities","B":"Mesenteric, hepatic, and splenic visceral restrictions","C":"Pelvic, lumbar, and sacral dysfunctions","D":"Cervical, upper thoracic, and rib dysfunctions","E":"Lymphatic, venous, and fascial pump dysfunctions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that Still technique rapidly resolves pelvic, lumbar, and sacral dysfunctions and is highly effective for specific unilateral pelvic shears, the innominate upslips. Ligamentous laxity of the iliolumbar, posterior sacroiliac, and sacrotuberous ligaments is the target of balanced ligamentous tension and ligamentous articular strain, and visceral restrictions are not among the course's Still indications.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: an innominate upslip is a unilateral pelvic shear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Ligamentous laxity of the ilio"}},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is Still technique indicated to rapidly resolve in a low back pain treatment plan?","choices":{"A":"Chronic, recurrent facet stiffness with loss of passive range of motion","B":"Ligamentous laxity, with microinstability of the iliolumbar and sacrotuberous ligaments","C":"Localized, tender muscle and joint pain with dominant tender points","D":"Acute, complex joint restrictions in the pelvis and lumbar spine","E":"Central pain sensitization, with anxiety, depression, and poor sleep latency"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the course's low back pain table, Still technique is indicated to rapidly resolve acute, complex joint restrictions of the pelvic and lumbar regions using an indirect-to-direct hybrid path. Chronic facet stiffness is the indication for side-lying articulation, and ligamentous laxity with microinstability is the indication for balanced ligamentous tension and ligamentous articular strain. Tender-point dominant localized pain is the indication for strain-counterstrain, not Still technique. Central sensitization with anxiety and poor sleep is addressed by osteopathy in the cranial field, not Still technique.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: match the technique to the dominant component of the dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Tender-point dominant localized pain is the indication for s","E":"Central sensitization with anxiety and poor sleep is address"}},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which position does the DO exaggerate in FPR?","choices":{"A":"The restrictive barrier using a short lever","B":"The neutral position using a short lever","C":"The tender point using a rapid impulse","D":"The side opposite the dysfunction using a long lever","E":"The position of ease using a long lever"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric directs the DO to exaggerate the named dysfunction or the position of ease with a long lever after disengaging the spine with compression. FPR uses no rapid impulse or tender-point target; after the exaggeration the rubric holds 3 to 5 seconds and returns to neutral. The rubric exaggerates the position of ease using a long lever, not the restrictive barrier with a short lever. The rubric exaggerates the position of ease, not the side opposite the dysfunction.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the phrase long lever is the rubric's wording for exaggerating the position of ease in FPR.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The rubric exaggerates the position of ease using a long lev","D":"The rubric exaggerates the position of ease, not the side op"}},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, what does the DO flatten when performing FPR for an articular dysfunction of the neck?","choices":{"A":"The thoracic kyphosis","B":"The lumbar lordosis","C":"The cervical lordosis","D":"The sacral base","E":"The occipital condyles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric for cervical FPR has the DO flatten the cervical lordosis after diagnosing the somatic dysfunction. Flattening the thoracic kyphosis is the thoracic FPR step, and the rubric's FPR has no sacral or occipital flattening step. The cervical FPR flattens the cervical lordosis, not the lumbar lordosis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: FPR starts by removing the natural curve of the region being treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The cervical FPR flattens the cervical lordosis, not the lum"}},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, in which position is the patient placed for FPR of the thoracic spine?","choices":{"A":"In the prone position","B":"In the seated position","C":"In the supine position","D":"In the standing position","E":"In the sidelying position"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient in the seated position, in contrast to the other body positions listed here. The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not prone. The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not supine. The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not standing. The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not side-lying.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric names the seated position for thoracic FPR.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not prone","C":"The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not supin","D":"The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not stand","E":"The thoracic FPR rubric places the patient seated, not side-"}},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which two types of somatic dysfunction are the common indications for Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Articular and myofascial somatic dysfunctions","B":"Visceral and lymphatic somatic dysfunctions","C":"Neurologic and vascular somatic dysfunctions","D":"Ligamentous and membranous somatic dysfunctions","E":"Cranial and sutural somatic dysfunctions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric lists two common indications: articular somatic dysfunctions with intersegmental motion restriction, and myofascial somatic dysfunctions with muscle hypertonicity or fascial bind. Visceral and lymphatic dysfunctions are not the rubric's indications for Still technique, which are articular and myofascial.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the two indications split into a joint component and a soft tissue component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Visceral and lymphatic dysfunctions are not the rubric's ind"}},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which of these is an absolute contraindication to Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Mild to moderate instability of the treated area","B":"An unstable fracture affected by treatment positioning","C":"Moderate to severe intervertebral foraminal stenosis","D":"Severe sprains and strains of the area to be treated","E":"A congenital anomaly that limits the needed position"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric lists four absolute contraindications. The first is an unstable fracture affected by treatment positioning. The other options are on the relative list.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric lists four absolute contraindications and six relative ones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The other options are on the r","C":"The other options are on the r","D":"The other options are on the r","E":"The other options are on the r"}},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which of these is a relative contraindication to Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Treatment positioning that exacerbates neurologic or life-threatening symptoms","B":"Mild restriction of a single intersegmental motion","C":"A somatic dysfunction with a clear position of ease","D":"Vertebrobasilar insufficiency or carotid pathology","E":"Fascial bind in the thoracolumbar region"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric lists vertebrobasilar insufficiency and/or carotid pathology among its six relative contraindications. Positioning that exacerbates neurologic or life-threatening symptoms is an absolute contraindication, while intersegmental motion restriction and fascial bind are the rubric's indications for Still technique, and the position of ease is where the technique starts.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric's relative list has six items, and this one is vascular.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where does the DO stand for seated cervical Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Seated at the side of the table","B":"Standing at the foot of the table","C":"Kneeling in front of the patient","D":"Seated facing the patient's knees","E":"Standing behind the patient"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For the seated cervical technique the patient is seated and the DO is standing behind the patient. The rubric has the DO standing behind the patient, not seated at the side. The patient is seated, so the DO stands behind the patient, not at the foot of the table. The rubric has the DO standing behind the patient, not kneeling in front. The rubric has the DO standing behind the patient, not seated facing the patient's knees.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: for the supine cervical version the DO is instead seated behind the patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The rubric has the DO standing behind the patient, not seated at the side.","B":"The patient is seated, so the DO stands behind the patient, ","C":"The rubric has the DO standing behind the patient, not kneel","D":"The rubric has the DO standing behind the patient, not seated facing the patient"}},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where do the index finger and thumb of the monitoring hand contact the spine in seated cervical Still technique?","choices":{"A":"The transverse processes of the affected segment","B":"The spinous process of the segment above the affected one","C":"The mastoid processes on both sides of the skull","D":"The occiput and the first rib on the affected side","E":"The angles of the mandible on both sides of the face"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The monitoring hand's index finger and thumb contact the transverse processes of the affected segment. No step in the rubric contacts the mandible; the head is controlled from its top. The monitoring fingers contact the transverse processes of the affected segment, not the spinous process of the segment above. The mastoid processes are not the contact points; the fingers monitor the transverse processes of the affected segment. The occiput and first rib are not the contact points; the monitoring fingers contact the affected segment's transverse processes.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the monitoring finger stays on the transverse processes of the affected segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The monitoring fingers contact the transverse processes of t","C":"The mastoid processes are not the contact points; the finger","D":"The occiput and first rib are not the contact points; the mo"}},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, what does the DO's other hand do in seated cervical Still technique?","choices":{"A":"Holds the patient's chin to guide rotation","B":"Contacts the top of the patient's head","C":"Cradles the occiput from below with the palm","D":"Rests on the contralateral shoulder of the patient","E":"Supports the patient's forehead from the front"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the seated cervical technique the DO's other hand is placed in contact with the top of the patient's head, as stated in the lab rubric. Cradling the occiput from below is not a step in the rubric's seated technique. The rubric has the other hand on top of the head, not holding the chin. The other hand contacts the top of the head, not the contralateral shoulder. The other hand contacts the top of the head, not the forehead.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the same hand placement is used in the supine cervical version.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The rubric has the other hand on top of the head, not holdin","D":"The other hand contacts the top of the head, not the contral","E":"The other hand contacts the top of the head, not the forehea"}},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO has positioned a patient's neck toward the position of ease and introduced compression to the dysfunctional segment during Still technique. According to the lab rubric, what is the next movement?","choices":{"A":"Hold the position of ease and slowly release the compression","B":"Thrust through the restrictive barrier with a maximal rotary force","C":"Move through neutral into the previously restricted range","D":"Add traction to the head and return directly to the neutral position","E":"Repeat the exaggeration of the position of ease on the opposite side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After compression, the monitoring arm simultaneously reduces flexion or extension and sidebending and rotates the spine through neutral and then into the previously restricted range. The rubric does not repeat the exaggeration on the opposite side; compression is reduced only after the spine reaches the previously restricted range. The rubric does not hold the position of ease and release; the spine is carried through neutral into the previously restricted range. Still technique is a continuous force through the barrier, not a thrust with maximal rotary force. Traction and a direct return to neutral are not the next rubric step; the segment moves through neutral into the restricted range.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this passage through neutral into the restricted range is the direct phase of the hybrid technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The rubric does not hold the position of ease and release; t","B":"Still technique is a continuous force through the barrier, n","D":"Traction and a direct return to neutral are not the next rub"}},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has acute psoas spasm with a pelvic side shift, and the upper lumbar key dysfunction has been treated. According to the course, which approach releases the pelvic fascia?","choices":{"A":"Local heat followed by passive rest","B":"Still technique or myofascial release","C":"Counterstrain of the anterior psoas tender points","D":"Muscle energy for the upper lumbar key segment","E":"A cranial vault hold to balance the dura"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists HVLA or muscle energy for the L1 and L2 key dysfunction, counterstrain for the anterior psoas and iliacus tender points, and Still technique or myofascial release to release the pelvis. Local heat typically worsens acute psoas spasm. A cranial vault hold is not among the course's psoas syndrome techniques. Counterstrain of the anterior psoas tender points treats the psoas itself; the course uses Still technique or myofascial release to release the pelvic fascia. Muscle energy is the course's option for the L1 and L2 key dysfunction, which has already been treated, not for releasing the pelvic fascia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: treating the key upper lumbar segment first prevents lumbosacral and sacroiliac dysfunctions from recurring.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 — osteopathic evaluation of the low back pain patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Counterstrain of the anterior psoas tender points treats the","D":"Muscle energy is the course's option for the L1 and L2 key d"}},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review basic concepts of Still technique treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO compares the seated and supine cervical Still techniques in the lab rubric. According to the rubric, which step appears only in the seated version?","choices":{"A":"Stabilizing the neck below the affected segment with the monitoring hand's other fingers","B":"Placing the monitoring hand's index finger over the spinous process of the affected segment","C":"Positioning the head and spine toward the position of ease with flexion or extension before compression","D":"Rotating the spine through neutral with the monitoring arm while compression is held","E":"Reducing the compression and passively returning the patient to neutral at the end of the technique"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The seated cervical technique includes a step in which the other fingers of the monitoring hand stabilize the neck below the affected segment. The supine rubric has no such step, and its other technique steps match the seated version. Positioning toward the position of ease before compression appears in both the seated and supine versions. Placing the index finger over the spinous process is not the seated-only step; the monitoring contact is on the transverse processes in both versions. Rotating through neutral while holding compression appears in both versions, not only the seated one. Reducing compression and returning to neutral occurs in both versions, so it is not seated-only.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the supine cervical rubric has no step for stabilizing the neck below the affected segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — Still technique in low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Placing the index finger over the spinous process is not the","D":"Rotating through neutral while holding compression appears i","E":"Reducing compression and returning to neutral occurs in both"}},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO is performing FPR for an articular dysfunction of the thoracic spine and has just flattened the thoracic kyphosis. According to the lab rubric, what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Exaggerate the dysfunction toward the barrier","B":"Return the patient to neutral","C":"Reassess the somatic dysfunction","D":"Diagnose the somatic dysfunction along the thoracic spine","E":"Disengage the spine with compression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After flattening the kyphosis, the rubric has the DO disengage the spine with compression. The exaggeration comes after compression and is toward the named dysfunction or position of ease, not the barrier; the return to neutral and reassessment come last. Diagnosing the somatic dysfunction is the first step, before flattening the kyphosis, not the step that follows it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: FPR is performed in an ordered sequence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Diagnosing the somatic dysfunction is the first step, before"}},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A student compares FPR with Still technique in the lab rubrics. According to the rubrics, how does the movement that follows compression differ?","choices":{"A":"FPR exaggerates the position of ease, whereas Still passes through neutral into the restricted range","B":"FPR passes through neutral into the restricted range, whereas Still exaggerates the position of ease","C":"FPR thrusts through the restrictive barrier, whereas Still holds the spine at neutral","D":"FPR holds the restrictive barrier, whereas Still stops at the position of ease with a long lever","E":"FPR stays in neutral throughout, whereas Still holds the spine at the position of ease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In FPR the DO exaggerates the position of ease with a long lever. In Still technique the DO moves through neutral into the previously restricted range, and only afterward reduces compression and returns to neutral. This reverses the techniques; FPR exaggerates the position of ease, and Still passes through neutral into the restricted range. FPR exaggerates the position of ease with a long lever, whereas Still moves through neutral into the previously restricted range (rubrics); neither is described as a thrust. FPR does not stay in neutral: it exaggerates the position of ease with a long lever, whereas Still moves through neutral into the restricted range (rubrics).","boardPrep":"COMLEX: only Still moves the segment through neutral into the previously restricted range.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"This reverses the techniques; FPR exaggerates the position o","C":"FPR exaggerates the position of ease with a long lever, wher","E":"FPR does not stay in neutral: it exaggerates the position of"}},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Review basic concepts of FPR technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO is reviewing the lab rubrics for FPR and Still technique. According to the rubrics, which element do both techniques include?","choices":{"A":"A high-velocity, low-amplitude thrust at the barrier","B":"A patient isometric contraction against counterforce","C":"Compression to the dysfunctional segment","D":"Flattening of the thoracic kyphosis at the start","E":"Sustained traction applied to the head throughout the technique"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both rubrics use compression to disengage the spine. FPR also flattens the lordosis or kyphosis, and neither technique uses a thrust or an isometric contraction. Neither rubric uses sustained traction to the head throughout; both use compression to disengage the spine.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: both techniques apply compression to the dysfunctional segment, and FPR describes it as disengagement of the spine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — gentle indirect techniques for the frail patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"FPR also flattens the lordosis","E":"Neither rubric uses sustained traction to the head throughou"}},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis has almost no intersegmental motion in the cervical segments to be treated. According to the lab rubric, how is Still technique classified for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Relatively contraindicated","B":"Indicated with a longer lever","C":"Indicated with compression omitted","D":"Absolutely contraindicated","E":"Safe when performed in the supine position"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Severe loss of intersegmental motion secondary to spondylosis, osteoarthritis, or rheumatoid arthritis in the area to be treated is on the rubric's absolute contraindication list. A longer lever, omitting compression, or supine positioning does not make it acceptable, because an absolute contraindication rules the technique out in that area. Severe loss of intersegmental motion from rheumatoid arthritis is an absolute contraindication on the rubric, not a relative one.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: severe loss of intersegmental motion from spondylosis, osteoarthritis, or rheumatoid arthritis is an absolute contraindication, not a relative one.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Severe loss of intersegmental motion from rheumatoid arthrit","B":"A longer lever, omitting compr","C":"A longer lever, omitting compr","E":"A longer lever, omitting compr"}},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has marked ligamentous laxity with severe instability in the lumbar region a DO plans to treat with Still technique. According to the lab rubric, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Do not perform Still technique at that segment","B":"Use Still technique with caution as a relative case","C":"Avoid Still technique if mild to moderate instability exists","D":"Modify Still technique by omitting the compression step","E":"Treat with Still technique after using a longer lever"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe joint instability in the area to be treated is an absolute contraindication on the rubric. Mild to moderate joint instability is only a relative contraindication. Omitting compression or adding a longer lever does not change an absolute contraindication. Severe instability is an absolute contraindication, so Still technique cannot be used with caution as a relative case.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the severity of joint instability decides whether the contraindication is absolute or relative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Severe instability is an absolute contraindication, so Still"}},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — List indications and contraindications for Still technique.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with no neurologic deficit has a herniated cervical disc, and the DO recognizes that Still positioning could exacerbate it. According to the lab rubric, how is this classified?","choices":{"A":"An absolute contraindication to Still technique","B":"A relative contraindication needing positional caution","C":"An indication for the seated Still technique approach","D":"A contraindication limited to the supine position","E":"An incidental finding irrelevant to positioning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A herniated disc where the positioning could exacerbate the condition is one of the six relative contraindications to Still technique. A herniated disc where positioning could exacerbate it is a relative, not absolute, contraindication. A herniated cervical disc is a contraindication, not an indication for the seated technique. A herniated disc is one of the six relative contraindications, so it is not irrelevant to positioning.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a herniated disc is a relative, not absolute, contraindication to Still technique when the positioning could exacerbate it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — contraindications in the geriatric patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A herniated disc where positioning could exacerbate it is a ","C":"A herniated cervical disc is a contraindication, not an indi","E":"A herniated disc is one of the six relative contraindication"}},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"A DO treats a right-sided lumbar somatic dysfunction with seated Still technique. According to the lab rubric, where do the DO's monitoring finger and the hand of the other arm, which crosses over the opposite shoulder, end up?","choices":{"A":"Finger on the left prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's left shoulder","B":"Finger on the right prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's left shoulder","C":"Finger on the right spinous process, other hand on the patient's right iliac crest","D":"Finger on the left prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's right shoulder","E":"Finger on the right prominent transverse process, other hand on the patient's right shoulder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The monitoring finger, on the same side as the somatic dysfunction, contacts the prominent transverse process. The DO's other arm crosses over the opposite shoulder around the superior chest wall, and its hand rests on the patient's shoulder on the side of the dysfunction. The finger goes on the right transverse process, on the same side as the dysfunction, and the hand on the right shoulder, not the left. The finger goes on the same side as the dysfunction, the right side, not the left, and the hand rests on the shoulder of the dysfunction side.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the same positioning is used for the thoracic Still technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The finger goes on the right transverse process, on the same","B":"The DO's other arm crosses ove","C":"The DO's other arm crosses ove","D":"The finger goes on the same side as the dysfunction, the rig"}},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old frail woman has an articular dysfunction of the cervical spine, and the DO is performing FPR. According to the lab rubric, when does the DO return the patient to neutral?","choices":{"A":"After the patient reports relief of the tenderness","B":"After 30 to 60 seconds of sustained compression","C":"Immediately without exaggerating the position of ease","D":"After 3 to 5 seconds in the exaggerated position","E":"After the segment passes through the restrictive barrier"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric has the DO demonstrate and verbalize the return to neutral after 3 to 5 seconds, followed by reassessment of the somatic dysfunction. The rubric exaggerates the named dysfunction or position of ease before the return to neutral, so an immediate return skips that step. The return to neutral is timed at 3 to 5 seconds in the exaggerated position, not based on relief of tenderness. Thirty to sixty seconds is far longer than the rubric's 3 to 5 seconds. FPR does not pass through the restrictive barrier; it exaggerates the position of ease, then returns to neutral.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: know the hold time before the return to neutral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 -> Obj 2 — OMT techniques for low back pain","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The return to neutral is timed at 3 to 5 seconds in the exag","B":"Thirty to sixty seconds is far longer than the rubric's 3 to","E":"FPR does not pass through the restrictive barrier; it exagge"}},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Demonstrate application of Still techniques of the axial skeleton and FPR techniques in a geriatric patient.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man receives seated thoracic Still technique for an articular dysfunction at T6. Afterward the segment moves more freely, but his rotation is still well short of what a young adult would have. According to the course, what should the DO do?","choices":{"A":"Repeat the technique with firmer compression until full motion returns","B":"Accept the gain, since restoring youthful range is not a goal","C":"Add a high-velocity thrust to finish restoring the range","D":"Hold the restricted position for 90 seconds to stretch the joint","E":"Switch to forceful end-range articulation to gain more motion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course tells the DO not to demand full range of motion in older patients: they naturally have less articular motion, and restoring a complete youthful range is not a clinical goal. Repeating with firmer compression, adding a thrust, or forcing end-range articulation all raise the dose, while the course advises a smaller stimulus advanced slowly with gentle, controlled force in frail older adults. A 90-second hold belongs to counterstrain, not to Still technique.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: in older adults, start low and go slow, and accept the age-appropriate range of motion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — treatment plan for the frail patient","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"A 90-second hold belongs to co"}}],"10":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how is visceral mobility defined?","choices":{"A":"Inherent, active organ motion arising without external forces","B":"Slow rhythmic motion around the embryologic axes of origin","C":"Fluid exchange across the sheaths of the visceral fascia","D":"Reflex motor output to the related paraspinal muscles","E":"Passive organ movement in response to external mechanical forces"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course defines visceral mobility as the passive movement of viscera in response to external mechanical forces such as gravity, digestion, physical activity, and diaphragmatic expansion. Motility is the inherent, active, independent motion of each organ. Reflex motor output to paraspinal muscles is a viscerosomatic reflex, not a kind of organ movement. Slow rhythmic motion around the embryologic axes describes motility, not visceral mobility. Fluid exchange through the fascial sheaths is how visceral attachments affect circulation; mobility is passive organ movement in response to external mechanical forces.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mobility is passive and tested with respiration. Motility is intrinsic and assessed along the organ's longitudinal axis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — autonomic reflexes and the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Slow rhythmic motion around the embryologic axes describes m","C":"Fluid exchange through the fascial sheaths is how visceral a"}},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which two phases make up visceral motility?","choices":{"A":"Inhalation and exhalation","B":"Flexion and extension","C":"Expir and inspir","D":"Systole and diastole","E":"Abduction and adduction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course describes motility as a slow rhythmic motion with two phases. Expir moves organs toward the midline, and inspir moves them away from the midline in a rotation around their embryologic axes. Inhalation and exhalation drive mobility, and motility runs at a different frequency than breathing; flexion-extension, systole-diastole, and abduction-adduction are not its phases.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: motility runs at a different frequency than breathing and is described as associated with the craniosacral rhythm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — autonomic reflexes and the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, in how many dimensions must the thoracic and abdominal viscera move during respiration?","choices":{"A":"Three dimensions, inferior-superior, lateral, and anterior-posterior","B":"Two dimensions, inferior-superior and lateral, without anterior-posterior","C":"Three dimensions, medial-lateral, rotational, and torsional","D":"One dimension, inferior-superior, without lateral or anterior-posterior","E":"Two dimensions, lateral and rotational, around the embryologic axis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that during quiet and forceful respiration the viscera must move in three dimensions, inferior-superior, lateral, and anterior-posterior, as the diaphragm, pelvic floor, and viscera descend with inhalation. Visceral motion occurs in three dimensions, including anterior-posterior, not just two. The three dimensions are inferior-superior, lateral, and anterior-posterior, not rotational or torsional. Respiratory visceral motion is three-dimensional, not only inferior-superior. Respiratory visceral motion is three-dimensional, and the dimensions are not lateral and rotational.","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral mobility follows the diaphragm during inhalation. COMLEX: loss of this three-dimensional glide marks a mobility restriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — autonomic reflexes and the musculoskeletal relationship to systemic dysfunction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Visceral motion occurs in three dimensions, including anteri","C":"The three dimensions are inferior-superior, lateral, and ant","D":"Respiratory visceral motion is three-dimensional, not only i","E":"Respiratory visceral motion is three-dimensional, and the di"}},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which situation is an absolute contraindication to a splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"Chronic constipation from slow transit","B":"Adhesions after pelvic or colon surgery","C":"Functional nausea with early satiety","D":"Stress urinary incontinence in women","E":"Splenomegaly from acute mononucleosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that a splenic pump is absolutely contraindicated in an enlarged spleen, such as acute mononucleosis or hematologic malignancy, because of the severe risk of splenic rupture. Constipation, postsurgical adhesions, nausea, and stress urinary incontinence are indications for visceral manipulation in the course, not contraindications.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: infectious mononucleosis is a listed contraindication in both the lecture and the Week 4 lab rubric.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Constipation, postsurgical adh","B":"Constipation, postsurgical adh","C":"Constipation, postsurgical adh"}},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which findings suggest an aortic aneurysm that needs medical evaluation?","choices":{"A":"Borborygmi with a lateral pulsation narrower than 1 inch","B":"Rebound tenderness with absent bowel sounds","C":"Saddle anesthesia with new urinary retention","D":"A bruit with a lateral pulsation wider than 1 inch","E":"Fever with localized spinal bony tenderness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course describes an abdominal aortic aneurysm as severe tearing mid-back or abdominal pain, an abdominal bruit, or a lateral aortic pulsation wider than 1 inch. The other findings point to surgical abdomen, cauda equina syndrome, and spinal infection. The course describes an abdominal bruit with a lateral aortic pulsation wider than 1 inch as the aneurysm finding, not borborygmi with a narrower pulsation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: know the bruit and the 1-inch limit for a normal lateral aortic pulsation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course describes an abdominal bruit with a lateral aorti","B":"The other findings point to su","C":"The other findings point to su","E":"The other findings point to su"}},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which technique is contraindicated in a patient with acute hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal ganglion inhibition at the celiac level","B":"Mesenteric lift toward the midline","C":"Liver pump or forceful rib cage compression","D":"Colonic stimulation along the descending colon","E":"General listening with a hand on the head"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that forceful compression of the rib cage or a liver pump is contraindicated in active hepatitis to prevent parenchymal liver damage. Ganglion inhibition, the mesenteric lift, colonic stimulation, and general listening do not compress the liver and are not part of the course's hepatitis contraindication.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: match each contraindication to the structure that is at risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Reduce hypersympathetic outflow to the gut","B":"Raise sphincter tone to slow intestinal transit","C":"Clear portal venous congestion through rib compression","D":"Increase vagal output to the stomach","E":"Relieve traction on the stretch-sensitive mesenteric roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Abdominal ganglion inhibition reduces hypersympathetic outflow to the gastrointestinal and pelvic viscera. Hypersympathetic tone raises sphincter tone, so inhibition lowers it; portal decongestion by rib compression is the liver pump, relieving mesenteric root traction is the mesenteric lift, and increasing vagal output is not a goal the course gives.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ganglion inhibition targets the celiac and mesenteric ganglia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the main physiologic goal of the splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"Reduce hypersympathetic outflow to the gastrointestinal viscera","B":"Free the lymphatic channels running through the mesentery","C":"Decongest the portal venous system of metabolic waste","D":"Mobilize stored red and white blood cells into circulation","E":"Raise vagal tone to speed gastric emptying"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The spleen stores red and white blood cells. Compression changes applied to it mobilize those cells into active circulation, which helps patients who need heightened immune defense or have some anemias. Reducing sympathetic outflow is the goal of ganglion inhibition, freeing mesenteric lymphatics the mesenteric lift's, and portal decongestion the liver pump's; raising vagal tone is not a pump goal in the course.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the splenic pump is used for immune support.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Reducing sympathetic outflow i"}},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course's visceral techniques matrix, what is the goal of colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Mobilize stored blood cells to fight infection","B":"Restore sliding and distensibility of bowel segments","C":"Reduce sympathetic outflow at the celiac ganglion","D":"Promote lymph flow through the hepatic channels","E":"Free the lymphatic channels from mesenteric stretch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The matrix lists colonic stimulation as resolving mechanical restrictions of colonic segments to restore sliding and gliding, and the technique description adds distensibility. Mobilizing blood cells is the splenic pump, reducing celiac outflow is ganglion inhibition, hepatic lymph flow is the liver pump, and freeing mesenteric lymphatics is the mesenteric lift.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic stimulation mobilizes segments of the large intestine in several directions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — therapeutic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, continuous afferent bombardment from a dysfunctional organ leaves spinal interneurons in what state?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition, a chronic hypoexcitable state","B":"Reciprocal inhibition, a transient relaxed state","C":"Autogenic inhibition, a stretch-induced silent state","D":"Facilitation, a chronic hyperexcitable state","E":"Spinal shock, a flaccid areflexic state"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistent visceral afferent bombardment keeps spinal interneurons at that segment chronically hyperexcitable, called segmental facilitation or central sensitization. The threshold for motor and sympathetic output is lowered. Reciprocal and autogenic inhibition are relaxing reflexes, the opposite of the lowered firing threshold the course describes. Persistent afferent bombardment leaves spinal interneurons hyperexcitable, not hypoexcitable. Persistent visceral afferent bombardment leaves spinal interneurons chronically hyperexcitable (segmental facilitation); spinal shock is not the state the course describes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: facilitation lowers firing threshold at the dorsal horn. COMLEX: facilitated segments show tissue texture change and restricted motion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Persistent afferent bombardment leaves spinal interneurons h","E":"Persistent visceral afferent bombardment leaves spinal inter"}},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what end-feel characterizes somatic dysfunction of visceral origin?","choices":{"A":"A hard bony feel, at an absolute articular barrier","B":"Reluctance to motion, a soft rubbery feel","C":"An empty feel, with severe guarding","D":"A springy feel, that is symmetric","E":"Free motion, with a sharp capsular stop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that somatic dysfunction of visceral origin shows a characteristic reluctance to motion, a soft, rubbery, or hesitant end-feel, rather than an absolute hard articular barrier. Dysfunction of visceral origin shows a soft, rubbery reluctance to motion rather than an absolute hard articular barrier. The course describes a reluctance to motion with a soft, rubbery, hesitant end-feel for dysfunction of visceral origin, not an empty feel with severe guarding. The course describes a reluctance to motion with a soft, rubbery, hesitant end-feel for dysfunction of visceral origin, not a springy symmetric feel. The course describes a reluctance to motion with a soft, rubbery, hesitant end-feel for dysfunction of visceral origin, not free motion with a sharp capsular stop.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: reluctance to motion suggests a viscerosomatic reflex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Dysfunction of visceral origin shows a soft, rubbery relucta","C":"The course describes a reluctance to motion with a soft, rubbery, hesitant end-feel for dysfunction of visceral origin, not an empty feel wi","D":"The course describes a reluctance to motion with a soft, rubbery, hesitant end-feel for dysfunction of visceral origin, not a springy symmet","E":"The course describes a reluctance to motion with a soft, rubbery, hesitant end-feel for dysfunction of visceral origin, not free motion with"}},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where are Chapman's points typically found in patients with gastrointestinal or pelvic disease?","choices":{"A":"Around the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus","B":"Over the occiput, posterior neck, and shoulder","C":"Along the medial forearm, wrist, and thenar eminence","D":"Over the sternum, clavicles, and upper ribs","E":"Around the knee, ankle, and plantar foot"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes Chapman's points as localized, gangliform, edematous fascial congestion typically found around the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus in patients with gastrointestinal or pelvic disease. The course's statement for GI and pelvic disease names the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus, not the occiput and neck, the forearm and thenar eminence, or the sternum and upper ribs. The course places Chapman's points around the pelvis, lateral thigh, and umbilicus for gastrointestinal or pelvic disease, not around the knee, ankle, and plantar foot.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: Chapman's points are reflex zones of fascial congestion that reflect visceral stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — segmentalization, viscerosomatic reflexes, and facilitation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course's statement for GI ","C":"The course's statement for GI ","D":"The course's statement for GI ","E":"The course places Chapman's points around the pelvis, latera"}},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, before any visceral OMT is applied, what must the physician do?","choices":{"A":"A direct mesenteric lift to find the restriction","B":"A splenic pump to mobilize immune cells","C":"Colonic stimulation along the bowel","D":"Home stretching and hydration instructions","E":"A safety check for red flags and surgical abdomen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that the physician must first rule out red-flag conditions or a surgical abdomen before any visceral OMT. The mesenteric lift, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation are treatments that follow the safety check and the neurovascular and spinal releases, and self-care advice comes last.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: screen for acute abdomen, aneurysm, and other red flags before treating.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 1 — general visceral screening examination","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The mesenteric lift, splenic p"}},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, which neurovascular roots are palpated and released before direct treatment of the organs they supply?","choices":{"A":"Femoral and popliteal arteries with the inguinal nodes","B":"Celiac and mesenteric ganglia and arteries","C":"Carotid and subclavian arteries with the cervical nodes","D":"Inferior vena cava and azygos veins with thoracic nodes","E":"Renal and gonadal arteries with the lumbar plexus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that treatment begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes. Releasing these neurovascular roots must precede direct treatment of the organs. The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the femoral and popliteal arteries. The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the carotid and subclavian arteries. The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the vena cava and azygos veins. The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the renal and gonadal arteries.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the celiac and mesenteric roots are the neurovascular roots of the abdominal organs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 1 — general visceral screening examination","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the femoral and pop","C":"The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the carotid and sub","D":"The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the vena cava and a","E":"The course begins with the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, ganglia, and lymph nodes, not the renal and gonad"}},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, when are related lower thoracic and lumbar spinal segments and the rib cage treated?","choices":{"A":"Before the safety check for red flags","B":"After direct organ treatment is complete","C":"Before the direct treatment of the organs","D":"In place of the arterial and ganglia releases","E":"Before the neurovascular root releases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course has the physician first treat the associated lower thoracic and lumbar segments and rib cage to reduce sympathetic outflow to the abdomen, then perform direct visceral work such as mesenteric lifts or colonic stimulation. The safety check comes before everything, the spinal work adds to rather than replaces the arterial and ganglia releases, and those neurovascular releases come before it. The related spinal segments and ribs are treated before direct organ work, not after it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: normalize sympathetic outflow through the spine and ribs before direct organ work.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 1 — general visceral screening examination","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The related spinal segments and ribs are treated before dire"}},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has persistent abdominal congestion after surgery, with palpable fascial tethering. According to the course, how does visceral manipulation improve her circulation?","choices":{"A":"It raises sympathetic outflow to increase peristaltic pressure","B":"It lengthens the lymphatic contraction cycle to one per minute","C":"It frees the attachments that carry vessels and lymphatics","D":"It fuses adhesions so the organ moves as one unit","E":"It increases afferent bombardment to reset facilitation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arteries, veins, and lymphatics supplying the organs travel through the visceral attachments and fascial sheaths. Visceral manipulation removes the mechanical constriction there, improving arterial perfusion and venous and lymphatic drainage. Raising sympathetic outflow restricts peristalsis, adhesions are what the technique breaks down rather than fuses, and visceral manipulation dampens afferent bombardment rather than increasing it. Lymphatic channels contract 5 to 8 times per minute at rest; the course says visceral manipulation frees the attachments carrying vessels, not that it slows this cycle.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: fascial tethering after surgery impairs fluid exchange. Releasing the attachments restores it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — mesenteric lift to free the lymphatic channels","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Lymphatic channels contract 5 to 8 times per minute at rest;"}},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":2,"stem":"A student is explaining how lymph moves toward the heart in a patient with abdominal congestion. According to the course, at what rate do lymphatic channels contract at rest?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 times per minute","B":"12 to 16 times per minute","C":"20 to 30 times per minute","D":"60 to 80 times per minute","E":"5 to 8 times per minute"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that lymphatic channels contract rhythmically at 5 to 8 times per minute at rest to move fluid toward the heart. Visceral techniques alter abdominal pressure gradients to move congested lymph. One to two contractions per minute is slower than the course's 5 to 8 per minute. Twelve to sixteen per minute is faster than the course's 5 to 8 per minute. Twenty to thirty per minute is much faster than the course's 5 to 8 per minute. Sixty to eighty times per minute is not the course's resting lymphatic contraction rate, which is 5 to 8 per minute.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymph flow depends on intrinsic vessel contraction and pressure gradients. COMLEX: pumps and lifts change those gradients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — liver and splenic pump goals","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"One to two contractions per minute is slower than the course","B":"Twelve to sixteen per minute is faster than the course's 5 t","C":"Twenty to thirty per minute is much faster than the course's","D":"Sixty to eighty times per minute is not the course's resting"}},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the osteopathic principles of visceral manipulation, including its effects on mobility, circulation, lymphatic flow, and autonomic function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has chronic visceral irritation with sustained sympathetic overactivity. According to the course, what does visceral manipulation do for his autonomic balance?","choices":{"A":"It amplifies visceral afferent input to reset sympathetic tone","B":"It dampens nociceptive input and lowers cortisol output","C":"It raises circulating cortisol to restore autonomic balance","D":"It increases HPA axis activation to speed healing","E":"It removes the chemosensitivity of visceral afferent receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that chronic visceral irritation overstimulates afferent pathways and the sympathetic nervous system. Visceral manipulation dampens the nociceptive barrage, down-regulates HPA axis activation, lowers cortisol, and restores balanced sympathetic and parasympathetic tone. Visceral manipulation dampens, not amplifies, visceral afferent input. The course says visceral manipulation lowers cortisol, not raises it. Visceral manipulation down-regulates HPA axis activation rather than increasing it. The course says visceral manipulation dampens the nociceptive barrage from chemo-, mechano-, and thermosensitive afferents, not that it removes their chemosensitivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic stress raises cortisol and allostatic load. COMLEX: reducing nociceptive input lowers sympathetic drive.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — HPA axis and cortisol","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Visceral manipulation dampens, not amplifies, visceral affer","C":"The course says visceral manipulation lowers cortisol, not r","D":"Visceral manipulation down-regulates HPA axis activation rat","E":"The course says visceral manipulation dampens the nociceptiv"}},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man who smokes asks for visceral OMT for chronic low back and buttock pain. The pain comes on with walking and eases when he stops, whatever his posture. He reports erectile dysfunction, and his femoral and popliteal pulses are weak. According to the course, which condition must be addressed first?","choices":{"A":"Leriche syndrome needing vascular evaluation","B":"Neurogenic claudication from lumbar stenosis","C":"Psoas syndrome from iliopsoas spasm","D":"Piriformis spasm referring pain down the thigh","E":"Iliolumbar ligament syndrome from a leg length difference"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists Leriche syndrome among the conditions that need medical evaluation rather than OMT: severe atherosclerosis of the distal aorta and iliac arteries causes persistent low back or buttock pain, leg claudication, impotence, and weak femoral and popliteal pulses. Neurogenic claudication from stenosis improves with sitting or forward flexion rather than simply with stopping, and does not reduce the pulses. Psoas syndrome produces a forward-flexed posture with a lean and a pelvic side shift. Piriformis spasm refers pain down the posterior thigh without vascular signs. Iliolumbar ligament syndrome causes tenderness at the iliac crest above the PSIS with groin referral.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: vascular masqueraders of back pain, such as Leriche and May-Thurner syndromes, are referred for evaluation before any OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — recognizing contraindications in the lab","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has severe mid-back and abdominal pain, a midline abdominal bruit, and a widened lateral aortic pulsation. According to the course, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition to relieve sympathetic spasm","B":"Perform a mesenteric lift to decongest the intestines","C":"Withhold abdominal OMT and arrange emergent evaluation","D":"Perform colonic stimulation to restore bowel function","E":"Observe at home and recheck in one week"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that abdominal visceral OMT is absolutely contraindicated when an abdominal aortic aneurysm is suspected, which is a life-threatening emergency suspected from a bruit or a widened lateral pulsation of the aorta. The mesenteric lift and colonic stimulation are abdominal visceral OMT, which is absolutely contraindicated here, and a suspected aneurysm is a life-threatening emergency, not something to recheck in a week. Ganglion inhibition applies deep sustained pressure just anterior to the aorta, and the course strictly contraindicates direct manipulation over an aortic aneurysm, which needs emergency referral.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: suspect AAA with severe tearing back or abdominal pain, a bruit, or a pulsation wider than 1 inch.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — contraindications requiring emergent evaluation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Ganglion inhibition applies deep sustained pressure just ant"}},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation and recognize situations requiring medical evaluation rather than OMT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has distension, vomiting, and high-pitched tinkling sounds on auscultation. According to the course, which interpretation and decision fit?","choices":{"A":"Paralytic ileus that resolves with gentle abdominal OMT","B":"Functional constipation that is suitable for a mesenteric lift","C":"Parasympathetic hypoactivity that is suitable for ganglion inhibition","D":"Mechanical obstruction needing urgent surgical evaluation","E":"Normal peristalsis that is suitable for a liver pump"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that high-pitched tinkling bowel sounds indicate an active obstruction, while a complete absence of bowel sounds indicates a paralytic ileus. Both contraindicate direct abdominal manipulation and require immediate medical or surgical evaluation. Tinkling sounds with distension and vomiting are not functional constipation, parasympathetic hypoactivity, or normal peristalsis, and direct abdominal manipulation is contraindicated.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: tinkling sounds mean obstruction, and absent sounds mean ileus. Neither is treated with direct abdominal OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — obstruction and ileus as contraindications","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has chronic constipation. According to the course, restricted mobility of which colonic segment is classically associated with it?","choices":{"A":"The cecum and ileocecal valve","B":"Sigmoid colon or rectum","C":"The hepatic flexure","D":"The ascending colon","E":"The transverse colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that osteopathic literature links a lack of sigmoid colon or rectal mobility to constipation, whereas a lack of cecal mobility is linked to diarrhea. The course ties no bowel habit to the hepatic flexure. Lack of cecal mobility is linked to diarrhea, not constipation. The course links sigmoid colon or rectal immobility to constipation, not the ascending colon. The course does not tie transverse colon mobility to constipation; it names the sigmoid colon or rectum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic stimulation targets the restricted segment. Sigmoid or rectal work addresses constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — colonic stimulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Lack of cecal mobility is linked to diarrhea, not constipati","D":"The course links sigmoid colon or rectal immobility to const","E":"The course does not tie transverse colon mobility to constip"}},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has bloating and a heavy, congested feeling in the lower abdomen. According to the course, which technique and goal fit best?","choices":{"A":"Splenic pump to mobilize stored red cells into circulation","B":"Liver pump to clear portal venous congestion","C":"Celiac ganglion inhibition to mobilize stored white cells","D":"Colonic stimulation to compress the stretch-sensitive mesentery","E":"Mesenteric lift to decongest bowel and free lymphatics"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The mesenteric lift decongests the intestines, improves arterial flow, and frees the lymphatic channels running through the stretch-sensitive mesentery. It relieves mechanical stress on the mesenteric roots. The splenic pump mobilizes stored cells and the liver pump clears portal congestion, neither aimed at the bowel, and celiac ganglion inhibition reduces sympathetic outflow rather than mobilizing white cells. Colonic stimulation restores sliding and gliding of colonic segments; decongesting the bowel and freeing mesenteric lymphatics is the goal of the mesenteric lift.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the mesenteric lift treats mesenteric tension and congestion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Colonic stimulation restores sliding and gliding of colonic "}},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the physiologic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man recovering from abdominal surgery has sluggish bowel function and spastic sphincters. According to the course, which technique and goal fit best?","choices":{"A":"Ganglion inhibition to lower sympathetic tone and restore transit","B":"Splenic pump to enhance immune surveillance and lymph return","C":"Liver pump to drive fluid through the hepatic lymphatic channels","D":"Mesenteric lift to mobilize stored blood cells into circulation","E":"Colonic stimulation to mobilize stored leukocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypersympathetic stimulation restricts peristalsis and increases sphincter tone. Abdominal ganglion inhibition reduces that outflow, relaxes smooth muscle, decreases sphincter spasm, and restores peristalsis. The splenic pump targets immunity and stored cells, the liver pump hepatic lymph, and the mesenteric lift bowel congestion; none targets the sympathetic tone behind sphincter spasm. Colonic stimulation restores sliding and gliding of colonic segments; the course ties mobilizing stored white cells to the splenic pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic outflow slows gut motility. COMLEX: inhibit the celiac and mesenteric ganglia to restore motility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Colonic stimulation restores sliding and gliding of colonic "}},{"id":"sdl10_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man passing a kidney stone has widespread back muscle tightness. According to the course, which somatic finding fits?","choices":{"A":"A single-segment Type II dysfunction at C2","B":"An isolated rib head dysfunction at T1","C":"Flaccid paraspinal muscles with a diffuse soft tissue deficit","D":"Rigid lumbar group curves with paraspinal spasm","E":"A hard-end-feel anterior innominate rotation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course gives the example that the passage of a kidney stone down the ureter can create diffuse paraspinal spasm and rigid lumbar group curves, a facilitated-segment presentation of visceral origin. An isolated T1 rib head or a hard-end-feel innominate rotation does not fit: dysfunctions of visceral origin show a soft, rubbery reluctance to motion, and the course's kidney stone example is lumbar. A kidney stone creates diffuse paraspinal spasm with rigid lumbar group curves (course), not an isolated single-segment dysfunction at C2. A kidney stone creates paraspinal spasm with rigid lumbar group curves (course), not flaccid paraspinal muscles.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: visceral pathology produces segmentally related paraspinal hypertonicity and persistent group curves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — viscerosomatic reflex arcs","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A kidney stone creates diffuse paraspinal spasm with rigid l","C":"A kidney stone creates paraspinal spasm with rigid lumbar gr"}},{"id":"sdl10_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with a Crohn flare has a flexed, rotated lumbar spine and severe low back pain from a tight muscle. According to the course, what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic floor weakness compresses the L1 nerve root against the psoas","B":"Bowel inflammation irritates psoas fascia and drives chronic contraction","C":"Diaphragmatic paralysis tilts the lumbar spine into flexion","D":"Colonic invasion of the vertebral body rotates the lumbar spine","E":"Loss of hip abductor tone rotates the sacrum and strains the psoas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course describes reflex psoas major spasm, in which local inflammation of the colon, appendix, or pelvic organs irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces the muscle into chronic spasm, causing a flexed, rotated lumbar spine with severe low back pain. The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not pelvic floor weakness compressing a nerve root. The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not diaphragmatic paralysis. The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not invasion of the vertebral body. The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not hip abductor weakness.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: inflammatory bowel disease, appendicitis, and pelvic inflammation can present as psoas spasm and low back pain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — viscerosomatic relationships","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not pelvic f","C":"The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not diaphrag","D":"The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not invasion","E":"The course describes reflex psoas spasm: bowel inflammation irritates the adjacent psoas fascia and forces chronic contraction, not hip abdu"}},{"id":"sdl10_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the relationship between visceral dysfunction, viscerosomatic reflexes, and associated somatic dysfunction.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has active gastric inflammation with a viscerosomatic reflex. According to the course, which temperature finding is expected on exam?","choices":{"A":"Localized cooling over the related paraspinal tissues","B":"No temperature change at the involved segments","C":"Localized heat over the related paraspinal tissues","D":"Diffuse cooling across the entire back","E":"Heat confined to the distal extremities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that active viscerosomatic reflexes are often accompanied by localized cutaneous heat over the segmentally related paraspinal tissues, palpable on the physical exam. The heat is localized over the segmentally related paraspinal tissues, not diffuse cooling across the back. Active viscerosomatic reflexes produce localized heat, not cooling. Active viscerosomatic reflexes are often accompanied by localized heat over the related paraspinal tissues, so a temperature change is expected. The heat is over the segmentally related paraspinal tissues, not confined to the distal extremities.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: acute viscerosomatic reflexes show warmth, tissue texture change, and reluctance to motion in the related segments.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — somatic findings of visceral reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Active viscerosomatic reflexes produce localized heat, not c","B":"Active viscerosomatic reflexes are often accompanied by loca","D":"The heat is localized over the","E":"The heat is over the segmentally related paraspinal tissues,"}},{"id":"sdl10_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has severe visceral irritation, and her paraspinal dysfunction is maintained by a highly active viscerosomatic reflex. According to the course, which approach fits?","choices":{"A":"Deep direct mesenteric traction to break the reflex","B":"Gentle direct stretch over the inflamed organ","C":"High-velocity thrust at the facilitated segment","D":"Direct fingertip pressure over the inflamed organ","E":"Gentle indirect somatic OMT such as counterstrain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course advises avoiding direct, aggressive manipulation over the organ in severe visceral irritation. The physician should prioritize gentle indirect somatic OMT, such as strain-counterstrain, cranial, or balanced ligamentous tension, directed at segmentally related somatic structures with minimal stimulation. Deep direct mesenteric traction is aggressive direct manipulation near the organ, which the course advises avoiding in severe irritation. In severe visceral irritation the course directs gentle indirect OMT at segmentally related somatic structures, not treatment over the inflamed organ itself. High-velocity thrust at the facilitated segment is aggressive, not the gentle indirect approach the course prioritizes. Fingertips are contraindicated on abdominal organs, and the course avoids direct manipulation over the organ in severe visceral irritation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: dose down when the reflex is highly active, and use the soft palmar surfaces rather than fingertips on abdominal organs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — treating the low-reserve patient with minimal stimulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Deep direct mesenteric traction is aggressive direct manipul","B":"In severe visceral irritation the course directs gentle indi","C":"High-velocity thrust at the facilitated segment is aggressiv","D":"Fingertips are contraindicated on abdominal organs, and the "}},{"id":"sdl10_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with sluggish bowel function and a core rehabilitation need finishes his visceral treatment. According to the course, which self-care advice fits?","choices":{"A":"Bed rest, fluid restriction, and avoidance of core exercise","B":"Heavy lifting, high-intensity crunches, and caffeine loading","C":"Daily deep self-massage, laxative use, and fasting","D":"Customized hydration, diet changes, and abdominal bracing","E":"Prolonged sitting, low fiber intake, and abdominal binding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course pairs visceral OMT with supportive self-care, including customized hydration advice, nutritional modifications, and core rehabilitation exercises such as posterior pelvic tilts, abdominal bracing, or the drawing-in maneuver. Heavy lifting and high-intensity crunches are not the course's core rehabilitation, which uses posterior pelvic tilts, abdominal bracing, and the drawing-in maneuver. The course pairs visceral OMT with hydration advice, nutritional changes, and core rehabilitation exercise, not bed rest, fluid restriction, or avoiding core exercise. The course pairs visceral OMT with hydration advice, nutritional changes, and core rehabilitation exercise, not self-massage, laxatives, and fasting. The course pairs visceral OMT with hydration advice, nutritional changes, and core rehabilitation exercise, not prolonged sitting, low fiber, and abdominal binding.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: self-care supports lumbosacral stability and lower gastrointestinal regulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — dosing and post-treatment care","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course pairs visceral OMT with hydration advice, nutritional changes, and core rehabilitation exercise, not bed rest","C":"The course pairs visceral OMT with hydration advice, nutritional changes, and core rehabilitation exercise, not self-mas","E":"The course pairs visceral OMT with hydration advice, nutritional changes, and core rehabilitation exercise, not prolonge"}},{"id":"sdl10_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe how visceral manipulation is incorporated into a comprehensive osteopathic treatment plan.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has abdominal congestion and restricted breathing. According to the course, why is thoracic diaphragm release included in her plan?","choices":{"A":"Diaphragm motion drives passive visceral mobility and lymph drainage","B":"Diaphragm release replaces the ganglion and arterial releases","C":"Diaphragm release raises sympathetic outflow to the viscera","D":"Diaphragm release fuses fascial adhesions between organs","E":"Diaphragm release increases cortisol output from the adrenal glands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that release of the thoracic diaphragm must be included, because optimizing diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage. Diaphragm release is added to the ganglion and arterial releases, not substituted for them. The course includes diaphragm release because diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage, not to raise sympathetic outflow. The course includes diaphragm release because diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage, not to fuse adhesions. The course includes diaphragm release because diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage, not to increase cortisol output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the diaphragm drives visceral descent with inhalation. COMLEX: include diaphragm release alongside spinal and rib treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — thoracoabdominal diaphragm release","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Diaphragm release is added to the ganglion and arterial rele","C":"The course includes diaphragm release because diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage, not to raise sympatheti","D":"The course includes diaphragm release because diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage, not to fuse adhesions.","E":"The course includes diaphragm release because diaphragmatic excursion is essential for passive visceral mobility and lymphatic drainage, not to increase cortiso"}}],"11":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":1,"stem":"A hand placed on top of a standing patient's head senses a pull toward the front of the body. According to the course, what does this suggest?","choices":{"A":"Spinal, dural, or cranial dysfunction","B":"Extremity or paired organ dysfunction","C":"Muscular, tendinous, or ligamentous strain","D":"Normal global fascial balance with no pull","E":"Visceral, rib, or sternal restriction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In general listening, an anterior pull points to a visceral, anterior rib, or sternal restriction. A posterior pull points to a spinal or dural dysfunction and a lateral pull to the extremities or paired viscera; the course assigns no direction to muscular, tendinous, or ligamentous strain. A pull toward the front is an abnormal finding pointing to anterior visceral, rib, or sternal restriction, not normal balance.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: general listening uses the global fascial continuity model to choose the region to examine next.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — palpatory diagnostics and listening in the visceral treatment plan","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"A pull toward the front is an abnormal finding pointing to a"}},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":1,"stem":"In which position is local listening performed, using layer palpation?","choices":{"A":"Standing, with a hand resting on top of the head","B":"Prone, with lateral pressure over the flanks and back","C":"Supine, with slow compression through the abdomen","D":"Seated, with a hand over the sternum and upper ribs","E":"Side-lying, with a quick thrust over the viscera"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For local listening the patient is supine and the physician slowly compresses through the abdominal wall, evaluating the tissue pull and center of tension at each layer from skin to peritoneum. A hand on top of the standing patient's head is general listening, and the course describes no prone, seated, or side-lying local listening and no thrust.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: general listening is done standing at the head, and local listening is done supine at the abdomen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — palpatory diagnostics and listening in the visceral treatment plan","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"A hand on top of the standing "}},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, how is impaired visceral mobility reflected on examination?","choices":{"A":"Reduced respiratory motion with rubbery resistance","B":"Increased respiratory excursion with a springy end-feel","C":"Absent motility with normal respiratory motion","D":"A hard bony block at the articular surface","E":"A rapid intrinsic rhythm at the craniosacral rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Impaired mobility shows a reduced range of respiratory motion, altered distensibility of attachments, and a characteristic reluctance to motion or rubbery resistance. A hard block is an articular barrier rather than the soft reluctance of visceral restriction, and an intrinsic rhythm belongs to motility, not mobility. Impaired mobility shows a reduced, not increased, respiratory range of motion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mobility is the passive motion tested with respiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5 — palpatory diagnostics and listening in the visceral treatment plan","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Impaired mobility shows a reduced, not increased, respirator"}},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, in a direct visceral treatment the organ or its attachments are guided where?","choices":{"A":"Toward the position of ease","B":"Along the organ's motility rhythm without engaging tension","C":"Into the direction of tension with a sudden thrust","D":"Into the direction of tension","E":"Away from the organ's attachments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In direct visceral treatment the physician engages the restrictive barrier, guiding the dysfunctional organ or its attachments gently but firmly into the direction of tension. Moving toward the position of ease is indirect treatment. Following the motility rhythm without engaging tension is not direct treatment, which engages the restrictive barrier by guiding the tissue into the direction of tension. Direct treatment is gentle but firm, not a sudden thrust. Direct treatment guides tissues into the barrier, not away from the organ's attachments.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: direct treatment works at the restrictive barrier.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Following the motility rhythm without engaging tension is no","C":"Direct treatment is gentle but firm, not a sudden thrust.","E":"Direct treatment guides tissues into the barrier, not away f"}},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, toward what end point does the physician guide the tissues in an indirect visceral treatment?","choices":{"A":"A fascial balance point with minimal tension","B":"Into the restrictive barrier of the dysfunction","C":"To a point of maximal fascial stretch and tension","D":"Beyond the end of the normal physiologic range","E":"To the point of complete adhesion release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Indirect treatment moves the organ or attachments away from the restrictive barrier toward the position of ease, a point of fascial balance or pathologic neutral where tension is minimized. Indirect treatment minimizes tension, so carrying tissues beyond their normal range is the opposite of its aim. Indirect treatment minimizes tension, so carrying tissues to maximal stretch and tension is the opposite of its aim. Breaking down adhesions is the aim of direct treatment; indirect treatment guides tissues to a fascial balance point of minimal tension.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: indirect treatment seeks the balance point, then releases as the tissues relax.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Indirect treatment minimizes tension, so carrying tissues to","E":"Breaking down adhesions is the aim of direct treatment; indi"}},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the aim of a direct visceral treatment?","choices":{"A":"Minimize tension at a pathologic neutral point","B":"Elongate shortened tissue and restore its stretch","C":"Use a respiratory hold to release toward ease","D":"Reduce sympathetic outflow through the ganglia","E":"Avoid loading the dysfunctional attachments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Direct stretch aims to elongate shortened, inelastic connective tissues, break down fascial adhesions, and restore normal physiologic distensibility. Minimizing tension at pathologic neutral and using a respiratory hold toward ease describe indirect treatment, avoiding load on the attachments is the opposite of engaging the barrier, and reducing sympathetic outflow is the goal of ganglion inhibition.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: direct technique suits shortened, inelastic fascia and adhesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — indirect functional diagnostic and treatment techniques","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where does the pylorus project on the surface?","choices":{"A":"At the umbilicus just left of midline","B":"Two fingers below the umbilicus at midline","C":"At the xiphoid process left of midline","D":"At the left costal margin at the midclavicular line","E":"Four to five fingers above the umbilicus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pylorus projects four to five fingers superior to the umbilicus, just to the right of the midline, and feels palpatorily like an almond in size and firmness. The left costal margin lies over the stomach's upper left quadrant, while the pylorus sits just right of midline. The pylorus projects four to five fingers above the umbilicus, not two fingers below. The pylorus sits just right of midline, not left of the xiphoid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach lies in the left upper quadrant, and the pylorus crosses toward the right of midline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — colonic stimulation along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The pylorus projects four to five fingers above the umbilicu","C":"The pylorus sits just right of midline, not left of the xiph"}},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":1,"stem":"Which ribs protect the spleen, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Right ribs 7 to 10","B":"Left ribs 4 to 7","C":"Right ribs 8 to 12","D":"Left ribs 8 to 12","E":"Left ribs 6 to 9"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The spleen sits deep in the posterolateral left upper quadrant, protected by the lower left rib cage spanning ribs 8 to 12. Moore's anatomy text relates the spleen more narrowly to the left 9th to 11th ribs, with its long axis along the 10th rib. The liver lies under the right ribs 7 to 10. The course places the spleen under the lower left rib cage, ribs 8 to 12, not left ribs 4 to 7. The course places the spleen under the lower left rib cage, ribs 8 to 12, not left ribs 6 to 9.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the splenic pump contacts the left costal margin, and the liver pump contacts the lower right ribs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — colonic stimulation along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course places the spleen under the lower left rib cage, ribs 8 to 12, not left ribs 4 to 7.","E":"The course places the spleen under the lower left rib cage, ribs 8 to 12, not left ribs 6 to 9."}},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, where is the gallbladder located on the surface?","choices":{"A":"Below the liver edge at the right anterior axillary line","B":"At the right anterior superior iliac spine","C":"Below the liver edge at the right midnipple line","D":"Two fingers lateral to the xiphoid on the left","E":"Above the umbilicus at the left midclavicular line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The gallbladder lies just inferior to the lower edge of the liver on the right at the midnipple line. Beside the xiphoid on the left is the stomach's upper left quadrant, not the right side where the gallbladder lies. The course places the gallbladder just below the liver edge at the right midnipple line, not the right anterior axillary line. The course places the gallbladder just below the liver edge at the right midnipple line, not at the right anterior superior iliac spine. The course places the gallbladder on the right below the liver edge at the midnipple line, not at the left midclavicular line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder disease can refer pain to the right shoulder through the diaphragm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 4 — colonic stimulation along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course places the gallbladder just below the liver edge at the right midnipple line, not the rig","B":"The course places the gallbladder just below the liver edge at the right midnipple line, not at the ","E":"The course places the gallbladder on the right below the liv"}},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has functional dyspepsia with sympathetic hyperactivity, and the physician wants to inhibit the collateral ganglion that serves the stomach. According to the course, which ganglion is treated and at what vertebral level does it lie?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion at the superior border of L1","B":"Superior mesenteric ganglion at the L1 to L2 disk","C":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion at the level of L3","D":"Celiac ganglion at the level of the L4 vertebra","E":"Aorticorenal ganglion at the T9 to T10 disk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Abdominal ganglion inhibition applies steady, deep inhibitory pressure over a collateral ganglion to decrease sympathetic hyperactivity of the gut. The course places the celiac ganglion, which relays sympathetic outflow to the stomach, at the superior border of L1. The superior mesenteric ganglion lies at the L1 to L2 disk or the superior border of L2 and serves the small intestine and proximal colon. The inferior mesenteric ganglion lies at L3 and serves the distal colon and pelvic viscera. L4 is below all three ganglia, and the course does not place an aorticorenal ganglion at T9 to T10.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the celiac trunk branches into the common hepatic, splenic, and left gastric arteries of the foregut. COMLEX: celiac at L1, superior mesenteric at L1 to L2, inferior mesenteric at L3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 5 — hand placement for abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, what is the goal of the liver pump?","choices":{"A":"Raise gastric motility via vagal facilitation","B":"Decrease sympathetic hyperactivity of the gut","C":"Assist hepatic detoxification and mobilize lymph","D":"Free the lymphatic channels of the mesentery","E":"Treat postsurgical paralytic ileus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The liver pump assists hepatic detoxification and mobilizes lymph. The liver has a rich bed of lymphatic channels and portal venous vessels, and the compression gradient also enhances Kupffer cell interaction with toxins and antigens. Decreasing gut sympathetic hyperactivity and treating postsurgical ileus are the goals of ganglion inhibition and colonic stimulation, and the course gives no vagal goal for the liver pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells are the liver's resident macrophages.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Decreasing gut sympathetic hyp"}},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which change in blood counts has been shown with the splenic pump, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Decreased reticulocyte counts with unchanged leukocytes","B":"Increased reticulocyte and leukocyte counts","C":"Increased platelet and eosinophil counts","D":"Decreased red cell and platelet counts","E":"Increased hematocrit with decreased leukocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Compression-gradient changes applied to the spleen mobilize stored red and white blood cells into circulation, which has been shown to increase reticulocyte and leukocyte counts. Reticulocyte counts increase, not decrease, with the splenic pump. The course reports increases in reticulocytes and leukocytes, not platelets and eosinophils. The splenic pump mobilizes cells and increases counts, not decreases them. The course reports that the splenic pump increases leukocyte counts, not that it decreases them.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course ties the splenic pump to blood cell counts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goals of the visceral techniques","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Reticulocyte counts increase, not decrease, with the splenic","C":"The course reports increases in reticulocytes and leukocytes","D":"The splenic pump mobilizes cells and increases counts, not d","E":"The course reports that the splenic pump increases leukocyte"}},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the course, visceral OMT is highly indicated for which problem?","choices":{"A":"Acute appendicitis with rebound tenderness","B":"A suspected abdominal aortic aneurysm","C":"Acute bowel obstruction with tinkling sounds","D":"Active internal hemorrhage with guarding","E":"Functional gut discomfort or constipation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists functional gastrointestinal discomfort, diarrhea, and constipation as indications for visceral OMT. Appendicitis, a suspected aneurysm, bowel obstruction, and internal hemorrhage are contraindications that need medical or surgical evaluation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the Week 4 lab rubric lists a known diagnosis with a functional visceral component, or a viscerosomatic reflex, as indications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — indications, contraindications, and referral for medical evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman is on postoperative day 2 after a colectomy. She has a developing ileus with hypoactive bowel sounds, a soft abdomen, stable vital signs, and no peritoneal signs. According to the course, which outcome has visceral OMT been shown to produce in such patients?","choices":{"A":"Faster healing of the surgical anastomosis","B":"A lower rate of postoperative wound infection","C":"Less need for postoperative opioid analgesia","D":"A shorter time to flatus and hospital stay","E":"Fewer postoperative deep venous thromboses"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that visceral OMT is indicated in a post-surgical or developing ileus because it reduces sympathetic hyperactivity, and that this has been shown to decrease time to flatus and shorten hospital stays. The course makes no claim about anastomotic healing, wound infection rates, opioid requirements, or venous thrombosis. The picture must still be screened: high-pitched tinkling sounds suggest obstruction, and a complete absence of bowel sounds suggests a silent paralytic ileus, and both need medical evaluation instead of direct abdominal manipulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic overactivity slows gut motility and tightens sphincters. COMLEX: postoperative ileus is an indication for visceral OMT once obstruction and a silent abdomen are excluded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — indications, contraindications, and referral for medical evaluation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The course makes no claim abou"}},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hand contact does the course require when performing visceral OMT?","choices":{"A":"The fingertips for precise organ localization","B":"The soft palmar surfaces of the fingers and hands","C":"The tips of the thumbs for deep penetration","D":"The knuckles for broad, even pressure","E":"The point of the elbow for deep contact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that clinicians must use the soft, flat palmar surfaces of the fingers and hands, never the fingertips, to prevent tissue injury or laceration of delicate internal organs. Thumb tips, knuckles, and the elbow are not the soft palmar contact the course requires. The course says never to use the fingertips, which can injure delicate organs.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: fingertip contact on abdominal organs is a safety error.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — indications, contraindications, and referral for medical evaluation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course says never to use the fingertips, which can injur","C":"Thumb tips, knuckles, and the ","D":"Thumb tips, knuckles, and the ","E":"Thumb tips, knuckles, and the "}},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man stands while a physician rests a hand on top of his head and senses a posterior pull. According to the course, which region is most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"Visceral, anterior rib, or sternal restriction","B":"Paired bilateral visceral dysfunction","C":"Extremity restriction","D":"Gastric fascial adhesion","E":"Spinal or dural dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A posterior pull in general listening points to a spinal or dural dysfunction. An anterior pull indicates visceral, anterior rib, or sternal restriction, and a lateral pull suggests the extremities or paired bilateral viscera. A gastric adhesion is visceral, which the course links to an anterior pull.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: read the direction of global pull first, then localize with layer palpation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — general listening in the treatment plan","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has epigastric discomfort. According to the course, which palpatory finding supports localized inflammation of her stomach?","choices":{"A":"Tissue that feels softer than normal with no thermal change","B":"Tissue that feels normal with no thermal change","C":"Tissue that feels harder than normal with a thermal change","D":"Tissue that feels softer than normal with a thermal change","E":"Tissue that feels harder than normal with no thermal change"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that localized inflammation and density changes are diagnosed by palpating tissue consistency, such as an irritated stomach feeling harder than normal, and by noting manual thermal changes over the organ. Inflamed tissue feels harder, not softer, than normal, and it shows thermal change. Localized inflammation changes both tissue consistency and temperature, so normal feel with no thermal change does not support it. Inflamed tissue feels harder, not softer, than normal. Localized inflammation is accompanied by a thermal change, so no thermal change does not support it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: inflammation changes density and temperature, both of which can be felt over the organ.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — somatic and tissue findings of visceral disease","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Inflamed tissue feels harder, not softer, than normal, and i","B":"Localized inflammation changes both tissue consistency and t","D":"Inflamed tissue feels harder, not softer, than normal.","E":"Localized inflammation is accompanied by a thermal change, s"}},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the components of a general visceral screening examination, including assessment of fascial pull, tissue restriction, and inflammation.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician wants to assess the inherent motility of a patient's organ. According to the course, how is the hand placed?","choices":{"A":"Firmly across the organ's short axis","B":"Gently along the organ's longitudinal axis","C":"On top of the head to feel the global pull","D":"Deeply into each tissue layer in turn","E":"Over the rib cage during forced respiration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that motility, the organ's inherent, active, independent motion, is assessed by gently placing a hand along the organ's longitudinal axis. Mobility is assessed by motion-testing the attachments. A hand on top of the head is general listening, compressing through each layer is local listening, and testing during respiration assesses mobility. Motility is assessed with a gentle hand along the longitudinal axis, not firmly across the short axis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mobility is the passive respiratory motion, and motility is the intrinsic rhythm felt along the longitudinal axis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — mobility versus motility","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Motility is assessed with a gentle hand along the longitudin","D":"A hand on top of the head is g","E":"A hand on top of the head is g"}},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician guides a restricted organ attachment to a point of minimal tension, has the patient hold the breath in inhalation, and then works with the exhalation. Which approach is this, according to the course?","choices":{"A":"Direct treatment with respiratory cooperation","B":"Indirect treatment with a high-velocity thrust","C":"Direct treatment with an end-exhalation thrust","D":"Indirect treatment with respiratory cooperation","E":"Layer palpation without any treatment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Indirect treatment moves tissues away from the restrictive barrier toward the position of ease. The physician can add respiratory cooperation, having the patient hold the breath in inhalation to build tension and then engaging a new barrier during exhalation as the tissues release. Guiding to a point of minimal tension is indirect, not direct, treatment. The course describes indirect treatment as moving toward the position of ease with respiratory cooperation, not as a high-velocity thrust. Moving to a point of minimal tension is the course's indirect treatment; direct treatment guides tissues gently but firmly into the direction of tension. Layer palpation is an assessment, whereas the scenario describes treatment of an attachment with respiratory cooperation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: indirect visceral technique goes toward ease and can use a breath hold to enhance release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — indirect functional technique","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Guiding to a point of minimal tension is indirect, not direc","B":"The course describes indirect treatment as moving toward the","C":"Moving to a point of minimal tension is the course's indirec","E":"Layer palpation is an assessment, whereas the scenario descr"}},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":2,"stem":"During an indirect visceral treatment the patient holds the breath in inhalation. According to the course, what does the physician do during the following exhalation?","choices":{"A":"Engages a new barrier as the tissues relax","B":"Applies a high-velocity thrust through the barrier","C":"Returns the organ to the original restriction","D":"Maintains the breath hold until the patient tires","E":"Compresses the organ against the diaphragm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course describes the patient holding the breath in inhalation to build tension. The physician then engages a new barrier during exhalation as the tissues relax and release. Returning to the original restriction would undo the release, and the course's exhalation step does not compress the organ. The course describes engaging a new barrier as the tissues release during exhalation, not a high-velocity thrust. The breath hold in inhalation builds tension, and the physician then works with the exhalation rather than extending the hold.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: respiratory cooperation uses the patient's breathing phases as the treatment force.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — respiratory cooperation in the lab","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The course describes engaging a new barrier as the tissues r","D":"The breath hold in inhalation builds tension, and the physic"}},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate direct and indirect approaches to visceral treatment, including treatment toward the restrictive barrier versus the position of ease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has fixed fascial adhesions after abdominal surgery. Which approach does the course describe for breaking down these restrictions?","choices":{"A":"Indirect treatment toward the position of ease","B":"Observation without manual treatment","C":"Direct treatment into the restrictive barrier","D":"Gentle cranial balancing at the occiput","E":"Counterstrain at an anterior thoracic tender point"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Direct treatment guides the tissue firmly into the direction of tension, aiming to elongate shortened, inelastic connective tissue, break down fascial adhesions, and restore normal distensibility. Cranial balancing and counterstrain are not the course's approach to abdominal fascial adhesions. Breaking down fascial adhesions is the stated aim of direct treatment; indirect treatment instead moves tissues toward the position of ease. The course describes direct manual treatment to break down fascial adhesions, so observation alone is not appropriate.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: direct stretch is described for adhesions and shortened fascia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — direct versus indirect functional approaches","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Breaking down fascial adhesions is the stated aim of direct ","B":"The course describes direct manual treatment to break down f","E":"Cranial balancing and counters"}},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician palpates the right lower quadrant, medial to the right anterior superior iliac spine. According to the course, which structure lies there?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon, near the pelvic brim","B":"Cecum, near the ileocecal junction","C":"Descending colon, along the left flank","D":"Hepatic flexure, under the right ribs","E":"Splenic flexure, under the left ribs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course places the cecum in the right lower quadrant medial to the right ASIS. The sigmoid colon lies in the left lower quadrant medial to the left ASIS. The descending colon runs along the left flank, not the right lower quadrant. The hepatic flexure is not the structure medial to the right ASIS; the course places the cecum there. The splenic flexure is not the structure medial to the right ASIS; the course places the cecum there.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cecum is the first part of the large intestine and sits low on the right.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — hand placement along the course of the colon","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The descending colon runs along the left flank, not the righ","D":"The hepatic flexure is not the structure medial to the right","E":"The splenic flexure is not the structure medial to the right"}},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course, which colon segments lie retroperitoneally?","choices":{"A":"Ascending and descending colon","B":"Transverse and sigmoid colon","C":"Cecum and transverse colon","D":"Sigmoid colon and splenic flexure","E":"Hepatic flexure and sigmoid colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that the ascending colon sits retroperitoneally along the right flank to the hepatic flexure, and the descending colon runs retroperitoneally along the left flank to the left pelvic brim. The transverse and sigmoid colon hang on mesenteries, the transverse and sigmoid mesocolon; the course names the ascending and descending colon as retroperitoneal. The cecum is almost entirely enveloped by peritoneum and the transverse colon hangs on its mesocolon; the course names the ascending and descending colon as retroperitoneal. The sigmoid colon and splenic flexure are not the retroperitoneal segments the course names. The hepatic flexure and sigmoid colon are not the retroperitoneal segments; the ascending and descending colon are.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal with mesenteries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — colon anatomy for hand placement","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The transverse and sigmoid colon hang on mesenteries, the tr","C":"The cecum is almost entirely enveloped by peritoneum and the","D":"The sigmoid colon and splenic flexure are not the retroperit","E":"The hepatic flexure and sigmoid colon are not the retroperit"}},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the surface anatomy of the stomach, liver, spleen, gallbladder, and ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","batch":2,"stem":"A student is localizing the biliary outflow tract on a patient. According to the course, where does the sphincter of Oddi project on the surface?","choices":{"A":"About 2 cm below and to the left of the umbilicus","B":"At the xiphoid process on the midline","C":"About 5 cm lateral to the right iliac spine","D":"About 2 cm above and to the right of the umbilicus","E":"At the right costal margin, midclavicular line"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that the sphincter of Oddi projects approximately 2 cm superior and to the right of the umbilicus. The gallbladder lies below the liver edge at the right midnipple line. The xiphoid on the midline is not where the course places the sphincter. The course places the sphincter of Oddi about 2 cm above and to the right of the umbilicus, not 5 cm lateral to the right iliac spine. The course places the sphincter of Oddi about 2 cm above and to the right of the umbilicus, not at the right costal margin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sphincter of Oddi controls bile and pancreatic secretions into the duodenum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — surface landmarks for abdominal techniques","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The course places the sphincter of Oddi about 2 cm above and to the right of the umbilicus, not 5 cm","E":"The course places the sphincter of Oddi about 2 cm above and to the right of the umbilicus, not at t"}},{"id":"sdl11_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician applies inhibitory pressure over the superior mesenteric ganglion. According to the course, at what level is it located?","choices":{"A":"The L1 to L2 disk or the superior border of L2","B":"The superior border of L1 at the celiac level","C":"The level of L3 where the inferior ganglion lies","D":"The T10 to T11 disk behind the lower sternum","E":"The superior border of L5 above the sacral base"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course places the celiac ganglion at the superior border of L1, the superior mesenteric ganglion at the L1 to L2 disk or superior border of L2, and the inferior mesenteric ganglion at the level of L3. The superior border of L1 is the level of the celiac ganglion, not the superior mesenteric ganglion. The level of L3 is where the inferior mesenteric ganglion lies, not the superior mesenteric ganglion. The T10 to T11 disk is not the level the course gives for any of these ganglia. The superior border of L5 is not the level of the superior mesenteric ganglion; it is at the L1 to L2 disk.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lab rubric uses surface landmarks instead, with the superior mesenteric ganglion midway between the xiphoid and umbilicus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — ganglion inhibition hand placement","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The superior border of L1 is the level of the celiac ganglio","C":"The level of L3 is where the inferior mesenteric ganglion li","D":"The T10 to T11 disk is not the level the course gives for an","E":"The superior border of L5 is not the level of the superior m"}},{"id":"sdl11_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"The Visceral II lecture calls colonic stimulation 'paraspinal inhibition.' According to that description, what is its goal?","choices":{"A":"Mobilize stored white cells and reticulocytes into circulation","B":"Detoxify the liver and move hepatic lymph through the portal bed","C":"Decongest the intestines and free the mesenteric lymph channels","D":"Lower sympathetic hyperactivity and restore bowel function","E":"Raise sympathetic tone to speed peristalsis and gut transit"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Visceral II lecture describes colonic stimulation, also called paraspinal inhibition, as down-regulating sympathetic hyperactivity and restoring normal bowel function. Mobilizing white cells and reticulocytes is the splenic pump, hepatic detoxification and lymph flow is the liver pump, and decongesting the intestines through the mesenteric lymph channels is the mesenteric lift. Colonic stimulation lowers sympathetic hyperactivity; it does not raise sympathetic tone.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: another section of the course describes colonic stimulation as restoring sliding and gliding of colonic segments, so read which description a stem names.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — colonic stimulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Colonic stimulation lowers sympathetic hyperactivity; it doe"}},{"id":"sdl11_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic goals of abdominal ganglion inhibition, mesenteric lift, liver pump, splenic pump, and colonic stimulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man recovering from a systemic infection receives a liver pump. A student asks which resident liver cells the pump's compression-gradient changes help to interact with toxins and antigens. According to the course, which cells are these?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells","B":"Biliary cholangiocytes","C":"Zone 3 hepatocytes","D":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells","E":"Hepatic Kupffer cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course explains that the liver holds a rich bed of lymphatic channels and portal venous vessels, so manual compression-gradient changes move lymph through these channels and enhance the capacity of Kupffer cells, the resident liver macrophages, to interact with toxins and antigens. Stellate cells store vitamin A and drive fibrosis. Cholangiocytes line the bile ducts. Zone 3 hepatocytes sit around the central vein and handle much of drug metabolism. Sinusoidal endothelial cells form the fenestrated lining of the sinusoids. The course names none of these as the target of the pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells are the resident macrophages of the hepatic sinusoids. COMLEX: the liver pump aids detoxification and lymph flow but is contraindicated in acute hepatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — liver pump technique","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man who smokes asks for visceral OMT for chronic constipation. He has intermittent mid-back pain, a midline abdominal bruit, and a lateral aortic pulsation about 1.5 inches wide. The course bars abdominal visceral OMT in this patient. Which harm is that rule meant to prevent?","choices":{"A":"A reflex bradycardia from vagal stimulation","B":"Accelerated rupture with fatal internal bleeding","C":"Laceration of the bowel wall by deep pressure","D":"Worse constipation from a rise in sympathetic tone","E":"Spread of an occult abdominal infection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A bruit with a lateral aortic pulsation wider than 1 inch suggests an abdominal aortic aneurysm, which the course treats as an absolute contraindication to abdominal visceral OMT. The reason the course gives is that lumbar or abdominal manipulation can accelerate vascular rupture and lead to fatal internal hemorrhage. Reflex bradycardia and a rise in sympathetic tone are not the hazards the course names. Bowel laceration is the reason the course requires the soft palmar surfaces of the hands instead of the fingertips. Spread of infection is the hazard of forcing a segment with osteomyelitis or diskitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: abdominal aortic aneurysm is associated with smoking, age, and male sex. COMLEX: a suspected aneurysm needs emergent evaluation, not abdominal OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — aortic aneurysm as a red flag","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has chronic constipation and functional abdominal discomfort with no red flags. According to the course, which plan fits?","choices":{"A":"Withhold OMT because functional symptoms signal organic disease","B":"Visceral OMT to raise sympathetic tone and speed transit","C":"Visceral OMT to improve peristalsis and lymphatic drainage","D":"Visceral OMT to elevate cortisol and loosen adhesions","E":"Defer OMT until imaging excludes any possible cause"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists functional gastrointestinal discomfort, diarrhea, and constipation as indications for visceral OMT, which improves peristaltic coordination, resolves fascial restrictions, and optimizes lymphatic drainage. With no red flags, the course does not require imaging before treatment. Functional symptoms with no red flags are indications for visceral OMT, not a reason to withhold it. Visceral OMT lowers rather than raises sympathetic tone. Loosening adhesions is part of direct visceral work, but the course says visceral manipulation lowers cortisol rather than elevating it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: screen for red flags first, then treat functional complaints with visceral OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — indications in the lab rubric","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Functional symptoms with no red flags are indications for vi","B":"Visceral OMT lowers rather than raises sympathetic tone.","D":"Loosening adhesions is part of direct visceral work, but the","E":"With no red flags, the course "}},{"id":"sdl11_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss indications and contraindications for visceral OMT including constipation, ileus, gastrointestinal discomfort, infectious mononucleosis, and abdominal aortic aneurysm.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has severe abdominal pain, distension, and a complete absence of bowel sounds. According to the course, which interpretation and decision fit?","choices":{"A":"Functional constipation that is suitable for direct abdominal OMT","B":"Obstruction suggested by high-pitched tinkling sounds","C":"Normal bowel function with sympathetic hyperactivity","D":"Diarrheal illness that is suitable for colonic stimulation","E":"Paralytic ileus that needs immediate medical evaluation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course states that a complete absence of bowel sounds indicates a paralytic ileus, while high-pitched tinkling sounds indicate an active obstruction. Both contraindicate direct abdominal manipulation and require immediate medical or surgical evaluation. Absent bowel sounds point to paralytic ileus, not a diarrheal illness, and direct abdominal manipulation such as colonic stimulation is contraindicated. Absent bowel sounds with severe pain indicate paralytic ileus, and direct abdominal OMT is contraindicated. Complete absence of bowel sounds with severe pain and distension is not normal bowel function.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: absent bowel sounds with pain and distension is a surgical or medical emergency, not a visceral OMT case.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — surgical abdomen and red flags","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Absent bowel sounds with severe pain indicate paralytic ileu","C":"Complete absence of bowel sounds with severe pain and disten"}}],"12":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 4 lab rubric, which finding is an indication for visceral manipulation?","choices":{"A":"A suspected abdominal aortic aneurysm","B":"A potential internal bleed","C":"Rebound tenderness with rigidity","D":"An acute abdomen from appendicitis","E":"A viscerosomatic reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric lists a known medical diagnosis with a functional visceral component, and a viscerosomatic reflex, as indications. An aneurysm, a potential internal bleed, and an acute abdomen such as appendicitis or rebound tenderness with rigidity need emergent evaluation, which the rubric lists as a contraindication.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: viscerosomatic reflexes link visceral dysfunction to segmentally related somatic findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"An aneurysm, a potential inter"}},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":1,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman taking warfarin is scheduled for visceral OMT for constipation. Since a fall yesterday she has new left flank and abdominal pain, a heart rate of 112/min, a blood pressure of 94/58 mm Hg, and a hemoglobin 3 g/dL below her baseline. According to the Week 4 lab rubric, how is this presentation classified?","choices":{"A":"A relative contraindication needing gentler technique","B":"An indication because of a functional visceral component","C":"An indication because of a viscerosomatic reflex","D":"A potential internal bleed, a contraindication","E":"A precaution needing a lighter splenic pump"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Week 4 rubric lists any medical condition requiring emergent evaluation or intervention as a contraindication to visceral manipulation and names abdominal aortic aneurysm and potential internal bleed as examples. New abdominal pain after a fall on warfarin, tachycardia, hypotension, and a falling hemoglobin point to bleeding, so treatment is withheld and she is sent for emergent evaluation. This is not a relative contraindication that a gentler technique can work around. Her constipation would be a functional indication and a viscerosomatic reflex would be another, but neither applies while an emergency is possible. A splenic pump would compress the left upper quadrant directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: warfarin inhibits vitamin K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X. COMLEX: rule out an acute abdomen before any visceral OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Week 4 lab rubric, how is severe pain with palpation of the abdomen classified?","choices":{"A":"An indication for deeper pressure","B":"A contraindication to treatment","C":"A sign of a viscerosomatic reflex to treat","D":"A reason to switch to the splenic pump","E":"A normal finding in a tense patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric lists severe pain with palpation as a contraindication to visceral manipulation. Because it is a contraindication, severe pain is not a reason to press deeper, switch to another visceral technique, or dismiss the finding as normal. Severe pain with palpation is a contraindication, not a sign of a viscerosomatic reflex to treat.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: severe tenderness may signal an acute abdomen, which needs medical evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 5 — indications and contraindications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Because it is a contraindicati","C":"Severe pain with palpation is a contraindication, not a sign","E":"Because it is a contraindicati"}},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, which patient and physician positions are used for the mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Patient supine, physician seated to the side","B":"Patient prone, physician standing at the head","C":"Patient seated, physician standing behind","D":"Patient side-lying, physician kneeling at the foot","E":"Patient supine, physician standing at the head"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric places the patient supine with the physician seated to the side of the patient. The rubric places the patient supine with the physician seated to the side, not prone. The patient is supine, not seated, for the mesenteric lift. The patient is supine and the physician is seated at the side, not kneeling at the foot. The physician sits at the patient's side, not at the head.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the mesenteric lift is performed from the side with the patient supine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of the mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The rubric places the patient supine with the physician seated to the side, not ","C":"The patient is supine, not seated, for the mesenteric lift.","D":"The patient is supine and the physician is seated at the sid","E":"The physician sits at the patient's side, not at the head."}},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed for the mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Over the iliac crests on both sides","B":"On the pubic symphysis at the pelvic brim","C":"Over the anterior superior iliac spines","D":"On the lateral border of the mesentery","E":"On the lumbar paraspinal muscles bilaterally"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric places the hand on the lateral border of the mesentery, with the patient supine and the physician seated to the side. The iliac crests, pubic symphysis, anterior superior iliac spines, and lumbar paraspinals are not contacts in the rubric's mesenteric lift.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: hand placement differs for each visceral technique, so learn it with the technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of the mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, how do the fingers move during the mesenteric lift?","choices":{"A":"Push gently toward the diaphragm, then deepen with each exhalation","B":"Push toward the back, then draw to the midline and right upper quadrant","C":"Push gently toward the pelvis, then draw to the left lower quadrant","D":"Compress the lower ribs, then release at the end of exhalation","E":"Pull laterally toward the flank with a quick thrust"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric has the fingers gently push toward the patient's back and then draw toward the midline and right upper quadrant. The fingers push toward the back and draw to the midline and right upper quadrant, not toward the diaphragm. The fingers push toward the patient's back, not the pelvis. The rubric has the fingers push gently toward the patient's back and draw to the midline and right upper quadrant, not compress the lower ribs. The lift is gentle and not a quick thrust to the flank.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the course's lecture text describes traction medially and cephalad toward the umbilicus, so rely on the rubric's wording for the lab.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of the mesenteric lift","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The fingers push toward the back and draw to the midline and","C":"The fingers push toward the patient's back, not the pelvis.","D":"The rubric has the fingers push gently toward the patient's ","E":"The lift is gentle and not a quick thrust to the flank."}},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where does the physician stand or sit for the splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"On the patient's right side","B":"At the head of the table","C":"At the foot of the table","D":"On the patient's left side","E":"Behind the seated patient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rubric places the physician seated or standing on the left side of the supine patient for the splenic pump. The rubric performs both pumps from the patient's side (left for the spleen, right for the liver), not from the head or foot of the table. The right side is the position for the liver pump; the splenic pump is from the left side. The patient is supine, and the physician is at the left side, not behind a seated patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spleen lies in the left upper quadrant and the liver in the right upper quadrant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — goals of the liver and splenic pumps","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The right side is the position for the liver pump; the splen","B":"The rubric performs both pumps","E":"The patient is supine, and the physician is at the left side"}},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, what is the hand position for the liver pump and the splenic pump?","choices":{"A":"One hand under the lower ribs and one below the costal margin","B":"Both hands on the xiphoid process with the fingertips pressed in","C":"One hand on the sacrum and the other over the sternum","D":"Both palms over the lateral borders of the mesentery and colon","E":"Hands along the course of the descending colon and sigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For both pumps the rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other hand on the abdomen below the costal margin. Hands along the descending colon and sigmoid are the colonic stimulation contact. The rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other on the abdomen below the costal margin, not on the xiphoid process. The rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other on the abdomen below the costal margin, not on the sacrum and sternum. The rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other on the abdomen below the costal margin; hands on the lateral mesentery are the mesenteric lift contact.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: both pumps use the same hand position.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — goals of the liver and splenic pumps","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other on the abdomen below the costal margin, not on the xiphoid","C":"The rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other on the abdomen below the costal margin, not on the sacrum ","D":"The rubric places one hand under the lower ribs and the other on the abdomen below the costal margin; hands on the later"}},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, how is the contact adjusted during the liver and splenic pumps?","choices":{"A":"Released suddenly at the end of exhalation","B":"Deepened with each exhalation until release","C":"Deepened with each inhalation until release","D":"Held at the same depth throughout the technique","E":"Thrust quickly at the end of inhalation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric has the physician hold the position until release while deepening the contact with each exhalation. The rubric uses no thrust, and the contact does not stay at one depth. The rubric uses no sudden release; the contact is deepened until release. The contact deepens with each exhalation, not inhalation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lecture text describes the liver pump as compression followed by a sudden release at the end of exhalation, so rely on the rubric wording for the lab.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — goals of the liver and splenic pumps","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The rubric uses no sudden release; the contact is deepened u","C":"The contact deepens with each exhalation, not inhalation.","D":"The rubric uses no thrust, and","E":"The rubric uses no thrust, and"}},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where are the hands placed for colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"On the lateral border of the mesentery","B":"One hand under the lower ribs","C":"Fingers at the midline near the xiphoid","D":"Fingers just above the umbilicus","E":"Along the course of the colon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric places the hands along the course of the colon while the patient lies supine. The lateral border of the mesentery is the mesenteric lift contact, a hand under the lower ribs is the pump contact, and the midline near the xiphoid and just above the umbilicus are the celiac and inferior mesenteric ganglion contacts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal, and the transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 3 — surface anatomy of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"With respiratory cooperation during colonic stimulation, toward what does the hand gently push, according to the lab rubric?","choices":{"A":"The patient's diaphragm","B":"The pelvic floor","C":"The right upper quadrant","D":"The umbilicus at the midline","E":"The lateral flank"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rubric has the hand gently push toward the patient's diaphragm until the restrictive barrier is met. Drawing toward the midline and right upper quadrant is the mesenteric lift's direction; the rubric's colonic stimulation pushes toward neither the pelvic floor, the umbilicus, nor the flank.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic stimulation uses the same diaphragm-directed push as the liver and splenic pumps.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 3 — surface anatomy of the colon","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where does the physician position for colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Standing at the head of the table facing caudad","B":"Kneeling at the patient's feet at the table end","C":"Seated or standing at the patient's side","D":"Standing behind the seated patient at the table","E":"Seated at the head of the table facing the feet"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric positions the physician seated or standing on the side of the supine patient. The rubric positions the physician at the patient's side, not at the head of the table. The rubric positions the physician at the side of the supine patient, not at the feet. The patient is supine, not seated, and the physician is at the side. The physician is at the patient's side, not at the head facing the feet.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: most visceral techniques in the lab are performed from the patient's side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 3 — surface anatomy of the colon","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The rubric positions the physician at the patient's side, no","B":"The rubric positions the physician at the side of the supine","D":"The patient is supine, not seated, and the physician is at t","E":"The physician is at the patient's side, not at the head faci"}},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed to treat the celiac ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus","B":"On the midline near the xiphoid process","C":"Just superior to the umbilicus","D":"Just inferior to the umbilicus","E":"Over the lateral border of the mesentery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric places the hand on the midline near the xiphoid for the celiac ganglion. Just above the umbilicus is the inferior mesenteric ganglion contact, the rubric has no contact below the umbilicus, and the lateral border of the mesentery is the mesenteric lift contact. Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus is the superior mesenteric ganglion contact, not the celiac.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric places the ganglia by surface landmarks, while the lecture text gives vertebral levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus is the superior","C":"Just above the umbilicus is th"}},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, how do the fingers move during abdominal ganglion inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Push gently toward the diaphragm, then deepen with each exhalation","B":"Push gently toward the pelvis, then draw toward the flank","C":"Push gently toward the back, then draw toward the midline","D":"Press firmly toward the spine, then release suddenly","E":"Pull toward the flank, then apply a short thrust"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric has the fingers gently push toward the patient's back and then draw toward the midline. The fingers push gently toward the back and draw toward the midline, not toward the diaphragm. The fingers push toward the back, not the pelvis, and draw to the midline. The contact is gentle, not firm, and there is no sudden release. Ganglion inhibition is gentle and uses no short thrust.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ganglion inhibition is a sustained inhibitory pressure, not a thrust.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The fingers push gently toward the back and draw toward the ","B":"The fingers push toward the back, not the pelvis, and draw t","D":"The contact is gentle, not firm, and there is no sudden rele","E":"Ganglion inhibition is gentle and uses no short thrust."}},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed to treat the inferior mesenteric ganglion?","choices":{"A":"On the midline near the xiphoid process","B":"Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus","C":"Just inferior to the umbilicus","D":"Over the left iliac fossa","E":"Just superior to the umbilicus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric places the hand just superior to the umbilicus for the inferior mesenteric ganglion. The midline near the xiphoid is the celiac contact, and the rubric uses no iliac fossa contact. The rubric has no contact below the umbilicus for the inferior mesenteric ganglion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric places the three ganglia by surface landmarks down the midline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 3 — physiologic goal of abdominal ganglion inhibition","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The rubric has no contact below the umbilicus for the inferi"}},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has diagnosed irritable bowel syndrome with chronic constipation alternating with diarrhea and no red flags. According to the Week 4 lab rubric, what is the appropriate decision?","choices":{"A":"Withhold because irritable bowel syndrome is emergent","B":"Withhold because constipation signals an internal bleed","C":"Proceed because a functional visceral diagnosis is an indication","D":"Withhold because viscerosomatic reflexes contraindicate it","E":"Withhold because a functional diagnosis is a contraindication"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric lists a known medical diagnosis with a functional visceral component, such as constipation, diarrhea, or irritable bowel syndrome, as an indication. Emergent conditions and severe palpation pain are the contraindications. Constipation with no red flags is a functional visceral diagnosis, which the rubric lists as an indication; potential internal bleed is one of its contraindications. Viscerosomatic reflexes do not contraindicate treatment; they are a finding to address. A functional diagnosis such as irritable bowel syndrome is an indication, not a contraindication.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: screen for red flags first, and then treat functional complaints with visceral OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — functional indications for visceral OMT","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Constipation with no red flags is a functional visceral diag","D":"Viscerosomatic reflexes do not contraindicate treatment; the","E":"A functional diagnosis such as irritable bowel syndrome is a"}},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman asks for visceral OMT for pelvic congestion and low back pain. She also has left leg pain and new swelling of the entire left leg. According to the course, which condition should the physician suspect and work up before any OMT?","choices":{"A":"May-Thurner syndrome","B":"Leriche syndrome","C":"Piriformis syndrome","D":"Iliopsoas syndrome","E":"Maigne syndrome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists May-Thurner syndrome among the conditions that need medical evaluation instead of OMT: the overlying right common iliac artery compresses the common iliac vein, causing low back pain, pelvic congestion, leg pain, and unilateral leg swelling or deep venous thrombosis. Leriche syndrome is aortoiliac atherosclerosis with claudication, impotence, and weak pulses rather than leg swelling. Piriformis syndrome refers pain down the posterior thigh without edema. Iliopsoas syndrome causes a flexed posture with a lean and a pelvic side shift. Maigne syndrome refers pain from the thoracolumbar junction to the iliac crest and groin.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: new unilateral leg swelling with pelvic congestion and back pain needs a vascular workup for venous compression or thrombosis before OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — situations requiring medical evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Demonstrate appropriate clinical judgment by recognizing the indications and contraindications for visceral manipulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with a history of breast cancer is referred for visceral and lumbar OMT. According to the course, which combination of findings marks her new back pain as possible spinal metastasis that needs advanced imaging first?","choices":{"A":"New pain with weight loss and no improvement after 1 month of care","B":"Pain that eases with sitting and worsens with walking or extension","C":"Morning stiffness lasting over an hour that improves with exercise","D":"Pain after lifting with a positive straight-leg raise below the knee","E":"Groin pain with tenderness just above and lateral to the PSIS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists spinal metastasis as a masquerader of back pain marked by a history of cancer with new-onset back pain, unexplained weight loss, age over 50 or 70, and no improvement after 1 month of conservative care; these patients need imaging or referral before OMT. Pain eased by sitting and worsened by walking or extension describes neurogenic claudication from stenosis. Prolonged morning stiffness that improves with exercise suggests an inflammatory spondyloarthropathy. Pain after lifting with a positive straight-leg raise suggests a disc herniation with radiculopathy. Groin pain with tenderness above and lateral to the PSIS suggests iliolumbar ligament syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: breast, lung, prostate, renal, and thyroid cancers commonly metastasize to bone. COMLEX: bony metastasis at the target segment is an absolute contraindication to direct OMT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — situations requiring medical evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"During a mesenteric lift, a student's fingers have reached the restrictive barrier. According to the lab rubric, what comes next?","choices":{"A":"Release at once and reassess the patient","B":"Hold until release and repeat as needed","C":"Add a high-velocity thrust at the barrier","D":"Deepen contact with each exhalation until release","E":"Reverse to the position of ease for 90 seconds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rubric has the physician hold the position at the restrictive barrier until release and repeat into a new barrier if needed, then reassess. Releasing at once skips the hold; the rubric holds at the barrier until release. No high-velocity thrust is used; the rubric holds until release. Deepening with each exhalation is the technique for the pumps, but the mesenteric lift holds until release at the barrier. The rubric does not reverse to the position of ease; it holds until release.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lifts and ganglion inhibition are held until release. Respiratory deepening belongs to the colonic and pump techniques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — mesenteric lift goals","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Releasing at once skips the hold; the rubric holds at the ba","C":"No high-velocity thrust is used; the rubric holds until rele","D":"Deepening with each exhalation is the technique for the pump","E":"The rubric does not reverse to the position of ease; it hold"}},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has congestion of the descending colon after abdominal surgery. The physician applies a mesenteric lift as described in the course lecture text. How is the lift performed?","choices":{"A":"Fingertips press the colon straight down toward the spine and hold","B":"The heel of the hand pushes the colon laterally toward the flank","C":"The palm compresses the colon caudally toward the pelvic brim","D":"Finger pads sink to the segment and draw it medially and cephalad","E":"The knuckles knead the colon in small circles from the cecum outward"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture text describes sinking the soft pads of the fingers deep into the abdominal quadrant to contact the colon segment and gently tractioning the tissue medially and cephalad toward the umbilicus and its attachments. The Week 4 lab rubric describes the small-bowel version with a hand on the lateral border of the mesentery, pushing toward the back and then drawing toward the midline and right upper quadrant; both move the bowel toward its attachments. Fingertip pressure is avoided because it can injure the organs, and pushing the colon laterally or caudally moves it away from its attachments. Kneading with the knuckles is not part of either description.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: visceral techniques use the soft palmar surfaces of the fingers, never the fingertips, and lift the bowel toward its attachments.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — mesenteric lift goals","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Fingertip pressure is avoided ","C":"Fingertip pressure is avoided "}},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Perform a mesenteric lift using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A student performing a mesenteric lift on a patient with small-bowel congestion asks why the technique draws the bowel loops toward their attachments. According to the course, which answer is correct?","choices":{"A":"It eases traction on the stretch-sensitive mesentery and its vessels","B":"It stimulates vagal reflexes that speed small-bowel peristalsis","C":"It compresses the spleen to release its stored white blood cells","D":"It drains bile from the gallbladder into the second part of the duodenum","E":"It inhibits the celiac ganglion through deep pressure at the midline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course explains that the intestines hang in the stretch-sensitive mesentery, through which their arteries, veins, and lymphatic channels run. Drawing the loops toward their attachments relieves that traction, frees congested lymphatics, improves blood flow, and breaks the cycle of stretch, dysfunction, pain, and sympathetic tone. Vagal stimulation is not the mechanism the course gives. Releasing stored white cells is the goal of the splenic pump, and the lift does not drain bile. Celiac ganglion inhibition is a separate technique applied at the midline near the xiphoid.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the mesenteric lift decongests the bowel and frees the lymphatics in the mesentery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — mesenteric lift technique","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman needs a liver pump. According to the lab rubric, where does the physician position and toward what does the hand push?","choices":{"A":"On the left, pushing toward the diaphragm","B":"On the right, pushing toward the pelvis","C":"On the right, pushing to the diaphragm","D":"At the head, pushing toward the feet","E":"On the left, pushing toward the midline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric places the physician on the right side of the supine patient for the liver pump, and with respiratory cooperation the hand gently pushes toward the patient's diaphragm. The left side is the splenic pump position, and neither pump is performed from the head of the table or pushes toward the pelvis or the midline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver lies in the right upper quadrant under the right ribs 7 to 10.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — liver surface anatomy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man recovering from pneumonia receives a splenic pump to support immune function. According to the course lecture text, which description of the technique is correct?","choices":{"A":"Steady pressure over the left upper quadrant held for 90 seconds","B":"Deep pressure just below the xiphoid followed by a slow release","C":"Kneading of the left flank muscles while the patient inhales","D":"Rhythmic compression of left ribs 8 to 12, then sudden release","E":"Firm pressure on the right lower ribs held through inhalation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture text describes compressing the left costal margin over ribs 8 to 12 to catch and rhythmically compress the spleen, followed by a sudden release. The Week 4 lab rubric gives the same technique as the patient supine with the physician on the left, one hand under the lower ribs and one below the costal margin, pushing toward the diaphragm with respiratory cooperation and deepening with each exhalation until release. A 90-second hold is the counterstrain timing. Deep pressure below the xiphoid is celiac ganglion inhibition. Kneading the flank muscles is a soft tissue technique, and the lower right ribs are the site of the liver pump.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lecture compresses and suddenly releases, and the lab rubric deepens the contact with each exhalation until release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — splenic pump goals","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A 90-second hold is the counte"}},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Perform liver pump and splenic pump techniques using appropriate positioning, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A student is performing a liver pump. Which sequence matches the Week 4 lab rubric?","choices":{"A":"Supine patient, physician on the left, push toward the diaphragm, deepen with each exhalation","B":"Supine patient, physician on the right, push toward the pelvis, deepen with each inhalation","C":"Supine patient, physician on the right, push toward the diaphragm, deepen with each exhalation","D":"Prone patient, physician on the right, push toward the diaphragm, release suddenly","E":"Supine patient, physician at the head, push toward the pelvis, deepen with each exhalation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric positions the supine patient with the physician on the right, one hand under the lower ribs and one below the costal margin. The hand pushes toward the diaphragm with respiratory cooperation, holds until release, and deepens with each exhalation. The physician for the liver pump is on the right, not the left. The liver pump pushes toward the diaphragm and deepens with exhalation, not toward the pelvis with inhalation. The patient is supine, and the contact is held until release rather than released suddenly. The physician stands at the patient's right side, not at the head, and pushes toward the diaphragm.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: left side for the spleen, right side for the liver, and both push toward the diaphragm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — liver pump goals","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The physician for the liver pump is on the right, not the le","B":"The liver pump pushes toward the diaphragm and deepens with ","D":"The patient is supine, and the contact is held until release","E":"The physician stands at the patient's right side, not at the"}},{"id":"sdl12_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"During colonic stimulation the hand has reached the restrictive barrier. According to the lab rubric, what does the physician do?","choices":{"A":"Hold until release, deepening with each inhalation","B":"Add a rapid thrust at the restrictive barrier","C":"Release at once and ask the patient to inhale","D":"Reverse to the position of ease and hold there","E":"Hold until release, deepening with each exhalation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rubric has the physician hold the position until release while deepening the contact with each exhalation, then reassess. The rubric adds no thrust; the barrier is held until release. The contact deepens with exhalation, not inhalation. The rubric holds until release rather than releasing at once. The rubric does not reverse to the position of ease; it holds until release.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the rubric ties the deepening to exhalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — colonic stimulation goal","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The contact deepens with exhalation, not inhalation.","C":"The rubric holds until release rather than releasing at once","D":"The rubric does not reverse to the position of ease; it hold"}},{"id":"sdl12_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has chronic functional diarrhea with no red flags. Before colonic stimulation, the physician motion-tests each part of the large intestine. According to the course, a restriction in which part is tied to her loose stools?","choices":{"A":"The sigmoid colon","B":"The rectum","C":"The splenic flexure","D":"The cecum","E":"The descending colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During colonic stimulation the physician motion-tests and mobilizes each segment in superior, inferior, medial, lateral, and rotational directions. The course cites osteopathic literature tying a restricted cecum to diarrhea, so cecal work is used for loose stools. By contrast, a lack of mobility in the sigmoid colon or rectum is classically associated with constipation. The course gives the splenic flexure's respiratory descent of about 1.43 cm and describes the descending colon as retroperitoneal along the left flank, but it does not link either one to diarrhea.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic stimulation restores sliding and distensibility of the segments; cecal work for diarrhea, sigmoid and rectal work for constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — colonic stimulation goals","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Perform colonic stimulation using proper hand placement along the course of the colon, respiratory cooperation, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"According to the course's lecture text, in which directions are individual colonic segments mobilized during colonic stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Medial and lateral, without rotational motion","B":"Superior, inferior, medial, lateral, and rotational","C":"Superior and inferior, against a fixed anterior lock","D":"Rotational and torsional, around a fixed fulcrum","E":"Anterior and posterior, with a sudden release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course describes motion-testing and mobilizing individual segments of the large intestine in superior, inferior, medial, lateral, and rotational directions to restore sliding, gliding, and distensibility. Anterior-posterior mobilization with a sudden release is not the course's colonic stimulation; a sudden release appears in its liver and splenic pump descriptions. The course also includes superior, inferior, and rotational motion, not only medial and lateral. The course does not use a fixed anterior lock; segments move in several directions. The course lists superior, inferior, medial, lateral, and rotational directions, not rotational and torsional around a fixed fulcrum.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: colonic work is multidirectional and segment specific, from the cecum to the rectum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — colonic stimulation goal","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The course also includes superior, inferior, and rotational ","C":"The course does not use a fixed anterior lock; segments move","D":"The course lists superior, inferior, medial, lateral, and ro"}},{"id":"sdl12_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A student treats the superior mesenteric ganglion. According to the lab rubric, where is the hand placed?","choices":{"A":"On the midline near the xiphoid process","B":"Just superior to the umbilicus at the midline","C":"Midway between the xiphoid and the umbilicus","D":"Just inferior to the umbilicus at the midline","E":"Over the right costal margin near the nipple line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The rubric places the hand on the midline near the xiphoid for the celiac ganglion, midway between the xiphoid and umbilicus for the superior mesenteric ganglion, and just superior to the umbilicus for the inferior mesenteric ganglion. The midline near the xiphoid is the celiac ganglion contact. Just superior to the umbilicus is the inferior mesenteric ganglion contact. The rubric has no contact just inferior to the umbilicus. The right costal margin near the nipple line is not a ganglion contact; the rubric lists the midline near the xiphoid, midway to the umbilicus, and just above the umbilicus.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the lecture text gives vertebral levels instead, with the superior mesenteric ganglion at the L1 to L2 disk or the superior border of L2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — ganglion inhibition goal","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The midline near the xiphoid is the celiac ganglion contact.","B":"Just superior to the umbilicus is the inferior mesenteric ga","D":"The rubric has no contact just inferior to the umbilicus.","E":"The right costal margin near the nipple line is not a gangli"}},{"id":"sdl12_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A physician wants to lower hypersympathetic outflow to the pelvic viscera and treats the inferior mesenteric ganglion. According to the course, at which vertebra does it lie?","choices":{"A":"The superior border of S2","B":"The superior border of L1","C":"The L1 to L2 disk","D":"The T10 to T11 disk","E":"The level of L3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course places the celiac ganglion at the superior border of L1, the superior mesenteric ganglion at the L1 to L2 disk or superior border of L2, and the inferior mesenteric ganglion at the level of L3. The superior border of S2 is not the level the course gives for any of these ganglia. The superior border of L1 is the celiac ganglion. The L1 to L2 disk is the superior mesenteric ganglion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the inferior mesenteric ganglion supplies sympathetic fibers to the hindgut and pelvic viscera.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — ganglion inhibition levels","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The superior border of S2 is not the level the course gives ","B":"The superior border of L1 is the celiac ganglion.","C":"The L1 to L2 disk is the superior mesenteric ganglion."}},{"id":"sdl12_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Perform abdominal ganglion inhibition using correct patient positioning, physician positioning, hand placement, treatment procedure, and reassessment.","batch":2,"stem":"A student performs abdominal ganglion inhibition with deep, gentle layer palpation through the abdominal wall of a supine patient. According to the course, the collateral ganglia being treated lie directly anterior to which structure?","choices":{"A":"The inferior vena cava","B":"The cisterna chyli","C":"The psoas major muscle","D":"The left kidney","E":"The abdominal aorta"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course describes locating the celiac ganglion at the superior border of L1, the superior mesenteric ganglion at the superior border of L2, and the inferior mesenteric ganglion at L3, each just anterior to the aorta, and then applying sustained inhibitory pressure. The inferior vena cava runs to the right of the aorta, and the cisterna chyli lies behind it. The psoas major and the left kidney are lateral to the aorta and are not where the ganglia sit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral ganglia. COMLEX: palpate for the aortic pulsation and stop if it is widened or there is a bruit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — ganglion inhibition technique","isHighYield":false}]};
